::P|telemetry.tasks.disable|disabled|disabled|enabled|disabled|enabled|
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.001|TELEMETRY AND COMPATIBILITY SCHEDULED TASKS
::T|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.002|Turns off seven background tasks that inventory your apps and drivers and upload feedback data; only one of them (Compatibility Appraiser) ever costs you anything you would notice, and turning it off means Windows Update may offer a feature update it would otherwise have held back.
::T|FR|telemetry.tasks.disable.001|TACHES PLANIFIEES DE TELEMETRIE ET DE COMPATIBILITE
::T|FR|telemetry.tasks.disable.002|Desactive sept taches de fond qui inventorient vos applications et vos pilotes et envoient des donnees de retour ; une seule (Compatibility Appraiser) coute vraiment quelque chose de sensible, et la desactiver signifie que Windows Update peut proposer une mise a niveau qu il aurait sinon retenue.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.001|  What it is      : Seven scheduled tasks. Compatibility Appraiser
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.002|                    inventories your apps and drivers for upgrade-
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.003|                    readiness. Consolidator and UsbCeip are the Customer
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.004|                    Experience Improvement Program uploaders. DmClient and
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.005|                    DmClientOnScenarioDownload drive the feedback prompts.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.006|                    PcaPatchDbTask and StartupAppTask are local
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.007|                    housekeeping for the Program Compatibility Assistant
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.008|                    and for startup-app measurement. On Pro these tasks
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.009|                    are what actually moves data, because the
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.010|                    AllowTelemetry registry policy is clamped to Required
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.011|                    on non-Enterprise editions and writing 0 to it
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.012|                    achieves nothing.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.013|
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.014|  Actual effect   : Runs schtasks /Change /Disable on each of the seven.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.015|                    They stay installed and can be switched back on from
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.016|                    Task Scheduler at any time.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.017|
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.018|  Gain            : Partial, and worth being precise about. The only one
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.019|                    with a measurable machine cost is Compatibility
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.020|                    Appraiser: it can hold a core and the disk busy for
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.021|                    several minutes when it runs, which you notice on a
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.022|                    slow disk. The other six are cheap enough that turning
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.023|                    them off has no measurable gain in CPU, RAM or boot
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.024|                    time - the gain there is purely that less data leaves
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.025|                    the machine. Anyone who tells you this makes a PC
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.026|                    "telemetry-free" is wrong: DiagTrack itself is
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.027|                    untouched and the plain Compatibility Appraiser
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.028|                    variant may not even be matched by name.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.029|
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.030|  Cost            : Windows Update reads appraiser signals to decide
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.031|                    whether your exact hardware and drivers are safe for
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.032|                    the next feature update. With the appraiser off those
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.033|                    signals go stale, so a feature update that a known
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.034|                    incompatibility would have held back can be offered to
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.035|                    you anyway. Nothing breaks day to day.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.036|
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.037|  Windows default : All seven ship Enabled.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.038|
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.039|  Possible values:
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.040|    enabled              : The shipped state. Each task fires on its own
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.041|                           triggers - mostly idle-time and logon.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.042|                           Compatibility Appraiser walks the installed
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.043|                           application and driver inventory and writes it
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.044|                           under %windir%\appcompat\Programs, where
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.045|                           DiagTrack picks it up; Consolidator and UsbCeip
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.046|                           upload CEIP samples; the two DmClient tasks
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.047|                           schedule feedback prompts and download feedback
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.048|                           scenarios.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.049|    disabled             : schtasks /Change /Disable. The task definition
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.050|                           stays on disk with all its triggers and actions
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.051|                           intact - only the enabled flag flips. Nothing
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.052|                           else demand-starts these tasks, so a disabled
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.053|                           one simply never runs. One click in Task
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.054|                           Scheduler, or the restore branch of this
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.055|                           script, puts it back.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.056|
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.057|  Why these profiles : Three profiles agree and one dissents, and the
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.058|                       dissent is the interesting one. Gaming, server and
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.059|                       laptop all disable: none of them wants an idle-time
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.060|                       inventory sweep, and none of them relies on
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.061|                       Microsoft to gate feature updates. Office is the
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.062|                       profile that keeps them, because the office
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.063|                       archetype is exactly the machine you want Microsoft
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.064|                       to be conservative with - a family PC or a work
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.065|                       machine that installs feature updates automatically
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.066|                       is the one case where the appraiser's signal earns
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.067|                       its keep, and where a support tool or an IT audit
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.068|                       may expect a stock task set. Laptop is not a
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.069|                       battery decision: most of these tasks already carry
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.070|                       a "start only if on AC power" condition, so on
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.071|                       battery they were not running anyway - laptop
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.072|                       disables them for the same privacy reason as
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.073|                       gaming, not for runtime.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.074|
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.075|  Known problems  : None confirmed. See uncertain for the Startup impact
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.076|                    claim, which I have deliberately not asserted.
::X|EN|telemetry.tasks.disable.077|
