::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.006|
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.007|  Actual effect   : Deletes the three policy values and sets WSAIFabricSvc
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.008|                    back to demand, so Recall becomes available to opt
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.009|                    into where the hardware supports it.
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.010|
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.011|  Gain            : None on ordinary hardware - there is no Recall to
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.012|                    restore. On a Copilot+ machine you get the timeline
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.013|                    search back if you actually want it.
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.014|
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.015|  Cost            : On Copilot+, screen snapshots become possible again
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.016|                    once you opt in. On everything else, nothing changes
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.017|                    in either direction.
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.018|
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.019|  Windows default : All three values absent; the service left at whatever
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.020|                    the image shipped.
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.021|
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.022|  Possible values:
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.023|    DisableAIDataAnalysis=1 :
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.024|        Recall may not save snapshots. The Recall toggle in Settings is
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.025|        greyed with a policy note and the snapshot pipeline never starts.
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.026|    DisableAIDataAnalysis=0 :
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.027|        Not the default - it is an explicit "allowed by policy", a third
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.028|        state. It still greys the toggle as managed, just in the
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.029|        permissive direction. Never write this to undo the block; delete
