::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.031|    AllowRecallEnablement=0 :
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.032|        Recall cannot be turned on at all, even by an administrator who
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.033|        wants it. This is the harder of the two policies -
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.034|        DisableAIDataAnalysis stops the snapshots, this one removes the
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.035|        option.
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.036|    AllowRecallEnablement=1 :
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.037|        Explicit "may be enabled". A third state again, not the shipped
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.038|        condition.
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.039|    DELETE               : The shipped state for all three values: absent.
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.040|                           On Copilot+ hardware Recall then behaves as
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.041|                           designed - off until the user opts in,
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.042|                           snapshots stored locally and gated behind
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.043|                           Windows Hello. On non-Copilot+ hardware absent
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.044|                           and 1 are indistinguishable, because there is
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.045|                           nothing to snapshot with.
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.046|    WSAIFabricSvc=disabled :
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.047|        sc config start= disabled. The service that hosts the on-device AI
::X|EN|gr.recall.policy.del.048|        fabric cannot be started by anything, including the Recall
