::T|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.001|TURN THE ADVERTISING ID BACK ON
::T|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.002|Turns the per-user advertising ID back on so Store apps can read it again; this does not reduce or increase how many ads you see, only how well targeted they are.
::T|FR|gr.advertisingid.on.001|REACTIVER L IDENTIFIANT DE PUBLICITE
::T|FR|gr.advertisingid.on.002|Reactive l identifiant de publicite par utilisateur pour que les applications du Store puissent de nouveau le lire ; cela ne change pas le nombre de publicites, seulement leur pertinence.
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.001|  What it is      : A resettable identifier that Store apps read to key
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.002|                    personalised advertising. Same value as the setup
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.003|                    question, from the restore menu.
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.004|
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.005|  Actual effect   : Writes 1, so apps can read the ID again.
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.006|
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.007|  Gain            : None for you. This exists so the machine can be handed
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.008|                    on in a stock state.
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.009|
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.010|  Cost            : Store apps can profile you across sessions again. Note
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.011|                    the symmetry that people get wrong in both directions:
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.012|                    switching it off never reduced the number of ads, only
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.013|                    how well aimed they are.
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.014|
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.015|  Windows default : 1, present and enabled.
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.016|
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.017|  Possible values:
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.018|    1                    : Apps can read the per-user advertising ID
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.019|                           through the Windows Advertising API and use it
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.020|                           as a stable key to profile you across sessions
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.021|                           and across apps. The shipped value.
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.022|    0                    : The API returns an empty identifier, so apps
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.023|                           have no stable Windows-provided key to attach a
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.024|                           profile to. It does not block advertising, does
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.025|                           not reduce the number of ads, and costs no CPU
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.026|                           either way - it is a targeting knob, not a
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.027|                           performance one.
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.028|    DELETE               : Absent behaves as enabled. Deleting is
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.029|                           therefore not a way to turn it off - the script
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.030|                           writes an explicit value in both directions for
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.031|                           that reason.
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.032|
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.033|  Why these profiles : Four profiles write 0 and the table says so instead
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.034|                       of manufacturing a difference. There is nothing
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.035|                       here for gaming latency, server throughput or
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.036|                       laptop battery to disagree about, because the value
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.037|                       costs nothing to read and nothing to leave empty -
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.038|                       the only question anyone is actually answering is
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.039|                       whether they want a stable tracking key handed out
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.040|                       by the OS, and the answer for a machine you own is
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.041|                       no. Office is not an exception this time: ad
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.042|                       targeting is not a feature anyone loses, so the
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.043|                       features-on principle does not apply. Only the
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.044|                       Windows answer writes 1.
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.045|
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.046|  Target          : reg add "HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersio
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.047|                    n\AdvertisingInfo" /v "Enabled" /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f
::X|EN|gr.advertisingid.on.048|                    (OPTY.bat line 1677)
::X|FR|gr.advertisingid.on.001|  Ce que c est    : Un identifiant reinitialisable, propre a
