:: ---- reg.console.vt (preference) ------------------------------------
::P|reg.console.vt|1|1|1|1|DELETE|
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.001|COLORED CONSOLE OUTPUT
::T|EN|reg.console.vt.002|Lets the classic Command Prompt understand color codes so OPTY's own text is readable instead of raw escape codes - purely cosmetic, no performance effect, and invisible on Windows Terminal, which already handles color.
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.001|COULEURS DANS LA CONSOLE
::T|FR|reg.console.vt.002|Permet a l ancienne fenetre Invite de commandes de comprendre les codes de couleur pour que le texte d OPTY soit lisible au lieu de codes bruts - purement cosmetique, aucun effet sur la performance, et invisible sous Windows Terminal, qui gere deja les couleurs.
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.001|  What it is      : A single DWORD under HKCU\Console that tells the legacy
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.002|                    console host whether to interpret ANSI/VT escape sequences
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.003|                    or print them as literal characters. It is purely about
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.004|                    how text is drawn on screen.
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.005|
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.006|  Actual effect   : Written for the current user account, so it applies to
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.007|                    every CMD window that user opens, not just OPTY. OPTY
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.008|                    writes it at line 90, before it probes for the ESC
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.009|                    character, and never removes it afterwards.
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.010|
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.011|  Gain            : OPTY's own output becomes readable and colour-coded
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.012|                    instead of a wall of escape codes. There is no performance
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.013|                    gain of any kind - not CPU, not memory, not latency. On
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.014|                    Windows 11 with the default Windows Terminal, ANSI already
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.015|                    works and this write changes nothing visible at all.
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.016|
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.017|  Cost            : A very old console program that emitted raw ESC bytes as
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.018|                    data rather than as formatting could render oddly in a CMD
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.019|                    window afterwards. In practice, nothing. The value
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.020|                    persists after OPTY exits.
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.021|
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.022|  Windows default : Value absent, so VT interpretation is off in legacy
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.023|                    conhost.
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.024|
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.025|  Possible values:
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.026|    1      : conhost.exe parses ESC[ ... sequences in the output stream and
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.027|             acts on them: colour attributes, cursor moves, erase-line. This
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.028|             is the flag Microsoft added in Windows 10 1511 so classic CMD
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.029|             could do ANSI at all. Costs nothing measurable - it is one extra
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.030|             state machine in the console text parser.
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.031|    0      : conhost prints the escape bytes as literal characters, so OPTY's
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.032|             output arrives as lines starting with left-arrow[1;92m. Note this
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.033|             is behaviourally the same as the value being absent - 0 is an
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.034|             explicitly written off, not the shipped state.
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.035|    DELETE : The shipped state: the value does not exist and legacy conhost
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.036|             does not interpret VT. Windows Terminal - the default terminal
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.037|             application on Windows 11 - interprets ANSI on its own and never
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.038|             consults this value, so on a stock Windows 11 the deletion
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.039|             changes nothing you can see.
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.040|
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.041|  Why these profiles : Four identical answers, and that is the honest table:
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.042|                       this is a cosmetic display flag with no performance
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.043|                       dimension, so gaming, server, office and laptop have
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.044|                       nothing to distinguish them - all four want readable
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.045|                       output. The only real alternative is profile 5, which
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.046|                       exists for someone who wants a strictly untouched
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.047|                       console profile.
::X|EN|reg.console.vt.048|
