:: ---- reg.menuanimate (preference) -----------------------------------
::P|reg.menuanimate|0|0|1|1|1|
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.001|MENU AND WINDOW ANIMATIONS
::T|EN|reg.menuanimate.002|Turns off the roughly 200ms fade when menus and windows open - menus feel snappier and Remote Desktop sends less data, but it frees no CPU, GPU or RAM you could ever measure.
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.001|ANIMATIONS DES MENUS ET DES FENETRES
::T|FR|reg.menuanimate.002|Supprime le fondu d environ 200 ms a l ouverture des menus et des fenetres - les menus paraissent plus reactifs et le Bureau a distance envoie moins de donnees, mais cela ne libere aucun CPU, GPU ou RAM mesurable.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.001|  What it is      : MenuAnimate is a REG_SZ 0 or 1 under HKCU\Control
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.002|                    Panel\Desktop that controls the fade-and-slide transition
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.003|                    when menus and windows open and close, for the current
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.004|                    user.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.005|
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.006|  Actual effect   : 0 makes menus appear instantly instead of animating in
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.007|                    over roughly 200 ms. Nothing is disabled or unloaded - the
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.008|                    compositor still runs, it simply skips that one
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.009|                    transition. Because OPTY writes the registry without
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.010|                    notifying Explorer, expect it to take effect at the next
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.011|                    sign-in.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.012|
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.013|  Gain            : The interface feels snappier because you stop waiting on
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.014|                    animation frames. That is a perceived-latency gain: real,
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.015|                    but subjective. It frees no CPU, no GPU and no RAM you
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.016|                    could measure on any machine built in the last decade.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.017|                    Over a Remote Desktop session it is a genuine bandwidth
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.018|                    gain, because the animation frames are pixels that have to
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.019|                    cross the wire.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.020|
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.021|  Cost            : The desktop looks abrupt. Some people find instant menus
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.022|                    harsher to use. Nothing breaks.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.023|
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.024|  Windows default : 1 - animations on.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.025|
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.026|  Possible values:
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.027|    1 : Menus and windows fade and slide in over roughly 200 ms. The frames
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.028|        are drawn by DWM on the GPU; on any GPU of the last decade the cost is
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.029|        a rounding error, and the delay you feel is the animation duration
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.030|        itself, not a lack of horsepower.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.031|    0 : The transition is skipped: the menu is drawn in its final state on the
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.032|        first frame. Nothing is unloaded, DWM still composites the desktop.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.033|        The only thing you get back is the ~200 ms you were waiting on.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.034|
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.035|  Why these profiles : Gaming picks 0 for the feel, and only for the feel -
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.036|                       there is no frame-rate story here and it should not be
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.037|                       sold as one. Server picks 0 for a mechanical reason: a
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.038|                       headless box is driven over RDP, and every animation
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.039|                       frame is bandwidth on the wire. Office and laptop keep
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.040|                       1 because the animation costs nothing worth reclaiming
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.041|                       and the machine should look finished. Windows ships 1.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.042|
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.043|  Unverified         : OPTY writes the value with reg add and does not
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.044|                       broadcast SPI_SETMENUANIMATION, so a running Explorer
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.045|                       session keeps the old behaviour until the next sign-out
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.046|                       and sign-in. The menu says the change is applied;
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.047|                       strictly it is applied at the next logon.
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.048|
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.049|  Target             : HKCU\Control Panel\Desktop /v MenuAnimate /t REG_SZ /d
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.050|                       "0" or "1" (OPTY.bat lines 481-482, the -ani / +ani
::X|EN|reg.menuanimate.051|                       menu toggles)
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.001|  Ce que c est   : MenuAnimate est une chaine 0 ou 1 sous HKCU\Control
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.002|                   Panel\Desktop qui gere le fondu-glissement a l ouverture et
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.003|                   a la fermeture des menus et des fenetres, pour l
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.004|                   utilisateur courant.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.005|
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.006|  Effet reel     : 0 fait apparaitre les menus instantanement au lieu d une
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.007|                   animation d environ 200 ms. Rien n est desactive ni
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.008|                   decharge : le compositeur tourne toujours, il saute
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.009|                   simplement cette transition. Comme OPTY ecrit le registre
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.010|                   sans prevenir l Explorateur, l effet arrive a la prochaine
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.011|                   ouverture de session.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.012|
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.013|  Gain           : L interface parait plus vive parce qu on n attend plus les
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.014|                   images d animation. C est un gain de latence ressentie :
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.015|                   reel, mais subjectif. Il ne libere ni processeur, ni GPU,
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.016|                   ni memoire de facon mesurable sur une machine des dix
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.017|                   dernieres annees. En revanche, en Bureau a distance, c est
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.018|                   un vrai gain de bande passante : les images d animation
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.019|                   sont des pixels qui doivent traverser le reseau.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.020|
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.021|  Cout           : Le bureau parait sec. Certains trouvent les menus
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.022|                   instantanes plus agressifs a l usage. Rien ne casse.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.023|
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.024|  Defaut Windows : 1, animations activees.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.025|
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.026|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.027|    1 : Les menus et fenetres apparaissent en fondu-glissement sur environ 200
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.028|        ms. Les images sont dessinees par DWM sur le GPU ; sur n importe quel
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.029|        GPU des dix dernieres annees le cout est negligeable, et le delai
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.030|        ressenti vient de la duree de l animation elle-meme, pas d un manque
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.031|        de puissance.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.032|    0 : La transition est sautee : le menu est dessine dans son etat final des
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.033|        la premiere image. Rien n est decharge, DWM compose toujours le
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.034|        bureau. Le seul gain, ce sont les ~200 ms d attente.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.035|
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.036|  Pourquoi ces profils : Le profil jeu choisit 0 pour la sensation, et
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.037|                         uniquement pour ca : il n y a aucune histoire de FPS
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.038|                         ici et il ne faut pas la vendre comme telle. Le
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.039|                         profil serveur choisit 0 pour une raison mecanique :
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.040|                         une machine sans ecran se pilote en RDP, et chaque
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.041|                         image d animation, c est de la bande passante.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.042|                         Bureautique et portable gardent 1, l animation ne
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.043|                         coute rien qui vaille d etre recupere et la machine
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.044|                         doit avoir l air finie. Windows livre 1.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.045|
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.046|  Non verifie          : OPTY ecrit la valeur avec reg add et ne diffuse pas
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.047|                         SPI_SETMENUANIMATION, donc une session Explorateur
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.048|                         deja ouverte garde l ancien comportement jusqu a la
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.049|                         prochaine fermeture puis ouverture de session. Le
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.050|                         menu indique que le changement est applique ; a
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.051|                         proprement parler, il ne l est qu a la prochaine
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.052|                         connexion.
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.053|
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.054|  Cible                : HKCU\Control Panel\Desktop /v MenuAnimate /t REG_SZ
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.055|                         /d "0" or "1" (OPTY.bat lines 481-482, the -ani /
::X|FR|reg.menuanimate.056|                         +ani menu toggles)
