:: ---- rad.spectre.restore (repair) -------------------------------
::P|rad.spectre.restore|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.001|RE-ENABLE THE SPECTRE AND MELTDOWN MITIGATIONS
::T|EN|rad.spectre.restore.002|Restores CPU security protections against Spectre and Meltdown that some FPS tweak scripts disable - the performance you get back is a few percent on older Intel chips and close to nothing on modern ones, on any machine that ever opens a browser or runs a VM.
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.001|REACTIVER LES MITIGATIONS SPECTRE ET MELTDOWN
::T|FR|rad.spectre.restore.002|Retablit les protections du processeur contre Spectre et Meltdown que certains scripts FPS desactivent - le gain de performance recupere est de quelques pour cent sur les anciens processeurs Intel et quasi nul sur les recents, sur toute machine qui ouvre un navigateur ou fait tourner une machine virtuelle.
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.001|  What it is      : Two DWORDs under Memory Management that can switch off the
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.002|                    CPU side-channel mitigations at boot. They have no GUI;
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.003|                    the usual way to see the real state is Get-
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.004|                    SpeculationControlSettings or a third-party inspector.
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.005|
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.006|  Actual effect   : Deletes both, but only when they read exactly 3 and 3 -
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.007|                    the known disable pattern. Microsoft's own 0/3 hardening
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.008|                    recipe and any other combination are left alone. A reboot
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.009|                    is required before the change takes effect.
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.010|
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.011|  Gain            : Restores protection against speculative-execution attacks
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.012|                    that read memory across process and VM boundaries. On a
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.013|                    machine where the values were never set, this step does
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.014|                    nothing and says nothing - the gain only exists where a
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.015|                    tweak tool disabled them.
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.016|
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.017|  Cost            : On older Intel CPUs, disabling the mitigations was worth a
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.018|                    few percent in syscall-heavy and I/O-heavy work, and you
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.019|                    give that back. On CPUs with the in-silicon fixes - Intel
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.020|                    from roughly 9th gen, and AMD Zen 2 onward for the
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.021|                    variants that apply - the measurable cost is close to
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.022|                    nothing, and it is not a frame-rate story on either.
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.023|
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.024|  Windows default : Both values absent, mitigations applied as the CPU
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.025|                    requires.
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.026|
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.027|  Possible values:
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.028|    DELETE (both values) :
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.029|        The shipped state: neither value exists and the OS applies whatever
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.030|        mitigations the CPU and microcode call for. This is the only true
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.031|        restore - there is no single number that means default.
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.032|    3 / 3 (Override=3, Mask=3) :
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.033|        The known disable pattern, and the one every FPS tweak script writes.
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.034|        Bit 0 disables the branch-target-injection mitigation (Spectre v2),
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.035|        bit 1 disables the kernel-page-table isolation used against Meltdown;
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.036|        the mask says both bits are meaningful. This is the ONLY pattern OPTY
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.037|        acts on, and it acts on it because it is unambiguous.
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.038|    0 / 3 (Override=0, Mask=3) :
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.039|        Microsoft's own KB4073119 recipe to force the mitigations ENABLED. It
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.040|        is a third state, not the default, and it is a deliberate hardening
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.041|        choice. OPTY leaves it strictly alone - deleting it would quietly undo
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.042|        somebody's decision.
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.043|    other combinations :
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.044|        There are many, covering per-variant control on server workloads and
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.045|        hypervisor scenarios. OPTY does not attempt to interpret them and
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.046|        leaves them untouched, which is the right call for a tool that cannot
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.047|        ask why they are there.
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.048|
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.049|  Why these profiles : Five identical answers. There is no profile where a few
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.050|                       percent of syscall throughput is worth leaving a kernel
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.051|                       memory-disclosure class open on a machine that opens a
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.052|                       browser - and every one of these five does. The genuine
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.053|                       exception is an air-gapped benchmark box running
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.054|                       nothing but the benchmark, which is not one of the five
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.055|                       profiles and should not be pretended into one.
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.056|
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.057|  Target             : HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.058|                       Manager\Memory Management /v FeatureSettingsOverride
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.059|                       and /v FeatureSettingsOverrideMask - both deleted via
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.060|                       :killkey, and only when both read exactly 0x3 (OPTY.bat
::X|EN|rad.spectre.restore.061|                       lines 1865-1877)
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.001|  Ce que c est   : Deux DWORD sous Memory Management capables de couper au
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.002|                   demarrage les protections contre les canaux auxiliaires du
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.003|                   processeur. Aucune interface ne les montre ; on lit l etat
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.004|                   reel avec Get-SpeculationControlSettings ou un inspecteur
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.005|                   tiers.
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.006|
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.007|  Effet reel     : Supprime les deux, mais uniquement si elles valent
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.008|                   exactement 3 et 3, le motif de desactivation connu. La
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.009|                   recette de durcissement 0/3 de Microsoft et toute autre
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.010|                   combinaison sont laissees intactes. Un redemarrage est
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.011|                   necessaire.
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.012|
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.013|  Gain           : Retablit la protection contre les attaques par execution
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.014|                   speculative, qui lisent la memoire au travers des
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.015|                   frontieres de processus et de machines virtuelles. Sur une
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.016|                   machine ou ces valeurs n ont jamais ete posees, l etape ne
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.017|                   fait rien et ne dit rien : le gain n existe que la ou un
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.018|                   outil de tweak les avait coupees.
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.019|
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.020|  Cout           : Sur d anciens processeurs Intel, les couper valait quelques
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.021|                   pour cent sur les charges riches en appels systeme et en
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.022|                   E/S ; vous les rendez. Sur les processeurs corriges
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.023|                   materiellement - Intel a partir de la 9e generation
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.024|                   environ, AMD depuis Zen 2 pour les variantes concernees -
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.025|                   le cout mesurable est quasi nul. Et dans les deux cas, ce n
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.026|                   est pas une histoire de FPS.
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.027|
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.028|  Defaut Windows : Les deux valeurs absentes, mitigations appliquees selon ce
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.029|                   qu exige le processeur.
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.030|
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.031|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.032|    DELETE (les deux valeurs) :
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.033|        L etat livre : aucune des deux valeurs n existe et le systeme applique
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.034|        les mitigations que le processeur et le microcode reclament. C est la
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.035|        seule vraie restauration - aucun nombre ne signifie defaut.
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.036|    3 / 3 (Override=3, Mask=3) :
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.037|        Le motif de desactivation connu, celui qu ecrit chaque script FPS
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.038|        tweak. Le bit 0 desactive la mitigation contre l injection de cible de
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.039|        branchement (Spectre v2), le bit 1 desactive l isolation des tables de
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.040|        pages du noyau utilisee contre Meltdown ; le masque indique que les
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.041|        deux bits sont significatifs. C est le SEUL motif sur lequel OPTY
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.042|        agit, et il agit parce qu il est sans ambiguite.
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.043|    0 / 3 (Override=0, Mask=3) :
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.044|        La recette officielle de Microsoft (KB4073119) pour forcer les
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.045|        mitigations ACTIVEES. C est un troisieme etat, pas le defaut, et c est
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.046|        un durcissement volontaire. OPTY n y touche pas du tout : le supprimer
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.047|        annulerait en silence la decision de quelqu un.
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.048|    autres combinaisons :
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.049|        Il en existe beaucoup, pour un controle variante par variante sur des
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.050|        charges serveur et des scenarios d hyperviseur. OPTY ne cherche pas a
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.051|        les interpreter et n y touche pas, ce qui est le bon choix pour un
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.052|        outil qui ne peut pas demander pourquoi elles sont la.
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.053|
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.054|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq reponses identiques. Aucun profil ne justifie d
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.055|                         echanger quelques pour cent de debit d appels systeme
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.056|                         contre une classe de fuite de memoire noyau laissee
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.057|                         ouverte sur une machine qui ouvre un navigateur - et
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.058|                         les cinq en ouvrent un. La vraie exception, c est un
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.059|                         banc de test isole ne faisant tourner que le
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.060|                         benchmark : ce n est pas l un des cinq profils et il
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.061|                         ne faut pas faire semblant du contraire.
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.062|
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.063|  Cible                : HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.064|                         Manager\Memory Management /v FeatureSettingsOverride
::X|FR|rad.spectre.restore.065|                         and /v FeatureSettingsOverrideMask - both deleted via
