:: ---- rad.do.mode.killkey (repair) -------------------------------
::P|rad.do.mode.killkey|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.001|FIX A BROKEN UPDATE DOWNLOAD MODE
::T|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.002|Removes only the deprecated Bypass download mode that can leave Windows updates stuck at 0 percent forever - every other download mode, including the no-sharing ones, is left exactly as you set it.
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.001|REPARER UN MODE DE TELECHARGEMENT DES MISES A JOUR CASSE
::T|FR|rad.do.mode.killkey.002|Supprime uniquement le mode Bypass deprecie, qui peut bloquer les mises a jour Windows a 0 % indefiniment - tous les autres modes de telechargement, y compris ceux sans partage, restent exactement comme vous les avez regles.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.001|  What it is      : DODownloadMode is the policy that selects how Windows
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.002|                    fetches updates and Store content: peer to peer, LAN only,
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.003|                    plain HTTP, or bypassed entirely.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.004|
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.005|  Actual effect   : Deletes the policy only when it reads 100 (Bypass), which
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.006|                    Microsoft has deprecated and which is a known cause of
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.007|                    updates that never download. A deliberate mode 0, 1, 2, 3
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.008|                    or 99 (Simple - no cloud service, no peering) is left
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.009|                    alone. Note for the maintainer: line 1966 currently also
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.010|                    deletes 0x63 and contradicts this rule - it should be
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.011|                    removed.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.012|
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.013|  Gain            : On a machine stuck on mode 100, updates start downloading
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.014|                    again. On any other machine this step is a no-op and
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.015|                    should report nothing.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.016|
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.017|  Cost            : Nothing. If what you wanted was no peer sharing, mode 0
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.018|                    and mode 99 both still do that and neither is touched.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.019|
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.020|  Windows default : Policy value absent; Delivery Optimization falls back to
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.021|                    LAN peering on consumer editions.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.022|
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.023|  Possible values:
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.024|    100 (0x64) - Bypass  : Bypasses Delivery Optimization entirely and hands
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.025|                           downloads to the old BITS path. Microsoft has
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.026|                           deprecated it, and on 24H2 and later it is a
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.027|                           documented cause of updates that queue forever
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.028|                           without ever downloading. This is the ONLY value
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.029|                           this step removes.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.030|    99 (0x63) - Simple   : A documented, supported, working mode: downloads
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.031|                           come straight from the Microsoft servers, with no
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.032|                           Delivery Optimization cloud service and no peering.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.033|                           It is one of the modes Microsoft points people to
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.034|                           as the replacement for the deprecated Bypass.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.035|                           Deleting it would silently reverse a deliberate
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.036|                           configuration, so it must NOT be touched - this is
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.037|                           the correction applied to the previous version of
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.038|                           this card.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.039|    0 - HTTP only        : No peering at all, plain HTTP from Microsoft, but
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.040|                           the Delivery Optimization service still manages the
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.041|                           transfer. This is the value to use if what you want
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.042|                           is no sharing with other PCs - not 100.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.043|    1 - LAN peering      : Peers with other PCs on the same local network
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.044|                           only. The effective default behaviour on a consumer
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.045|                           Windows install.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.046|    2 - Group peering    : Peers within the same Active Directory site or
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.047|                           domain group. Intended for managed fleets.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.048|    3 - Internet peering : Peers with PCs on the internet as well as the LAN.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.049|                           Uses your upload bandwidth to serve strangers,
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.050|                           which is the setting people are usually looking for
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.051|                           when they go hunting in this key.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.052|    DELETE               : The shipped state: no policy value, and Delivery
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.053|                           Optimization uses its own default (LAN peering on
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.054|                           consumer editions).
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.055|
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.056|  Why these profiles : All five delete on 100 alone. A deprecated mode that
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.057|                       stops security updates from arriving is broken on every
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.058|                       kind of machine, and no profile has a reason to want
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.059|                       it. Everything else in this key is a deliberate
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.060|                       bandwidth choice, and a repair step has no business
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.061|                       overwriting one.
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.062|
::X|EN|rad.do.mode.killkey.063|  Known problems     : With mode 100 on 24H2 or later, Windows Update sits at
