:: ---- restore.point (risky) -------------------------------------
::P|restore.point|RUN|RUN|RUN|RUN|SKIP|
::T|EN|restore.point.001|CREATE A SYSTEM RESTORE POINT FIRST
::T|EN|restore.point.002|Before any risky step, OPTY saves a restore point so you can undo problems later, but it will not recover deleted files or removed apps.
::T|FR|restore.point.001|CREER D ABORD UN POINT DE RESTAURATION SYSTEME
::T|FR|restore.point.002|Avant toute etape risquee, OPTY enregistre un point de restauration pour permettre un retour arriere plus tard, mais il ne recupere ni fichiers supprimes ni applications retirees.
::X|EN|restore.point.001|  What it is      : Before anything destructive, OPTY temporarily lifts
::X|EN|restore.point.002|                    Windows' one-point-per-24-hours throttle, enables
::X|EN|restore.point.003|                    System Protection on the system drive, creates a
::X|EN|restore.point.004|                    MODIFY_SETTINGS restore point, and puts the throttle
::X|EN|restore.point.005|                    back.
::X|EN|restore.point.006|
::X|EN|restore.point.007|  Actual effect   : You get a restore point dated now, even if one was
::X|EN|restore.point.008|                    already made today. If System Protection was OFF on
::X|EN|restore.point.009|                    C:, this turns it ON - a real change, because Windows
::X|EN|restore.point.010|                    then starts reserving shadow-copy space on the system
::X|EN|restore.point.011|                    drive and keeps doing so after OPTY exits.
::X|EN|restore.point.012|
::X|EN|restore.point.013|  Gain            : A one-click way back if a later step goes wrong.
::X|EN|restore.point.014|                    Several steps in this tool have no OPTY undo at all -
::X|EN|restore.point.015|                    netsh int ip reset and winsock reset among them - so
::X|EN|restore.point.016|                    this is what makes the rest safe to try. Note what a
::X|EN|restore.point.017|                    restore point does not cover: it does not restore your
::X|EN|restore.point.018|                    files, and it will not undo a deleted cache or a
::X|EN|restore.point.019|                    removed app.
::X|EN|restore.point.020|
::X|EN|restore.point.021|  Cost            : Disk space: shadow storage typically takes a few
::X|EN|restore.point.022|                    percent of the system drive. It is also a genuine
::X|EN|restore.point.023|                    behaviour change on a machine where System Protection
::X|EN|restore.point.024|                    was deliberately off, and OPTY does not turn it back
::X|EN|restore.point.025|                    off afterwards. Creating the point takes 30-60
::X|EN|restore.point.026|                    seconds.
::X|EN|restore.point.027|
::X|EN|restore.point.028|  Windows default : The frequency value is absent, meaning 1440 minutes.
::X|EN|restore.point.029|                    On many Windows 11 clean installs System Protection is
::X|EN|restore.point.030|                    OFF for C:, so on those machines a 'yes' here is
::X|EN|restore.point.031|                    switching a feature on, not just taking a snapshot.
::X|EN|restore.point.032|
::X|EN|restore.point.033|  Possible values:
::X|EN|restore.point.034|    RUN                  : Turns System Protection on for the system drive
::X|EN|restore.point.035|                           if it was off, lifts the one-point-per-24-hours
::X|EN|restore.point.036|                           throttle, creates a restore point dated now,
::X|EN|restore.point.037|                           then puts the throttle back. Guarded by RP_DONE
::X|EN|restore.point.038|                           so it happens at most once per session. Takes
::X|EN|restore.point.039|                           30-60 seconds.
::X|EN|restore.point.040|    SKIP                 : No restore point, no change to System
::X|EN|restore.point.041|                           Protection, and no write to the frequency
::X|EN|restore.point.042|                           value. Everything downstream then runs with no
::X|EN|restore.point.043|                           OPTY-provided way back. Reasonable only if you
::X|EN|restore.point.044|                           already take full disk images.
::X|EN|restore.point.045|    SystemRestorePointCreationFrequency = 0 :
::X|EN|restore.point.046|        The throttle lifted: Windows will create a restore point on every
::X|EN|restore.point.047|        request instead of refusing a second one within 24 hours. OPTY
::X|EN|restore.point.048|        sets this only for the few seconds around Checkpoint-Computer.
