::X|EN|restore.point.050|        every application install can create its own point and shadow
::X|EN|restore.point.051|        storage fills much faster.
::X|EN|restore.point.052|    SystemRestorePointCreationFrequency = 1440 :
::X|EN|restore.point.053|        What OPTY writes back afterwards: 1440 minutes, i.e. one point per
::X|EN|restore.point.054|        24 hours. Behaviourally identical to the shipped state, but the
::X|EN|restore.point.055|        shipped state is the value being ABSENT - so this leaves behind a
::X|EN|restore.point.056|        value Windows never wrote. A :killkey would be the literal
::X|EN|restore.point.057|        restore.
::X|EN|restore.point.058|
::X|EN|restore.point.059|  Why these profiles : Four profiles run it, because the value of a way
::X|EN|restore.point.060|                       back does not depend on what the machine is for.
::X|EN|restore.point.061|                       Profile 5 is the only honest 'no': Windows does not
::X|EN|restore.point.062|                       create restore points on your behalf, and a machine
::X|EN|restore.point.063|                       that is already covered by full disk images gains
::X|EN|restore.point.064|                       nothing from a second, weaker safety net. If you
::X|EN|restore.point.065|                       say no, be aware that several later steps become
::X|EN|restore.point.066|                       one-way.
::X|EN|restore.point.067|
::X|EN|restore.point.068|  Known problems  : On a machine where System Protection is off and
::X|EN|restore.point.069|                    Enable-ComputerRestore fails - a Home edition with
::X|EN|restore.point.070|                    protection disabled by policy, or a system drive with
::X|EN|restore.point.071|                    no space for shadow storage - Checkpoint-Computer
::X|EN|restore.point.072|                    returns an error, OPTY prints 'Restore point not
::X|EN|restore.point.073|                    created' and continues. The rest of the run then has
::X|EN|restore.point.074|                    no restore point behind it.
::X|EN|restore.point.075|
::X|EN|restore.point.076|  Unverified      : OPTY writes 1440 back instead of deleting the value.
::X|EN|restore.point.077|                    The behaviour is identical to the absent default, but
::X|EN|restore.point.078|                    strictly this is not the shipped state and a later
::X|EN|restore.point.079|                    audit will read it as a deliberate setting. Also note
::X|EN|restore.point.080|                    this is the one place in OPTY that shells out to
::X|EN|restore.point.081|                    powershell.exe - the in-file comment justifies it as
::X|EN|restore.point.082|                    having no CMD equivalent since WMIC was removed; it is
::X|EN|restore.point.083|                    recorded here as a fact of the current implementation,
::X|EN|restore.point.084|                    not as a proposal.
::X|EN|restore.point.085|
::X|EN|restore.point.086|  Target          : :restore_point (OPTY.bat lines 3608-3632): Enable-
::X|EN|restore.point.087|                    ComputerRestore on %SystemDrive%, then
::X|EN|restore.point.088|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|restore.point.089|                    NT\CurrentVersion\SystemRestore /v
::X|EN|restore.point.090|                    SystemRestorePointCreationFrequency /t REG_DWORD /d 0,
::X|EN|restore.point.091|                    then Checkpoint-Computer -RestorePointType
::X|EN|restore.point.092|                    MODIFY_SETTINGS, then the frequency value written back
::X|EN|restore.point.093|                    as 1440
::X|FR|restore.point.001|  Ce que c est    : Avant toute operation destructrice, OPTY leve
::X|FR|restore.point.002|                    temporairement la limite d un point par 24 h, active
::X|FR|restore.point.003|                    la protection du systeme sur le disque systeme, cree
::X|FR|restore.point.004|                    un point de type MODIFY_SETTINGS, puis remet la
::X|FR|restore.point.005|                    limite.
::X|FR|restore.point.006|
::X|FR|restore.point.007|  Effet reel      : Vous obtenez un point de restauration date de
::X|FR|restore.point.008|                    maintenant, meme si un point a deja ete cree
::X|FR|restore.point.009|                    aujourd hui. Si la protection du systeme etait
::X|FR|restore.point.010|                    DESACTIVEE sur C:, elle est ACTIVEE : un vrai
::X|FR|restore.point.011|                    changement, car Windows se met alors a reserver de
::X|FR|restore.point.012|                    l espace pour les cliches sur le disque systeme, et
::X|FR|restore.point.013|                    continue apres la fermeture d OPTY.
::X|FR|restore.point.014|
::X|FR|restore.point.015|  Gain            : Un retour arriere en un clic si une etape ulterieure
::X|FR|restore.point.016|                    tourne mal. Plusieurs etapes de cet outil n ont aucune
::X|FR|restore.point.017|                    annulation dans OPTY - netsh int ip reset et winsock
::X|FR|restore.point.018|                    reset notamment : c est ce point qui rend le reste
::X|FR|restore.point.019|                    essayable sans risque. A savoir aussi ce qu un point
::X|FR|restore.point.020|                    de restauration ne couvre pas : il ne restaure pas vos
::X|FR|restore.point.021|                    fichiers et n annulera ni un cache supprime ni une
::X|FR|restore.point.022|                    application desinstallee.
::X|FR|restore.point.023|
::X|FR|restore.point.024|  Cout            : De l espace disque : le stockage des cliches prend
::X|FR|restore.point.025|                    typiquement quelques pour cent du disque systeme. Et
::X|FR|restore.point.026|                    c est un vrai changement de comportement sur une
::X|FR|restore.point.027|                    machine ou la protection avait ete coupee expres -
::X|FR|restore.point.028|                    OPTY ne la recoupe pas ensuite. La creation prend 30 a
::X|FR|restore.point.029|                    60 secondes.
::X|FR|restore.point.030|
::X|FR|restore.point.031|  Defaut Windows  : La valeur de frequence est absente, ce qui vaut 1440
::X|FR|restore.point.032|                    minutes. Sur beaucoup d installations propres de
::X|FR|restore.point.033|                    Windows 11, la protection du systeme est DESACTIVEE
::X|FR|restore.point.034|                    pour C: : sur ces machines, un « oui » ici active une
::X|FR|restore.point.035|                    fonctionnalite, il ne prend pas seulement un
::X|FR|restore.point.036|                    instantane.
::X|FR|restore.point.037|
::X|FR|restore.point.038|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|restore.point.039|    RUN                  : Active la protection du systeme sur le disque
::X|FR|restore.point.040|                           systeme si elle etait coupee, leve la limite
::X|FR|restore.point.041|                           d un point par 24 h, cree un point de
::X|FR|restore.point.042|                           restauration date de maintenant, puis remet la
::X|FR|restore.point.043|                           limite. Protege par RP_DONE : au plus une fois
::X|FR|restore.point.044|                           par session. Prend 30 a 60 secondes.
::X|FR|restore.point.045|    SKIP                 : Aucun point de restauration, aucun changement
::X|FR|restore.point.046|                           de la protection systeme, aucune ecriture de la
::X|FR|restore.point.047|                           valeur de frequence. Tout ce qui suit s execute
::X|FR|restore.point.048|                           alors sans retour arriere fourni par OPTY.
::X|FR|restore.point.049|                           Raisonnable seulement si vous faites deja des
::X|FR|restore.point.050|                           images completes du disque.
::X|FR|restore.point.051|    SystemRestorePointCreationFrequency = 0 :
::X|FR|restore.point.052|        Limite levee : Windows cree un point a chaque demande au lieu d en
::X|FR|restore.point.053|        refuser un second sous 24 h. OPTY ne la pose que le temps du
::X|FR|restore.point.054|        Checkpoint-Computer. Laissee a 0 en permanence - ce que faisaient
::X|FR|restore.point.055|        d anciennes versions d OPTY - chaque installation d application
::X|FR|restore.point.056|        peut creer son propre point et le stockage des cliches se remplit
::X|FR|restore.point.057|        bien plus vite.
::X|FR|restore.point.058|    SystemRestorePointCreationFrequency = 1440 :
::X|FR|restore.point.059|        Ce qu OPTY reecrit ensuite : 1440 minutes, soit un point par 24 h.
::X|FR|restore.point.060|        Comportement identique a l etat livre, mais l etat livre est la
::X|FR|restore.point.061|        valeur ABSENTE : cela laisse donc derriere soi une valeur que
::X|FR|restore.point.062|        Windows n a jamais ecrite. Un :killkey serait la restauration
::X|FR|restore.point.063|        litterale.
::X|FR|restore.point.064|
::X|FR|restore.point.065|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre profils l executent, car la valeur d un
::X|FR|restore.point.066|                         retour arriere ne depend pas de l usage de la
::X|FR|restore.point.067|                         machine. Le profil 5 est le seul « non » honnete
::X|FR|restore.point.068|                         : Windows ne cree pas de point de restauration de
::X|FR|restore.point.069|                         lui-meme, et une machine deja couverte par des
::X|FR|restore.point.070|                         images completes du disque ne gagne rien a un
::X|FR|restore.point.071|                         second filet de securite plus faible. Si vous
::X|FR|restore.point.072|                         refusez, sachez que plusieurs etapes suivantes
::X|FR|restore.point.073|                         deviennent sans retour.
::X|FR|restore.point.074|
::X|FR|restore.point.075|  Problemes connus : Sur une machine ou la protection est coupee et ou
::X|FR|restore.point.076|                     Enable-ComputerRestore echoue - edition Famille avec
::X|FR|restore.point.077|                     protection interdite par strategie, ou disque systeme
::X|FR|restore.point.078|                     sans place pour les cliches - Checkpoint-Computer
::X|FR|restore.point.079|                     renvoie une erreur, OPTY affiche « Restore point not
::X|FR|restore.point.080|                     created » et continue. Toute la suite s execute alors
::X|FR|restore.point.081|                     sans point de restauration derriere elle.
::X|FR|restore.point.082|
::X|FR|restore.point.083|  Non verifie (en)  : OPTY writes 1440 back instead of deleting the value.
::X|FR|restore.point.084|                      The behaviour is identical to the absent default,
::X|FR|restore.point.085|                      but strictly this is not the shipped state and a
::X|FR|restore.point.086|                      later audit will read it as a deliberate setting.
::X|FR|restore.point.087|                      Also note this is the one place in OPTY that shells
::X|FR|restore.point.088|                      out to powershell.exe - the in-file comment
::X|FR|restore.point.089|                      justifies it as having no CMD equivalent since WMIC
::X|FR|restore.point.090|                      was removed; it is recorded here as a fact of the
::X|FR|restore.point.091|                      current implementation, not as a proposal.
::X|FR|restore.point.092|
::X|FR|restore.point.093|  Cible           : :restore_point (OPTY.bat lines 3608-3632): Enable-
::X|FR|restore.point.094|                    ComputerRestore on %SystemDrive%, then
::X|FR|restore.point.095|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|restore.point.096|                    NT\CurrentVersion\SystemRestore /v
::X|FR|restore.point.097|                    SystemRestorePointCreationFrequency /t REG_DWORD /d 0,
::X|FR|restore.point.098|                    then Checkpoint-Computer -RestorePointType
::X|FR|restore.point.099|                    MODIFY_SETTINGS, then the frequency value written back
::X|FR|restore.point.100|                    as 1440
