::P|file.self.update|RUN|SKIP|RUN|RUN|SKIP|
::T|EN|file.self.update.001|OPTY UPDATES ITSELF FROM GITHUB
::T|EN|file.self.update.002|OPTY can download and replace itself with the latest version from GitHub so you get bug fixes, but that means running unreviewed code fetched over the network with no signature check.
::T|FR|file.self.update.001|OPTY SE MET A JOUR DEPUIS GITHUB
::T|FR|file.self.update.002|OPTY peut se telecharger et se remplacer par la derniere version depuis GitHub pour recuperer les corrections, mais cela veut dire executer du code non relu, telecharge sans signature ni verification.
::X|EN|file.self.update.001|  What it is      : OPTY compares its own 'set current_version=05.1'
::X|EN|file.self.update.002|                    against the tag_name of the latest GitHub release and,
::X|EN|file.self.update.003|                    if they differ, offers to download and replace itself.
::X|EN|file.self.update.004|                    The API call is unauthenticated, so it is rate limited
::X|EN|file.self.update.005|                    to 60 requests per hour per IP; if tag_name cannot be
::X|EN|file.self.update.006|                    read the check bails out and reports no update rather
::X|EN|file.self.update.007|                    than guessing.
::X|EN|file.self.update.008|
::X|EN|file.self.update.009|  Actual effect   : curl -f -L writes new_OPTY.bat next to the script. The
::X|EN|file.self.update.010|                    payload is rejected unless it contains 'set
::X|EN|file.self.update.011|                    current_version='. Line endings are forced to CRLF.
::X|EN|file.self.update.012|                    OPTY.bat is copied to OPTY_rollback.bat, the new file
::X|EN|file.self.update.013|                    is moved over it, and the script relaunches. On any
::X|EN|file.self.update.014|                    failure the download is deleted and nothing on disk
::X|EN|file.self.update.015|                    changes.
::X|EN|file.self.update.016|
::X|EN|file.self.update.017|  Gain            : You get the fixes. This script's job is deleting files
::X|EN|file.self.update.018|                    and writing registry values, so running the corrected
::X|EN|file.self.update.019|                    version matters more here than it would for ordinary
::X|EN|file.self.update.020|                    software - a bug in OPTY is a bug that touches your
::X|EN|file.self.update.021|                    machine's configuration.
::X|EN|file.self.update.022|
::X|EN|file.self.update.023|  Cost            : You run code you have not read, fetched over the
::X|EN|file.self.update.024|                    network. The -f flag and the content check block the
::X|EN|file.self.update.025|                    two failure modes that previously overwrote OPTY.bat
::X|EN|file.self.update.026|                    with a 404 error page, and OPTY_rollback.bat gives you
::X|EN|file.self.update.027|                    a way back, but nothing verifies the author: there is
::X|EN|file.self.update.028|                    no signature and no checksum. Trust here is entirely
::X|EN|file.self.update.029|                    trust in the repository. Nothing else on the machine
::X|EN|file.self.update.030|                    changes.
::X|EN|file.self.update.031|
::X|EN|file.self.update.032|  Windows default : Not applicable - this is OPTY updating itself, not a
::X|EN|file.self.update.033|                    Windows setting.
::X|EN|file.self.update.034|
::X|EN|file.self.update.035|  Possible values:
::X|EN|file.self.update.036|    RUN                  : Answer Y. curl fetches the release asset with
::X|EN|file.self.update.037|                           -f (fail on HTTP error instead of writing the
::X|EN|file.self.update.038|                           error page to disk). The file is rejected
::X|EN|file.self.update.039|                           unless it contains the literal string 'set
::X|EN|file.self.update.040|                           current_version='. It is then rewritten line by
::X|EN|file.self.update.041|                           line so every line ends CRLF. The current
::X|EN|file.self.update.042|                           OPTY.bat is copied to OPTY_rollback.bat, the
::X|EN|file.self.update.043|                           new file is moved over OPTY.bat, and the script
::X|EN|file.self.update.044|                           relaunches in a new console window. Any failure
::X|EN|file.self.update.045|                           at any step deletes the download and leaves
::X|EN|file.self.update.046|                           OPTY.bat untouched.
::X|EN|file.self.update.047|    SKIP                 : Anything other than Y, including a bare Enter.
::X|EN|file.self.update.048|                           Nothing is downloaded, nothing is replaced, the
::X|EN|file.self.update.049|                           run continues on the current version. This is
::X|EN|file.self.update.050|                           the code's own fall-through: an earlier build
::X|EN|file.self.update.051|                           fell straight into the update on Enter, which
::X|EN|file.self.update.052|                           is why the branch is now explicit.
::X|EN|file.self.update.053|
::X|EN|file.self.update.054|  Why these profiles : Three of the five say yes for the same reason and
::X|EN|file.self.update.055|                       it is not a performance reason: a desktop, an
::X|EN|file.self.update.056|                       office PC and a laptop all have someone at the
::X|EN|file.self.update.057|                       keyboard when the replaced script relaunches. The
::X|EN|file.self.update.058|                       server says no because of the relaunch specifically
::X|EN|file.self.update.059|                       - 'start "" OPTY.bat' opens a new console that
::X|EN|file.self.update.060|                       nobody is sitting in front of on a headless box,
::X|EN|file.self.update.061|                       and swapping the running maintenance script for an
::X|EN|file.self.update.062|                       unreviewed one on an unattended machine is not a
::X|EN|file.self.update.063|                       trade a server profile should make silently.
::X|EN|file.self.update.064|                       WINDOWS is SKIP because OPTY's own shipped answer
::X|EN|file.self.update.065|                       is no: anything that is not Y, including a bare
::X|EN|file.self.update.066|                       Enter, declines.
::X|EN|file.self.update.067|
::X|EN|file.self.update.068|  Known problems  : Measured against the live repository: the
::X|EN|file.self.update.069|                    releases/latest/download/OPTY.bat asset arrives with 0
::X|EN|file.self.update.070|                    CR and 1854 lone LF, while the
::X|EN|file.self.update.071|                    raw.githubusercontent.com copy arrives with 3530 CR
::X|EN|file.self.update.072|                    and no lone LF. CMD computes the return address of
::X|EN|file.self.update.073|                    call and goto :eof as a byte offset assuming 2-byte
::X|EN|file.self.update.074|                    CRLF, so an LF-only OPTY.bat drifts into the wrong
::X|EN|file.self.update.075|                    section partway through a long run. That is the
::X|EN|file.self.update.076|                    'sometimes it works, sometimes it jumps somewhere
::X|EN|file.self.update.077|                    random' the CRLF normalisation step now prevents.
::X|EN|file.self.update.078|                    Separately, behind a shared or NATed IP the
::X|EN|file.self.update.079|                    unauthenticated GitHub API can hit its 60/hour limit
::X|EN|file.self.update.080|                    and the check reports 'you are running the latest
::X|EN|file.self.update.081|                    version' when you are not.
::X|EN|file.self.update.082|
::X|EN|file.self.update.083|  Unverified      : The transfer is HTTPS to GitHub, but nothing verifies
::X|EN|file.self.update.084|                    who built the release - no signature, no checksum.
::X|EN|file.self.update.085|                    Whether the releases asset still ships LF-only today
::X|EN|file.self.update.086|                    was measured once, on the version quoted in the code
::X|EN|file.self.update.087|                    comments; GitHub could change that at any time, which
::X|EN|file.self.update.088|                    is precisely why the script normalises rather than
::X|EN|file.self.update.089|                    trusting the server.
::X|EN|file.self.update.090|
::X|EN|file.self.update.091|  Target          : OPTY.bat :update_opty / :update_found_and_accepted
::X|EN|file.self.update.092|                    (lines 218-305): curl -f -L -o "%~dp0new_OPTY.bat"
::X|EN|file.self.update.093|                    %GitHubLatestLink%OPTY.bat, validate with find /c "set
