:: ---- regsvr32.update.dlls (repair) ------------------------------
::P|regsvr32.update.dlls|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.001|RE-REGISTER THREE OLD WINDOWS COMPONENTS (ATL, URLMON, MSHTML)
::T|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.002|This re-registers three components from an old Windows 7-era Update fix, but on a healthy Windows 11 PC it changes nothing measurable, so OPTY skips it by default.
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.001|REENREGISTRER TROIS ANCIENS COMPOSANTS WINDOWS (ATL, URLMON, MSHTML)
::T|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.002|Ceci reenregistre trois anciens composants Windows issus d une astuce datant de Windows 7, mais sur un Windows 11 en bon etat cela ne change rien de mesurable, donc OPTY le saute par defaut.
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.001|  What it is      : Re-registers three COM libraries - atl.dll, urlmon.dll
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.002|                    and mshtml.dll - by rewriting their CLSID entries
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.003|                    under HKCR. It comes from the classic 'reset Windows
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.004|                    Update' recipe that circulated in the Windows 7 era,
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.005|                    where it sat alongside renaming SoftwareDistribution
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.006|                    and catroot2.
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.007|
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.008|  Actual effect   : Each DllRegisterServer call writes that library's COM
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.009|                    registration keys again. On a machine where those keys
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.010|                    are intact, the same values go back. Note that the
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.011|                    surrounding block has already stopped bits, wuauserv,
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.012|                    msiserver, cryptsvc and appidsvc, so this runs with
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.013|                    cryptographic services down.
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.014|
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.015|  Gain            : No measurable gain on a healthy Windows 11 install.
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.016|                    This repairs damage a modern machine essentially never
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.017|                    has, and there is no mechanism by which re-writing an
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.018|                    already-correct CLSID makes updates faster, more
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.019|                    reliable, or anything else. Saying it 'helps Windows
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.020|                    Update' is exactly the kind of claim this script must
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.021|                    not make.
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.022|
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.023|  Cost            : Essentially none, but not free of doubt. mshtml.dll
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.024|                    belongs to the Internet Explorer engine that Microsoft
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.025|                    has been removing from Windows 11, so that third call
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.026|                    can simply fail; /s hides the result either way, so
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.027|                    OPTY cannot tell you and does not claim to. The real
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.028|                    cost is time and the false confidence of having 'done
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.029|                    something'.
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.030|
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.031|  Windows default : Already registered on any healthy install. This is a
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.032|                    no-op unless the COM entries were genuinely damaged.
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.033|
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.034|  Possible values:
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.035|    RUN                  : regsvr32 /s loads each DLL and calls its
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.036|                           DllRegisterServer entry point, which rewrites
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.037|                           that library's CLSID, ProgID and InprocServer32
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.038|                           entries under HKCR. On a machine where those
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.039|                           keys are intact it writes byte-identical values
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.040|                           back. /s suppresses the result dialog, so a
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.041|                           success and a failure look exactly the same
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.042|                           from OPTY's side.
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.043|    SKIP                 : Nothing runs. The COM registrations stay
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.044|                           exactly as Windows shipped them, which on any
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.045|                           healthy install is the same thing RUN would
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.046|                           have produced.
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.047|
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.048|  Why these profiles : All five are SKIP, and that is the finding rather
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.049|                       than a gap in the table. This step has no
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.050|                       performance, power or throughput dimension at all,
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.051|                       so there is nothing for gaming, server, office and
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.052|                       laptop to disagree about - and since it is a no-op
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.053|                       on an undamaged machine, WINDOWS lands on the same
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.054|                       answer for a different reason. The one case where
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.055|                       it belongs is not a profile: it is a machine whose
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.056|                       Windows Update is genuinely failing with COM or
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.057|                       0x8007 errors, being worked through a repair
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.058|                       sequence by hand.
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.059|
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.060|  Unverified      : Whether mshtml.dll still registers successfully on
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.061|                    current Windows 11 25H2 builds was not verified, and
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.062|                    /s hides the result either way, so OPTY cannot report
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.063|                    whether any of the three calls did anything.
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.064|
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.065|  Target          : No longer executed at all. The :startready block
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.066|                    used to call regsvr32.exe /s on these three DLLs right
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.067|                    after stopping bits, wuauserv, msiserver, cryptsvc and
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.068|                    appidsvc; those three lines were removed from the code,
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.069|                    precisely because this card already recommended SKIP
::X|EN|regsvr32.update.dlls.070|                    on every profile.
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.001|  Ce que c est    : Reenregistre trois bibliotheques COM - atl.dll,
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.002|                    urlmon.dll et mshtml.dll - en reecrivant leurs entrees
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.003|                    CLSID sous HKCR. Ca vient de la recette classique de «
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.004|                    reinitialisation de Windows Update » de l epoque
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.005|                    Windows 7, ou elle voisinait avec le renommage de
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.006|                    SoftwareDistribution et de catroot2.
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.007|
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.008|  Effet reel      : Chaque appel a DllRegisterServer reecrit les cles
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.009|                    d enregistrement COM de la bibliotheque. Sur une
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.010|                    machine ou ces cles sont intactes, les memes valeurs
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.011|                    reviennent. A noter : le bloc environnant vient
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.012|                    d arreter bits, wuauserv, msiserver, cryptsvc et
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.013|                    appidsvc, donc tout ceci tourne avec les services
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.014|                    cryptographiques a l arret.
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.015|
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.016|  Gain            : Aucun gain mesurable sur une installation Windows 11
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.017|                    saine. Ca repare un degat qu une machine moderne n a
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.018|                    pour ainsi dire jamais, et aucun mecanisme ne fait
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.019|                    qu une CLSID deja correcte, reecrite a l identique,
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.020|                    rende les mises a jour plus rapides ou plus fiables.
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.021|                    Dire que « ca aide Windows Update » est precisement le
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.022|                    genre d affirmation que ce script ne doit pas
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.023|                    produire.
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.024|
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.025|  Cout            : Quasiment nul, mais pas sans zone d ombre. mshtml.dll
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.026|                    appartient au moteur d Internet Explorer que Microsoft
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.027|                    retire progressivement de Windows 11 : ce troisieme
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.028|                    appel peut simplement echouer. Le /s masque le
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.029|                    resultat dans les deux cas, donc OPTY ne peut rien
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.030|                    vous en dire, et il ne pretend pas le contraire. Le
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.031|                    vrai cout, c est le temps et la fausse assurance
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.032|                    d avoir « fait quelque chose ».
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.033|
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.034|  Defaut Windows  : Deja enregistrees sur toute installation saine. Sans
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.035|                    effet, sauf si les entrees COM etaient reellement
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.036|                    abimees.
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.037|
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.038|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.039|    RUN                  : regsvr32 /s charge chaque DLL et appelle son
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.040|                           point d entree DllRegisterServer, qui reecrit
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.041|                           les entrees CLSID, ProgID et InprocServer32 de
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.042|                           la bibliotheque sous HKCR. Sur une machine ou
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.043|                           ces cles sont intactes, il reecrit des valeurs
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.044|                           strictement identiques. Le /s supprime la boite
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.045|                           de dialogue de resultat : cote OPTY, un succes
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.046|                           et un echec se ressemblent exactement.
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.047|    SKIP                 : Rien ne s execute. Les enregistrements COM
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.048|                           restent tels que Windows les a livres, ce qui,
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.049|                           sur toute installation saine, donne exactement
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.050|                           le meme resultat que RUN.
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.051|
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.052|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq profils repondent SKIP, et c est la
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.053|                         conclusion, pas un trou dans le tableau. Cette
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.054|                         etape n a aucune dimension performance, energie
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.055|                         ou debit : gaming, serveur, bureautique et
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.056|                         portable n ont rien sur quoi diverger - et comme
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.057|                         c est une operation sans effet sur une machine
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.058|                         intacte, WINDOWS arrive au meme endroit pour une
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.059|                         autre raison. Le seul cas ou elle a sa place
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.060|                         n est pas un profil : c est une machine dont
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.061|                         Windows Update echoue vraiment avec des erreurs
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.062|                         COM ou en 0x8007, qu on repare a la main etape
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.063|                         par etape.
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.064|
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.065|  Non verifie (en)  : Whether mshtml.dll still registers successfully on
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.066|                      current Windows 11 25H2 builds was not verified, and
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.067|                      /s hides the result either way, so OPTY cannot
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.068|                      report whether any of the three calls did anything.
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.069|
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.070|  Cible           : Plus execute du tout. Le bloc :startready appelait
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.071|                    autrefois regsvr32.exe /s sur ces trois DLL juste
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.072|                    apres avoir arrete bits, wuauserv, msiserver, cryptsvc
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.073|                    et appidsvc ; ces trois lignes ont ete retirees du
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.074|                    code, precisement parce que cette carte recommandait
::X|FR|regsvr32.update.dlls.075|                    deja SKIP sur tous les profils.
