::T|EN|storagesense.enable.001|TURN ON STORAGE SENSE (AUTOMATIC DISK CLEANUP)
::T|EN|storagesense.enable.002|This turns on Windows automatic disk cleanup so temp files get removed between OPTY runs, but it also gives that cleanup permission to empty the Recycle Bin on whatever schedule this machine already had.
::T|FR|storagesense.enable.001|ACTIVER L ASSISTANT STOCKAGE (NETTOYAGE AUTOMATIQUE)
::T|FR|storagesense.enable.002|Ceci active le nettoyage automatique du disque de Windows pour supprimer les fichiers temporaires entre deux passages d OPTY, mais cela donne aussi a ce nettoyage le droit de vider la Corbeille selon la regle deja presente sur cette machine.
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.001|  What it is      : Storage Sense is the cleanup engine built into Windows
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.002|                    11 (Settings > System > Storage). This step writes the
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.003|                    master ON switch for the account running OPTY, in HKCU
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.004|                    - deliberately not the HKLM policy, so the Settings
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.005|                    toggle stays clickable instead of showing 'managed by
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.006|                    your organization'.
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.007|
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.008|  Actual effect   : Sets StoragePolicy value 01 to 1. From then on Windows
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.009|                    runs its own cleanup pass on the cadence set by value
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.010|                    2048, with no help from OPTY.
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.011|
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.012|  Gain            : The machine keeps cleaning temp files between OPTY
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.013|                    runs. That is the whole benefit - it does not make
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.014|                    anything faster, it just means the disk does not
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.015|                    silently refill in the months you forget to run this
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.016|                    tool.
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.017|
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.018|  Cost            : You give a scheduled job standing permission to delete
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.019|                    files. Downloads deletion is pinned off by the
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.020|                    companion step, but the Recycle Bin rule is NOT
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.021|                    written by OPTY - values 08 and 128 keep whatever this
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.022|                    machine already had, which on a second-hand or
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.023|                    previously 'optimised' PC can be as short as one day.
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.024|                    Files Storage Sense removes from the Recycle Bin are
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.025|                    gone for good; there is no second bin. Open Settings >
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.026|                    System > Storage > Storage Sense after the run and set
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.027|                    the Recycle Bin threshold yourself.
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.028|
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.029|  Windows default : Varies by image. The toggle ships Off on some Windows
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.030|                    11 installs and On after OOBE on others, and Windows
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.031|                    turns it on by itself when free space gets low. OPTY
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.032|                    cannot tell you which one your machine was without
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.033|                    reading the value first.
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.034|
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.035|  Possible values:
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.036|    1                    : Master switch on. The scheduled task under
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.037|                           \Microsoft\Windows\DiskCleanup is allowed to
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.038|                           run Storage Sense on the cadence set by value
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.039|                           2048, and it applies whichever sub-rules are
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.040|                           set on this machine - temp files (04), Recycle
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.041|                           Bin (08 and 128), Downloads (32 and 512), cloud
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.042|                           content dehydration. It is the enabler; it does
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.043|                           not itself decide what gets deleted.
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.044|    0                    : Master switch explicitly off. No scheduled pass
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.045|                           runs and the sub-rules are inert - but Windows
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.046|                           still turns Storage Sense on by itself when
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.047|                           free space gets critically low, so 0 is not a
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.048|                           permanent guarantee.
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.049|    DELETE               : Remove the value. Settings shows the toggle
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.050|                           Off, exactly as 0 does, but leaves no state
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.051|                           written by anyone - which is the honest 'as
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.052|                           shipped' answer given that the shipped state
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.053|                           genuinely varies between images. Windows will
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.054|                           still self-enable at low free space.
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.055|
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.056|  Why these profiles : Four of the five write 1 and there is no
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.057|                       performance argument among them - a gaming rig, a
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.058|                       media and torrent box, a family desktop and a
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.059|                       laptop all fill their disks with the same temp
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.060|                       files and all benefit from the same unattended
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.061|                       sweep, so inventing a split here would be
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.062|                       dishonest. If anything the server case is the
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.063|                       strongest: an unattended box is exactly the machine
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.064|                       nobody notices filling up. WINDOWS is DELETE rather
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.065|                       than 0 because the shipped state genuinely varies
::X|EN|storagesense.enable.066|                       between images and 'no value written by anyone' is
