::
:: ---- windows.update.policy.reenable (repair) --------------------
::P|windows.update.policy.reenable|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.001|UNBLOCK WINDOWS UPDATE POLICIES
::T|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.002|This removes four registry policies that GPOs or debloat scripts commonly use to cripple Windows Update, restoring updates and driver delivery, though it also clears a deliberate driver-pinning choice if you had made one.
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.001|DEBLOQUER LES STRATEGIES DE WINDOWS UPDATE
::T|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.002|Ceci supprime quatre strategies de registre couramment utilisees par des GPO ou des scripts de debloat pour paralyser Windows Update, ce qui retablit les mises a jour et les pilotes, mais annule aussi un blocage de pilote que vous auriez pose exprès.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.001|  What it is      : Four registry policies are commonly used - by company
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.002|                    GPOs and by 'debloat' scripts - to cripple Windows
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.003|                    Update: DisableWindowsUpdateAccess and
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.004|                    SetDisableUXWUAccess (both hide or disable the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.005|                    Settings page), ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.006|                    (blocks driver updates), and UseWUServer (points the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.007|                    PC at a WSUS server that may no longer exist).
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.008|
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.009|  Actual effect   : The correct effect is deletion, not zeroing, because
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.010|                    the shipped state is that none of these values exist.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.011|                    Writing 0 works behaviourally but materialises a
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.012|                    policy key that was absent, and a populated
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.013|                    WindowsUpdate policy key is one of the things that
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.014|                    makes Settings display 'Some settings are managed by
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.015|                    your organization'. The code as it stands today writes
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.016|                    0 to all four - that is a known deviation from the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.017|                    shipped state, and OPTY's own doctrine elsewhere (the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.018|                    killkey helper, and the rad-wu-policy-purge step,
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.019|                    which does delete these very names) says the opposite.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.020|                    The service is stopped and restarted around the change
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.021|                    so it is picked up.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.022|
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.023|  Gain            : Windows Update works again: the Settings page opens,
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.024|                    updates come from Microsoft instead of a dead WSUS
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.025|                    server, and driver updates are allowed. On an ex-
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.026|                    corporate second-hand PC this is often the difference
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.027|                    between a machine that patches itself and one that
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.028|                    silently never does.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.029|
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.030|  Cost            : Re-allowing driver updates is a real trade-off: some
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.031|                    people set ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1 on
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.032|                    purpose to stop Windows overwriting a hand-picked GPU
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.033|                    or audio driver. If that is you, put it back to 1
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.034|                    afterwards - deliberately, as your own choice rather
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.035|                    than as a leftover. On a domain-joined PC the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.036|                    corporate GPO rewrites all four at the next refresh,
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.037|                    so the effect there is temporary either way.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.038|
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.039|  Windows default : None of these values exist on a stock Windows 11
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.040|                    install - the policy key is absent, which is 'not
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.041|                    configured'.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.042|
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.043|  Possible values:
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.044|    DELETE               : Remove all four values. This is the only write
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.045|                           that reproduces the shipped state, because on a
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.046|                           stock Windows 11 install the WindowsUpdate
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.047|                           policy key does not exist. When the key ends up
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.048|                           empty, Settings > Windows Update stops showing
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.049|                           'Some settings are managed by your
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.050|                           organization' and the greyed-out controls
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.051|                           become clickable again. On a machine that was
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.052|                           never blocked, deleting is a silent no-op.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.053|    DisableWindowsUpdateAccess=1 :
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.054|        The user cannot reach Windows Update at all: the Settings page
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.055|        refuses to open and manual checks are blocked. Set to 0 it permits
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.056|        access, but the value still exists, so the policy key remains
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.057|        populated.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.058|    SetDisableUXWUAccess=1 :
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.059|        Hides or disables the Windows Update surface in Settings - the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.060|        modern counterpart of the value above. 0 re-enables the page and,
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.061|        again, leaves a policy value behind.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.062|    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1 :
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.063|        Windows Update never offers drivers or firmware as part of a
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.064|        quality update. Some people set this deliberately so Windows
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.065|        cannot overwrite a hand-picked GPU or audio driver. 0 re-allows
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.066|        drivers; absent means the same thing, without the managed marker.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.067|    UseWUServer=1        : Windows takes its updates from the WSUS server
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.068|                           named in WUServer instead of from Microsoft. If
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.069|                           that server no longer exists - the normal case
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.070|                           on an ex-corporate machine sold on - every scan
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.071|                           fails and the PC quietly stops patching. 0
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.072|                           sends it back to Microsoft; deleting does the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.073|                           same and removes the policy.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.074|
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.075|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETEs, and the uniformity is the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.076|                       finding: this is not a tuning knob, it is the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.077|                       removal of a block. There is no machine role that
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.078|                       benefits from Windows Update being unreachable or
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.079|                       from pointing at a WSUS server that answers
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.080|                       nothing. The one place a profile could plausibly
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.081|                       differ is ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate, where a
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.082|                       gaming rig might want drivers pinned - but that
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.083|                       argument belongs to the driver-delivery cards, and
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.084|                       re-arming it should be a deliberate decision made
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.085|                       afterwards, not a leftover this step preserved by
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.086|                       accident.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.087|
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.088|  Known problems  : Two concrete ones. First, writing 0 rather than
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.089|                    deleting leaves the policy key populated, and the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.090|                    symptom is visible in the Settings app: Windows Update
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.091|                    keeps the 'Some settings are managed by your
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.092|                    organization' banner and some controls stay greyed
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.093|                    even though updates now work. Second, the block runs
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.094|                    'Net stop wuauserv' and 'Net start wuauserv' with no
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.095|                    output redirection and no error check - on a machine
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.096|                    where wuauserv has been set to Disabled, which is
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.097|                    exactly the machine this step is aimed at, net start
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.098|                    fails with 'The requested service has already been
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.099|                    started' or 'The service cannot be started' and the
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.100|                    error scrolls past while OPTY reports nothing.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.101|
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.102|  Unverified      : Whether an empty-but-present WindowsUpdate policy key
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.103|                    alone is enough to keep the 'managed by your
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.104|                    organization' banner, or whether the key must contain
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.105|                    at least one value, was not tested byte-for-byte on
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.106|                    25H2. Deleting the values is correct either way.
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.107|
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.108|  Target          : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.109|                    /v DisableWindowsUpdateAccess, /v
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.110|                    SetDisableUXWUAccess, /v
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.111|                    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate and HKLM\SOFTWARE\Poli
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.112|                    cies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate\AU /v UseWUServer
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.113|                    - OPTY.bat :enable_windows_update, lines 1447-1453,
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.114|                    currently four REG ADD ... /d 0 /f between Net stop
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.115|                    wuauserv and Net start wuauserv. The profile table
::X|EN|windows.update.policy.reenable.116|                    below calls for :killkey on all four instead.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.001|  Ce que c est    : Quatre strategies de registre servent couramment - aux
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.002|                    GPO d entreprise comme aux scripts de « debloat » - a
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.003|                    paralyser Windows Update : DisableWindowsUpdateAccess
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.004|                    et SetDisableUXWUAccess (masquent ou desactivent la
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.005|                    page des Parametres), ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.006|                    (bloque les pilotes) et UseWUServer (pointe le PC vers
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.007|                    un serveur WSUS parfois disparu).
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.008|
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.009|  Effet reel      : L effet correct est la suppression, pas la mise a
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.010|                    zero, parce que l etat d origine est que ces valeurs
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.011|                    n existent pas. Ecrire 0 fonctionne en comportement
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.012|                    mais materialise une cle de strategie qui etait
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.013|                    absente, et une cle WindowsUpdate peuplee est
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.014|                    justement une des choses qui font afficher « Certains
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.015|                    parametres sont geres par votre organisation » dans
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.016|                    les Parametres. Le code actuel ecrit 0 dans les quatre
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.017|                    : c est un ecart assume par rapport a l etat
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.018|                    d origine, et la doctrine d OPTY ailleurs (l assistant
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.019|                    killkey, et l etape rad-wu-policy-purge qui supprime
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.020|                    exactement ces noms) dit l inverse. Le service est
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.021|                    arrete et redemarre autour du changement pour qu il
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.022|                    soit pris en compte.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.023|
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.024|  Gain            : Windows Update refonctionne : la page des Parametres
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.025|                    s ouvre, les mises a jour viennent de Microsoft et non
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.026|                    d un serveur WSUS mort, et les pilotes sont de nouveau
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.027|                    autorises. Sur un PC d occasion venant d une
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.028|                    entreprise, c est souvent la difference entre une
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.029|                    machine qui se met a jour et une qui ne le fait plus
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.030|                    jamais en silence.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.031|
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.032|  Cout            : Reautoriser les pilotes est un vrai compromis :
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.033|                    certains mettent volontairement
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.034|                    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1 pour empecher
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.035|                    Windows d ecraser un pilote graphique ou audio choisi
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.036|                    a la main. Si c est votre cas, remettez-le a 1 ensuite
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.037|                    - deliberément, comme votre choix, et non comme un
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.038|                    residu. Sur un PC dans un domaine, la GPO d entreprise
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.039|                    reecrit les quatre au prochain rafraichissement :
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.040|                    l effet y est de toute facon temporaire.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.041|
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.042|  Defaut Windows  : Aucune de ces valeurs n existe sur une installation
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.043|                    Windows 11 d origine - la cle de strategie est
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.044|                    absente, ce qui signifie « non configure ».
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.045|
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.046|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.047|    DELETE               : Supprimer les quatre valeurs. C est la seule
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.048|                           ecriture qui reproduise l etat d origine,
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.049|                           puisque sur une installation Windows 11
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.050|                           d origine la cle de strategie WindowsUpdate
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.051|                           n existe pas. Une fois la cle vide, Parametres
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.052|                           > Windows Update cesse d afficher « Certains
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.053|                           parametres sont geres par votre organisation »
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.054|                           et les controles grises redeviennent
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.055|                           cliquables. Sur une machine jamais bloquee, la
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.056|                           suppression ne fait rien et ne dit rien.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.057|    DisableWindowsUpdateAccess=1 :
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.058|        L utilisateur ne peut plus atteindre Windows Update du tout : la
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.059|        page des Parametres refuse de s ouvrir et les recherches manuelles
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.060|        sont bloquees. Mise a 0 elle autorise l acces, mais la valeur
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.061|        existe toujours, donc la cle de strategie reste peuplee.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.062|    SetDisableUXWUAccess=1 :
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.063|        Masque ou desactive la partie Windows Update des Parametres -
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.064|        l equivalent moderne de la valeur precedente. 0 reactive la page
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.065|        et, la encore, laisse une valeur de strategie derriere elle.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.066|    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1 :
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.067|        Windows Update ne propose plus jamais de pilotes ni de firmware
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.068|        dans une mise a jour de qualite. Certains la posent volontairement
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.069|        pour que Windows n ecrase pas un pilote graphique ou audio choisi
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.070|        a la main. 0 reautorise les pilotes ; l absence signifie la meme
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.071|        chose, sans la marque « gere ».
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.072|    UseWUServer=1        : Windows prend ses mises a jour sur le serveur
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.073|                           WSUS indique dans WUServer au lieu de
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.074|                           Microsoft. Si ce serveur n existe plus - le cas
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.075|                           normal sur une machine d occasion venue d une
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.076|                           entreprise - chaque recherche echoue et le PC
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.077|                           cesse discretement de se corriger. 0 le renvoie
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.078|                           vers Microsoft ; la suppression fait de meme et
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.079|                           retire la strategie.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.080|
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.081|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq DELETE identiques, et cette uniformite est
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.082|                         la conclusion : ce n est pas un reglage fin,
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.083|                         c est le retrait d un blocage. Aucun role de
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.084|                         machine ne gagne a ce que Windows Update soit
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.085|                         inaccessible ou pointe vers un serveur WSUS qui
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.086|                         ne repond plus. Le seul endroit ou un profil
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.087|                         pourrait diverger est
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.088|                         ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate, ou une machine
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.089|                         de jeu voudrait figer ses pilotes - mais cet
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.090|                         argument appartient aux fiches sur la livraison
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.091|                         des pilotes, et le rearmer doit etre une decision
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.092|                         prise ensuite, pas un residu conserve par
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.093|                         accident.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.094|
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.095|  Problemes connus : Deux cas concrets. D abord, ecrire 0 au lieu de
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.096|                     supprimer laisse la cle de strategie peuplee, et le
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.097|                     symptome se voit dans l application Parametres :
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.098|                     Windows Update conserve le bandeau « Certains
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.099|                     parametres sont geres par votre organisation » et
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.100|                     certains controles restent grises alors que les mises
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.101|                     a jour fonctionnent de nouveau. Ensuite, le bloc
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.102|                     execute « Net stop wuauserv » et « Net start wuauserv
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.103|                     » sans redirection de sortie ni controle d erreur :
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.104|                     sur une machine ou wuauserv a ete mis sur Desactive -
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.105|                     c est-a-dire exactement la machine visee par cette
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.106|                     etape - net start echoue avec « Le service demande a
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.107|                     deja ete demarre » ou « Le service n a pas pu
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.108|                     demarrer », et l erreur defile pendant qu OPTY n en
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.109|                     dit rien.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.110|
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.111|  Non verifie (en)  : Whether an empty-but-present WindowsUpdate policy
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.112|                      key alone is enough to keep the  managed by your
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.113|                      organization  banner, or whether the key must
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.114|                      contain at least one value, was not tested byte-for-
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.115|                      byte on 25H2. Deleting the values is correct either
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.116|                      way.
::X|FR|windows.update.policy.reenable.117|
