:: ---- rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys (cleanup) -------------------------
::P|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.001|CLEAR UPDATE BANDWIDTH CAP LEFTOVERS
::T|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.002|Removes three old Delivery Optimization bandwidth-limit entries that Windows 11 mostly ignores already - it is leftover cleanup, not a speed fix, so do not expect faster updates.
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.001|RETIRER LES LIMITES DE DEBIT RESIDUELLES DES MISES A JOUR
::T|FR|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.002|Retire trois anciennes valeurs de limite de debit Delivery Optimization que Windows 11 ignore deja la plupart du temps - c est du nettoyage de residus, pas une acceleration, n attendez pas de mises a jour plus rapides.
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.001|  What it is      : Three Delivery Optimization policy values that were
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.002|                    the way to throttle how fast updates download and
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.003|                    upload. All three are the pre-Windows-10-2004 spelling
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.004|                    of the setting.
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.005|
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.006|  Actual effect   : Deletes all three if present, using the killkey helper
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.007|                    so a value that was never there is left alone and
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.008|                    nothing is reported. Alongside this, the code
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.009|                    separately deletes DODownloadMode but only when it
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.010|                    currently reads 100 (Bypass, deprecated and known to
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.011|                    break downloads on 24H2 and later) or 99 (Simple, the
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.012|                    offline mode).
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.013|
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.014|  Gain            : No measurable gain on Windows 11, and this is the
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.015|                    honest answer rather than the flattering one. These
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.016|                    three names were superseded by the Background and
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.017|                    Foreground pairs in Windows 10 version 2004, so a
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.018|                    modern machine most likely ignores them - which means
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.019|                    deleting them is not what un-throttles a slow update.
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.020|                    It is leftover cleanup: it removes stale policy
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.021|                    entries and it removes the confusion of seeing a cap
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.022|                    in the registry that no longer does anything. If your
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.023|                    updates genuinely crawl, the values to look at are
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.024|                    DOPercentageMaxBackgroundBandwidth and
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.025|                    DOPercentageMaxForegroundBandwidth, which OPTY does
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.026|                    not touch.
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.027|
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.028|  Cost            : None on any machine. If you had deliberately capped
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.029|                    Delivery Optimization, note that these three probably
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.030|                    were not what was enforcing it anyway; set a cap you
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.031|                    can trust in Settings > Windows Update > Advanced
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.032|                    options > Delivery Optimization > Advanced options.
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.033|
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.034|  Windows default : All three absent, no cap.
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.035|
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.036|  Possible values:
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.037|    DELETE               : Remove all three values. Their shipped state is
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.038|                           absence, so this is a true restore rather than
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.039|                           a rewrite. On a machine that never had them,
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.040|                           killkey checks first and does nothing at all.
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.041|    DOMaxDownloadBandwidth=<KB/s> :
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.042|        An absolute ceiling in kilobytes per second on what Delivery
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.043|        Optimization may download; 0 meant unlimited. This is the pre-2004
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.044|        spelling of the setting - current Windows reads
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.045|        DOMaxBackgroundDownloadBandwidth and
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.046|        DOMaxForegroundDownloadBandwidth instead, so on Windows 11 this
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.047|        name is most likely inert.
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.048|    DOPercentageMaxDownloadBandwidth=1..100 :
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.049|        A percentage of the measured link speed. This is the value a
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.050|        tweaking tool sets to 5 or 10, after which updates crawl for
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.051|        hours. Same story as above: the current names are
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.052|        DOPercentageMaxBackgroundBandwidth and
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.053|        DOPercentageMaxForegroundBandwidth, and those are the ones that
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.054|        bite today.
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.055|    DOMaxUploadBandwidth=<KB/s> :
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.056|        Caps what this PC uploads to other machines sharing update
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.057|        content. It never affected your downloads, only your contribution
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.058|        to the peer cache. Also superseded, and today the practical
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.059|        control is DOMonthlyUploadDataCap plus the Delivery Optimization
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.060|        page in Settings.
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.061|
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.062|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETEs, and there is genuinely
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.063|                       nothing to split on. Deleting a value whose default
::X|EN|rad.do.bandwidth.killkeys.064|                       is absence is the same action as restoring Windows,
