:: ---- powercfg.ultimate.create (preference) --------------------------
::P|powercfg.ultimate.create|ACTIVATE|CREATE|SKIP|SKIP|DELETE|
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.001|UNLOCK THE HIDDEN ULTIMATE PERFORMANCE PLAN
::T|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.002|Unlocks Microsoft's hidden power plan without switching to it, so you can try it yourself - activating it removes core-parking delay, but there is no published proof it beats Balanced in FPS, and it does cost battery life and extra heat.
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.001|LE MODE D ALIMENTATION MASQUE PERFORMANCES ULTIMES
::T|FR|powercfg.ultimate.create.002|Debloque le mode d alimentation masque de Microsoft sans l activer, pour que vous puissiez l essayer vous-meme - l activer supprime le delai de deparcage des coeurs, mais rien ne prouve qu il batte Utilisation normale en FPS, et il coute bien de l autonomie et de la chaleur.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.001|  What it is      : A power scheme Microsoft ships but hides on most
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.002|                    consumer SKUs. It pins the minimum processor state at
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.003|                    100 percent, disables core parking, and never idles
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.004|                    the disk. It does not disable CPU idle states. OPTY
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.005|                    duplicates the template to a fixed GUID, does NOT
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.006|                    switch to it, then opens powercfg.cpl so you choose
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.007|                    for yourself.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.008|
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.009|  Actual effect   : powercfg -duplicatescheme creates the plan and it
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.010|                    shows up in the power options list. On builds where
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.011|                    the template is hidden or absent the command simply
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.012|                    fails and OPTY reports it as unavailable rather than
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.013|                    pretending. The fixed destination GUID matters: an
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.014|                    earlier version searched for the template GUID, which
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.015|                    never appears in powercfg /list because each copy gets
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.016|                    a fresh random GUID, so the guard never fired and this
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.017|                    machine ended up with three identical Ultimate
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.018|                    Performance plans.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.019|
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.020|  Gain            : If you then select it, background-idle behaviour
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.021|                    stops: no core is ever parked, so nothing has to be
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.022|                    unparked before it can run, and the CPU never drops
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.023|                    below its base clock. That is the substantiated
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.024|                    mechanism, and it is a latency mechanism. What is not
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.025|                    substantiated is the usual claim: no published
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.026|                    measurement shows Ultimate Performance beating
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.027|                    Balanced with the Settings power-mode slider at Best
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.028|                    Performance in frame rate or 1% lows on a modern
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.029|                    desktop. The cases with a real argument are real-time
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.030|                    audio and workloads sensitive to core-parking wake
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.031|                    latency. Merely creating the plan gains nothing at
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.032|                    all.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.033|
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.034|  Cost            : Creating it costs nothing but a line in a list.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.035|                    Selecting it costs continuous idle power, heat and fan
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.036|                    noise, and noticeably shorter battery life on a laptop
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.037|                    - and on a thermally limited laptop that heat can make
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.038|                    the machine slower, not faster. The disk never idling
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.039|                    is its own small cost on a box with spinning drives.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.040|
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.041|  Windows default : The scheme is not present. It is hidden on most
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.042|                    consumer SKUs, and on some battery-powered systems the
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.043|                    template is not exposed at all, in which case
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.044|                    duplicatescheme fails.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.045|
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.046|  Possible values:
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.047|    CREATE               : powercfg -duplicatescheme copies the hidden
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.048|                           Ultimate Performance template to the fixed GUID
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.049|                           9f9d6f1a-0b7e-4c3a-9c8e-0a1b2c3d4e5f. The plan
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.050|                           appears in Power Options and nothing else
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.051|                           changes - your active plan, your clocks and
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.052|                           your fan curve are untouched until you select
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.053|                           it yourself. The gain from creating it, on its
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.054|                           own, is zero: it adds one line to a list.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.055|    ACTIVATE             : Create it, then powercfg /setactive
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.056|                           9f9d6f1a-0b7e-4c3a-9c8e-0a1b2c3d4e5f. Now it
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.057|                           does something: minimum processor state is
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.058|                           pinned at 100 percent, core parking is off so
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.059|                           no core is ever unparked-on-demand, and the
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.060|                           disk never idles down. Note what it does NOT
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.061|                           do: it does not disable CPU idle states - that
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.062|                           is the separate PROCIDLEDISABLE override, and
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.063|                           the card rad-pwr-idledisable explains why you
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.064|                           do not want it.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.065|    SKIP                 : Do not create it. The Power Options list stays
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.066|                           as Windows shipped it. On a machine that will
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.067|                           never select the plan, this is the answer that
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.068|                           leaves the least behind.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.069|    DELETE               : powercfg -delete
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.070|                           9f9d6f1a-0b7e-4c3a-9c8e-0a1b2c3d4e5f removes a
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.071|                           copy a previous run created. It fails if that
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.072|                           plan is currently active, so switch to Balanced
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.073|                           first: powercfg /setactive
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.074|                           381b4222-f694-41f0-9685-ff5bb260df2e, then
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.075|                           delete.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.076|
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.077|  Why these profiles : Gaming is the only profile that activates it, and
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.078|                       for the honest reason rather than the popular one:
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.079|                       the profile is defined by latency, the plan's one
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.080|                       substantiated effect is removing core-parking wake
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.081|                       latency, and a mains desktop has already accepted
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.082|                       the idle-power cost. Do not expect frames from it.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.083|                       Server creates it but stays off it: under sustained
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.084|                       load Windows unparks every core anyway, so a 24/7
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.085|                       throughput box gets nothing from the plan while
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.086|                       paying idle power around the clock - having the
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.087|                       option on hand costs nothing, though. Office and
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.088|                       Laptop skip it entirely: a quiet family PC gains
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.089|                       nothing, and a laptop should be on Balanced whether
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.090|                       or not it is plugged in. Windows answers DELETE,
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.091|                       because a shipped machine does not have this scheme
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.092|                       at all - and remember to switch to Balanced before
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.093|                       deleting, or powercfg refuses.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.094|
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.095|  Known problems  : Before the fixed-GUID guard at line 2225, repeated
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.096|                    runs created a new Ultimate Performance plan every
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.097|                    time - this machine accumulated three identical
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.098|                    copies. That is fixed, but a machine that ran the
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.099|                    older build still has the duplicates, and they must be
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.100|                    removed by hand with powercfg -delete on each stray
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.101|                    GUID from powercfg /list.
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.102|
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.103|  Unverified      : I can describe what the scheme sets, but I have no
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.104|                    published measurement showing Ultimate Performance
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.105|                    beating Balanced-at-Best-Performance in frame rate or
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.106|                    1% lows on modern hardware. The core-parking wake-
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.107|                    latency argument is a mechanism, not a benchmark: I
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.108|                    cannot tell you how many microseconds it is worth on a
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.109|                    given CPU, and on a desktop that is already at the
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.110|                    Best Performance slider setting the practical
::X|EN|powercfg.ultimate.create.111|                    difference may well be zero.
