:: ---- dism.restorehealth (repair) --------------------------------
::P|dism.restorehealth|SKIP|RUN|RUN|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.001|DISM COMPONENT STORE REPAIR
::T|EN|dism.restorehealth.002|Repairs the Windows component store so both DISM and SFC have a healthy source to restore damaged system files from - on a healthy PC it changes nothing, but with /ResetBase it also permanently blocks uninstalling currently installed updates.
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.001|REPARATION DU MAGASIN DE COMPOSANTS (DISM)
::T|FR|dism.restorehealth.002|Repare le magasin de composants pour que DISM et SFC aient une source saine pour restaurer les fichiers systeme abimes - sur un PC sain ca ne change rien, mais avec /ResetBase ca bloque aussi definitivement la desinstallation des mises a jour actuellement installees.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.001|  What it is : The component store (C:\Windows\WinSxS) holds the reference
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.002|               copy of every system file Windows can restore from. OPTY runs
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.003|               three read-only passes first, then the repair, then a cleanup
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.004|               WITH /ResetBase.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.005|
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.006|  Actual effect : /RestoreHealth compares the store against its manifests and
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.007|                  replaces anything whose hash does not match, pulling
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.008|                  payloads from Windows Update when the local copy is
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.009|                  unusable. /StartComponentCleanup then discards superseded
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.010|                  component versions; with /ResetBase it discards EVERY
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.011|                  superseded version rather than only those past their grace
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.012|                  period, which is where the real space is.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.013|
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.014|  Gain : No performance gain whatsoever - this is not a tuning step, and on a
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.015|         healthy machine it prints "no component store corruption detected"
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.016|         and changes nothing. The mechanism that makes it worth running is
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.017|         narrow but real: SFC repairs damaged system files by copying them out
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.018|         of the component store, so a corrupt store leaves SFC with no clean
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.019|         source and it reports it cannot fix anything. DISM is what gives SFC
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.020|         something to repair from.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.021|
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.022|  Cost : Time. Five to thirty minutes, occasionally longer, with a progress
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.023|         bar that sits at 20 percent for a long stretch. All cores stay busy
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.024|         and Windows Update bandwidth is used. No user file, profile or
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.025|         setting is touched. The real cost is elsewhere: with /ResetBase,
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.026|         updates already installed can no longer be uninstalled. A bad update
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.027|         then has to be fixed forward rather than rolled back - the same trade
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.028|         already accepted for Windows.old and the upgrade staging folders.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.029|
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.030|  Windows default : Not a setting. Windows never runs /RestoreHealth on its
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.031|                    own - it only runs the StartComponentCleanup scheduled
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.032|                    task, on idle, after a 30-day grace period.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.033|
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.034|  Possible values:
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.035|  RUN : Executes the five DISM passes in order. The first three read only:
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.036|        /AnalyzeComponentStore reports the WinSxS size and whether a cleanup
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.037|        is recommended, /ScanHealth walks the whole store and hashes every
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.038|        component against its manifest, /CheckHealth reads the corruption flag
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.039|        those scans set. /RestoreHealth is the only step that writes: it
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.040|        replaces components whose hash does not match, downloading replacement
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.041|        payloads from Windows Update when the local copy is itself damaged.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.042|        /StartComponentCleanup then discards superseded component versions.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.043|  SKIP : Nothing runs. No state is left behind, no flag is set - DISM is an
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.044|         operation, not a setting, so skipping it today costs nothing
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.045|         tomorrow. A machine with a corrupt component store stays corrupt, and
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.046|         the symptom you will eventually see is a Windows Update that fails
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.047|         with the same error every month, or SFC reporting it found damage it
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.048|         cannot fix.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.049|
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.050|  Why these profiles : Nothing here depends on hardware, so the split is
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.051|                       purely about who can afford the half hour and who would
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.052|                       not notice a silently broken update path. Server and
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.053|                       office run it: those machines are expected to keep
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.054|                       updating unattended, and a corrupt store surfaces
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.055|                       months later as an update that fails every single time.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.056|                       Gaming and laptop skip it - it buys no frames, and on a
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.057|                       laptop it is thirty minutes of all-core work plus radio
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.058|                       traffic on a machine whose entire profile is battery
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.059|                       life. Skip here means "run it by hand, plugged in, the
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.060|                       day you have a symptom", not "never". Answer 5 also
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.061|                       skips, because running DISM is not something a stock
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.062|                       Windows does.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.063|
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.064|  Known problems : The operation itself breaks nothing. Its own failure mode
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.065|                   is worth recognising: offline, or on a machine where
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.066|                   Windows Update is blocked by policy or redirected to a WSUS
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.067|                   server, /RestoreHealth ends with 0x800f0906 or 0x800f081f
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.068|                   and "the source files could not be found". That is a
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.069|                   missing source, not proof your Windows is damaged -
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.070|                   pointing it at a mounted ISO with /Source:wim: finishes the
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.071|                   job.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.072|
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.073|  Unverified : The exact grace-period behaviour of a manual
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.074|               /StartComponentCleanup with /ResetBase (whether it honours the
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.075|               same 30-day window as the scheduled task) has not been verified
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.076|               on 25H2. It matters only for how far back you can uninstall
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.077|               updates afterwards.
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.078|
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.079|  Target : OPTY.bat label :dism - dism /Online /Cleanup-Image
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.080|           /AnalyzeComponentStore, then /Cleanup-image /ScanHealth,
::X|EN|dism.restorehealth.081|           /CheckHealth, /RestoreHealth, /StartComponentCleanup /ResetBase
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.001|  Ce que c est : Le magasin de composants (C:\Windows\WinSxS) contient la
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.002|                 copie de reference de chaque fichier systeme a partir de
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.003|                 laquelle Windows sait se reparer. OPTY lance d abord trois
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.004|                 passes en lecture seule, puis la reparation, puis un
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.005|                 nettoyage AVEC /ResetBase.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.006|
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.007|  Effet reel : /RestoreHealth confronte le magasin a ses manifestes et
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.008|               remplace tout ce dont l empreinte ne correspond pas, en
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.009|               telechargeant les charges utiles depuis Windows Update quand la
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.010|               copie locale est inutilisable. /StartComponentCleanup elimine
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.011|               ensuite les versions remplacees ; avec /ResetBase il elimine
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.012|               TOUTES les versions remplacees et non seulement celles hors
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.013|               delai de grace, ce qui est la ou se trouve le vrai gain de
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.014|               place.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.015|
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.016|  Gain : Aucun gain de performance, point. Ce n est pas une etape d
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.017|         optimisation : sur une machine saine, elle affiche "aucune corruption
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.018|         detectee" et ne change rien. Le mecanisme qui justifie de la lancer
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.019|         est etroit mais bien reel : SFC repare les fichiers systeme en les
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.020|         recopiant depuis le magasin de composants ; si le magasin est
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.021|         lui-meme corrompu, SFC n a plus de source saine et annonce qu il n
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.022|         arrive a rien. DISM est ce qui redonne a SFC de quoi travailler.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.023|
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.024|  Cout : Du temps. Cinq a trente minutes, parfois davantage, avec une barre de
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.025|         progression qui stagne longuement a 20 %. Tous les coeurs travaillent
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.026|         et de la bande passante Windows Update est consommee. Aucun fichier,
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.027|         profil ou reglage utilisateur n est touche. Le vrai cout est ailleurs
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.028|         : avec /ResetBase, les mises a jour deja installees ne sont plus
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.029|         desinstallables. Une mise a jour ratee se corrige alors en avant,
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.030|         plus en arriere - le meme compromis que pour Windows.old et les
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.031|         dossiers de mise a niveau, accepte volontairement.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.032|
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.033|  Defaut Windows : Sans objet : ce n est pas un reglage. Windows ne lance
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.034|                   jamais /RestoreHealth de lui-meme ; il n execute que la
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.035|                   tache planifiee StartComponentCleanup, a l inactivite,
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.036|                   apres 30 jours de delai de grace.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.037|
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.038|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.039|  RUN : Enchaine les cinq passes DISM. Les trois premieres ne font que lire :
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.040|        /AnalyzeComponentStore annonce la taille de WinSxS et si un nettoyage
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.041|        est conseille, /ScanHealth parcourt tout le magasin et compare l
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.042|        empreinte de chaque composant a son manifeste, /CheckHealth relit l
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.043|        indicateur de corruption pose par ces analyses. /RestoreHealth est la
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.044|        seule etape qui ecrit : elle remplace les composants dont l empreinte
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.045|        ne colle pas, en telechargeant les charges utiles depuis Windows
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.046|        Update quand la copie locale est elle-meme abimee.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.047|        /StartComponentCleanup elimine ensuite les versions de composants
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.048|        remplacees.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.049|  SKIP : Rien ne s execute. Aucun etat n est laisse derriere, aucun indicateur
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.050|         pose : DISM est une operation, pas un reglage, donc sauter l etape
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.051|         aujourd hui ne coute rien demain. Une machine dont le magasin est
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.052|         corrompu le reste, et le symptome finit par apparaitre : une mise a
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.053|         jour Windows qui echoue avec la meme erreur tous les mois, ou SFC qui
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.054|         signale des degats qu il n arrive pas a reparer.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.055|
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.056|  Pourquoi ces profils : Rien ici ne depend du materiel : le partage porte
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.057|                         uniquement sur qui peut s offrir la demi-heure et qui
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.058|                         ne verrait pas passer une chaine de mise a jour
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.059|                         silencieusement cassee. Serveur et bureautique le
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.060|                         lancent : ces machines sont censees continuer a se
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.061|                         mettre a jour sans surveillance, et un magasin
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.062|                         corrompu se manifeste des mois plus tard par une mise
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.063|                         a jour qui echoue systematiquement. Jeu et portable
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.064|                         sautent l etape : elle ne rapporte aucune image par
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.065|                         seconde, et sur un portable c est trente minutes de
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.066|                         calcul sur tous les coeurs plus du reseau, sur une
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.067|                         machine dont tout le profil tient a l autonomie. Ici
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.068|                         sauter veut dire a lancer a la main, sur secteur, le
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.069|                         jour ou vous avez un symptome, pas jamais. La reponse
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.070|                         5 saute aussi, parce qu un Windows d origine ne lance
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.071|                         pas DISM.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.072|
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.073|  Problemes connus : L operation elle-meme ne casse rien. En revanche son
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.074|                     propre mode d echec merite d etre reconnu : hors ligne,
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.075|                     ou sur une machine ou Windows Update est bloque par
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.076|                     strategie ou redirige vers un WSUS, /RestoreHealth se
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.077|                     termine sur 0x800f0906 ou 0x800f081f et les fichiers
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.078|                     sources sont introuvables. C est une source manquante,
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.079|                     pas la preuve que votre Windows est abime : en le
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.080|                     pointant vers une ISO montee avec /Source:wim:, il va au
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.081|                     bout.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.082|
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.083|  Non verifie : Le comportement exact du delai de grace pour un
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.084|                /StartComponentCleanup manuel avec /ResetBase (respecte-t-il
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.085|                la meme fenetre de 30 jours que la tache planifiee) n a pas
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.086|                ete verifie sur 25H2. Ca ne compte que pour savoir jusqu ou
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.087|                vous pouvez desinstaller des mises a jour ensuite.
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.088|
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.089|  Cible : OPTY.bat label :dism - dism /Online /Cleanup-Image
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.090|          /AnalyzeComponentStore, puis /Cleanup-image /ScanHealth,
::X|FR|dism.restorehealth.091|          /CheckHealth, /RestoreHealth, /StartComponentCleanup /ResetBase
::
:: ---- rad.overlay.killkey (cleanup) -------------------------------
::P|rad.overlay.killkey|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.001|CLEAR LEFTOVER OVERLAY-BLOCKING TWEAKS
::T|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.002|Deletes four leftover registry values from old tuning scripts so the GPU can use its normal overlay and flip path again - less GPU power draw and a touch less latency in windowed or borderless games, no change in exclusive fullscreen.
::T|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.001|EFFACER LES RESIDUS QUI BLOQUENT L OVERLAY GPU
::T|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.002|Supprime quatre valeurs laissees par d anciens scripts de reglage pour que le GPU retrouve son chemin normal d overlay et de flip : un peu moins de consommation GPU et un peu moins de latence en fenetre ou en borderless, aucun changement en plein ecran exclusif.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.001|  What it is      : Four values left behind by older tuning scripts, all
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.002|                    of which suppress part of the modern presentation
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.003|                    path: OverlayTestMode, OverlayMinFPS and
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.004|                    DisableIndependentFlip under Dwm, and DisableOverlays
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.005|                    under GraphicsDrivers.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.006|
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.007|  Actual effect   : Deletes whichever of the four exist, and says so per
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.008|                    value. Writing 0 would not restore the default - only
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.009|                    absence does.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.010|
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.011|  Gain            : The GPU can use hardware planes and independent flip
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.012|                    again: DWM skips a full-screen composite pass per
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.013|                    frame while a window is on top, and a covering
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.014|                    borderless window can flip straight to the display
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.015|                    instead of being copied. Lower GPU power and one less
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.016|                    stage of latency in windowed and borderless. Nothing
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.017|                    changes in exclusive fullscreen.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.018|
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.019|  Cost            : If one of these was suppressing a real multi-monitor
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.020|                    flicker, it can come back. Reboot to take effect.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.021|
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.022|  Windows default : All four absent.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.023|
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.024|  Possible values:
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.025|    DELETE               : All four removed. :killkey queries first and
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.026|                           only deletes what is actually present, so it
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.027|                           reports 'leftover found' rather than pretending
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.028|                           it repaired something on a clean machine. This
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.029|                           is the shipped state for every one of the four.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.030|    DisableIndependentFlip=1 :
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.031|        The one value on this list that is not part of the MPO recipe.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.032|        Independent flip is what lets a fullscreen-borderless window whose
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.033|        swapchain covers the screen have its buffers flipped straight to
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.034|        the display, bypassing DWM's copy - it is why borderless can
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.035|        behave like exclusive fullscreen for latency and VRR. Forcing 1
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.036|        puts the copy back in.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.037|    OverlayTestMode=5    : The published MPO kill switch. DWM stops
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.038|                           offering hardware planes to the driver and
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.039|                           every windowed frame goes through a full
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.040|                           desktop composite.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.041|    OverlayMinFPS=0 / DisableOverlays=1 :
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.042|        The two script-folklore companions. Neither is in Microsoft's
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.043|        published procedure and I could not find documentation of their
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.044|        effect. They are deleted because absent is the shipped state, not
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.045|        because their effect is understood.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.046|    0 on any of the four : Never a restore. For all four, the Windows
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.047|                           default is the value not existing, so writing 0
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.048|                           leaves a written override behind that happens
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.049|                           to read as zero. Only deletion returns the
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.050|                           machine to stock - which is the entire reason
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.051|                           :killkey exists as a separate helper from
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.052|                           :regset.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.053|
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.054|  Why these profiles : All five delete. There is no honest way to split
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.055|                       this by intent: Windows never writes any of the
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.056|                       four, so on every profile the correct state is the
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.057|                       same one, and a difference invented here would be a
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.058|                       difference invented from nothing. The server
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.059|                       profile deletes too - a headless box gains nothing
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.060|                       from it, but it loses nothing either, and residue
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.061|                       that only matters once someone attaches a display
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.062|                       is exactly the residue worth removing while you are
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.063|                       already in the registry. The one machine that
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.064|                       should keep OverlayTestMode is one with a live,
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.065|                       reproducible flicker, and that decision belongs on
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.066|                       the flicker-workaround card.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.067|
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.068|  Known problems  : None from removing them. Concrete symptom of leaving
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.069|                    DisableIndependentFlip=1 in place: borderless windowed
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.070|                    games stay stuck at the desktop refresh rate with VRR
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.071|                    inactive, and the usual advice - switch to exclusive
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.072|                    fullscreen - masks it instead of fixing it.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.073|
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.074|  Unverified      : OPTY deliberately does NOT remove
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.075|                    UnsupportedMonitorModesAllowed from the same key, and
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.076|                    the in-file comment explains why: it is dxgkrnl's
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.077|                    custom-timing gate used by CRU and Adrenalin custom
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.078|                    modes, unrelated to MPO, and nothing in the script
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.079|                    would write it back. That reasoning looks right to me,
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.080|                    but it means this card is not 'clear the whole key'
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.081|                    and should not be described as such.
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.082|
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.083|  Target          : call :killkey on HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\Dwm
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.084|                    for OverlayTestMode, OverlayMinFPS,
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.085|                    DisableIndependentFlip, and on
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.086|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\GraphicsDrivers
::X|EN|rad.overlay.killkey.087|                    for DisableOverlays - OPTY.bat lines 1800-1803
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.001|  Ce que c est    : Quatre valeurs laissees par d anciens scripts de
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.002|                    reglage, qui toutes suppriment une partie du chemin de
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.003|                    presentation moderne : OverlayTestMode, OverlayMinFPS
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.004|                    et DisableIndependentFlip sous Dwm, et DisableOverlays
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.005|                    sous GraphicsDrivers.
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.006|
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.007|  Effet reel      : Supprime celles des quatre qui existent, et le dit
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.008|                    valeur par valeur. Ecrire 0 ne retablirait pas le
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.009|                    defaut : seule l absence le fait.
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.010|
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.011|  Gain            : Le GPU peut de nouveau utiliser les plans materiels et
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.012|                    l independent flip : DWM saute une passe de
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.013|                    composition plein ecran par image tant qu une fenetre
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.014|                    est au premier plan, et une fenetre borderless qui
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.015|                    couvre l ecran peut basculer directement vers
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.016|                    l affichage au lieu d etre recopiee. Moins de
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.017|                    consommation GPU et un etage de latence en moins en
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.018|                    fenetre et en borderless. Rien ne change en plein
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.019|                    ecran exclusif.
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.020|
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.021|  Cout            : Si l une d elles masquait un vrai scintillement multi-
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.022|                    ecran, il peut revenir. Redemarrage necessaire pour
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.023|                    que ce soit effectif.
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.024|
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.025|  Defaut Windows  : Les quatre absentes.
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.026|
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.027|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.028|    DELETE               : Les quatre supprimees. :killkey interroge
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.029|                           d abord et ne supprime que ce qui existe
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.030|                           reellement : il annonce « residu trouve »
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.031|                           plutot que de faire croire a une reparation sur
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.032|                           une machine propre. C est l etat d origine pour
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.033|                           chacune des quatre.
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.034|    DisableIndependentFlip=1 :
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.035|        La seule valeur de cette liste qui ne fait pas partie de la
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.036|        recette MPO. L independent flip, c est ce qui permet a une fenetre
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.037|        borderless plein ecran, dont le swapchain couvre tout l ecran, de
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.038|        voir ses tampons bascules directement vers l affichage, sans la
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.039|        copie de DWM : c est pour cela que le borderless peut se comporter
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.040|        comme du plein ecran exclusif en latence et en VRR. Forcer 1 remet
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.041|        la copie dans le chemin.
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.042|    OverlayTestMode=5    : L interrupteur d arret publie du MPO. DWM cesse
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.043|                           de proposer des plans materiels au pilote et
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.044|                           chaque image en fenetre repasse par une
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.045|                           composition complete du bureau.
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.046|    OverlayMinFPS=0 / DisableOverlays=1 :
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.047|        Les deux compagnons issus du folklore des scripts. Ni l un ni
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.048|        l autre ne figure dans la procedure publiee par Microsoft, et je
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.049|        n ai trouve aucune documentation sur leur effet. On les supprime
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.050|        parce que l absence est l etat d origine, pas parce qu on
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.051|        comprendrait ce qu elles font.
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.052|    0 on any of the four : Jamais un retour a l origine. Pour les quatre,
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.053|                           le defaut Windows est l inexistence de la
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.054|                           valeur : ecrire 0 laisse derriere un forcage
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.055|                           ecrit qui vaut zero. Seule la suppression
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.056|                           ramene la machine a l etat d usine - c est
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.057|                           toute la raison d etre de :killkey comme
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.058|                           fonction distincte de :regset.
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.059|
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.060|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq suppriment. Il n y a pas de facon
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.061|                         honnete de decouper cela par intention : Windows
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.062|                         n ecrit jamais aucune des quatre, donc sur chaque
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.063|                         profil le bon etat est le meme, et une difference
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.064|                         inventee ici serait une difference inventee a
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.065|                         partir de rien. Le profil serveur supprime aussi
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.066|                         : une machine sans ecran n y gagne rien, mais
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.067|                         elle n y perd rien non plus, et un residu qui ne
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.068|                         se manifeste qu une fois un ecran branche est
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.069|                         justement celui qu on retire pendant qu on est
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.070|                         deja dans le registre. La seule machine qui
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.071|                         devrait garder OverlayTestMode est celle qui a un
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.072|                         scintillement reel et reproductible, et cette
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.073|                         decision releve de la carte de contournement.
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.074|
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.075|  Problemes connus : Aucun du fait de la suppression. Symptome concret si
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.076|                     l on laisse DisableIndependentFlip=1 en place : les
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.077|                     jeux en fenetre sans bordure restent bloques a la
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.078|                     frequence du bureau, VRR inactif, et le conseil
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.079|                     habituel - passer en plein ecran exclusif - masque le
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.080|                     probleme au lieu de le corriger.
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.081|
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.082|  Non verifie (en)  : OPTY deliberately does NOT remove
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.083|                      UnsupportedMonitorModesAllowed from the same key,
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.084|                      and the in-file comment explains why: it is
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.085|                      dxgkrnl s custom-timing gate used by CRU and
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.086|                      Adrenalin custom modes, unrelated to MPO, and
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.087|                      nothing in the script would write it back. That
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.088|                      reasoning looks right to me, but it means this card
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.089|                      is not  clear the whole key  and should not be
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.090|                      described as such.
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.091|
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.092|  Cible           : call :killkey on HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\Dwm
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.093|                    for OverlayTestMode, OverlayMinFPS,
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.094|                    DisableIndependentFlip, and on
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.095|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\GraphicsDrivers
::X|FR|rad.overlay.killkey.096|                    for DisableOverlays - OPTY.bat lines 1800-1803
