:: ---- rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys (repair) ------------------------------
::P|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.001|RESTORE GPU CRASH RECOVERY (REMOVE TDR OVERRIDES)
::T|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.002|Deletes six registry values that a popular stability fix uses to disable GPU crash recovery - without it, a GPU hang means a hard freeze and a forced reboot instead of a two-second black-screen blink that recovers on its own.
::T|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.001|RETABLIR LA RECUPERATION GPU (SUPPRIMER LES FORCAGES TDR)
::T|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.002|Supprime six valeurs de registre qu un pretendu correctif de stabilite tres repandu utilise pour desactiver la recuperation du GPU : sans elle, un blocage GPU se transforme en gel complet et redemarrage force, au lieu d un clignotement noir de deux secondes qui se resout tout seul.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.001|  What it is      : Six values under GraphicsDrivers that change how long
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.002|                    Windows waits before deciding the GPU has stopped
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.003|                    responding, and what it does about it. All six ship
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.004|                    absent, and the code deletes only the ones it actually
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.005|                    finds.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.006|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.007|  Actual effect   : Deletes whichever of the six exist, restoring the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.008|                    built-in two-second watchdog and the reset-and-recover
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.009|                    behaviour.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.010|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.011|  Gain            : No frames, and this card is worth reading backwards:
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.012|                    the tweak these values come from is sold as a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.013|                    stability fix and is the opposite of one. TdrLevel=0
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.014|                    does not stop your GPU from hanging - it deletes the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.015|                    recovery. The same hang that would have been a two-
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.016|                    second black blink with a notification becomes a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.017|                    machine you hold the power button on. Deleting the six
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.018|                    buys back the blink.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.019|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.020|  Cost            : Nothing on consumer hardware doing consumer work.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.021|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.022|  Windows default : All six absent - effective behaviour is TdrLevel 3,
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.023|                    TdrDelay 2 seconds, TdrDdiDelay 5 seconds,
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.024|                    TdrLimitCount 5 within TdrLimitTime 60 seconds.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.025|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.026|  Possible values:
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.027|    DELETE               : All six removed, restoring the built-in
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.028|                           behaviour: a GPU that stops responding for two
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.029|                           seconds is reset, the driver is restarted in
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.030|                           place, the screen blinks black and you get the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.031|                           'display driver stopped responding and has
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.032|                           recovered' notification. The application
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.033|                           usually survives; the machine always does.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.034|    TdrLevel = 0 / 3     : 3 is the effective default: detect the timeout
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.035|                           and recover. 0 turns detection off entirely -
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.036|                           and this is the value the whole card is about,
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.037|                           because it is sold as a fix and is the opposite
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.038|                           of one. It does not stop the GPU hanging; it
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.039|                           removes the recovery, so the same hang that
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.040|                           would have been a two-second blink becomes a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.041|                           frozen machine you power-cycle. (1 bugchecks on
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.042|                           timeout, 2 is a legacy VGA-recovery mode;
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.043|                           neither is something to write by hand.)
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.044|    TdrDelay = 2 (default) or raised :
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.045|        Seconds the GPU may spend on a single scheduled packet before the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.046|        watchdog fires. Default 2. Raising it to 8, 10 or 60 is the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.047|        standard, legitimate workaround for long compute kernels - and the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.048|        standard, illegitimate one for hiding an unstable overclock, where
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.049|        it converts a recoverable blink into a longer freeze that ends the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.050|        same way.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.051|    TdrDdiDelay = 5 (default) :
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.052|        Seconds a driver DDI call may take before the OS considers the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.053|        driver stuck. Default 5. Raising it delays the moment Windows
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.054|        notices a wedged driver; it does not make the driver less wedged.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.055|    TdrLimitCount = 5 / TdrLimitTime = 60 (defaults) :
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.056|        The circuit breaker: more than TdrLimitCount recoveries within
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.057|        TdrLimitTime seconds and Windows stops recovering and bugchecks
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.058|        instead. Raising these lets a machine that is failing every few
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.059|        seconds keep limping along with a black-flashing screen rather
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.060|        than telling you something is wrong.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.061|    TdrDebugMode         : A developer knob, absent on any normal machine.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.062|                           Its documented values cover breaking to a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.063|                           kernel debugger, ignoring timeouts outright and
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.064|                           recovering with or without a prompt. Ignoring
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.065|                           timeouts on a machine with no debugger attached
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.066|                           is the same trap as TdrLevel=0. It ships
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.067|                           absent; leave it absent.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.068|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.069|  Why these profiles : All five delete, because the argument here is not
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.070|                       about intent at all - it is about a safety net that
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.071|                       no profile is better off without. A gaming machine
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.072|                       wants the blink so a driver hiccup does not end the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.073|                       session at the wall socket; a server wants it
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.074|                       because nobody is in front of it to power-cycle
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.075|                       anything; office and laptop want it for the same
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.076|                       reason everyone does. The one workload with a real
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.077|                       claim to a raised TdrDelay - multi-minute GPU
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.078|                       compute - is not one of the five profiles, and if
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.079|                       that is you, you set the value deliberately and you
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.080|                       will want to set it again.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.081|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.082|  Known problems  : Real and concrete: a raised TdrDelay is the standard
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.083|                    fix for 'display driver stopped responding' during
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.084|                    long GPU kernels in Blender Cycles renders, DaVinci
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.085|                    Resolve exports and CUDA or OpenCL jobs. If you set it
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.086|                    for that reason, deleting it brings those failures
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.087|                    back. Nothing else on a normal desktop depends on any
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.088|                    of the six.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.089|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.090|  Unverified      : TdrDebugMode's per-value semantics I have stated only
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.091|                    in general terms on purpose: the documented
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.092|                    enumeration covers break-to-debugger, ignore-timeout
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.093|                    and recover-with-or-without-prompt, but I am not
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.094|                    confident enough in which numeric value is the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.095|                    effective default to print a number. It does not
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.096|                    matter for this card, since the action is deletion and
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.097|                    the value ships absent either way.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.098|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.099|  Target          : for %%V in (TdrLevel TdrDelay TdrDdiDelay
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.100|                    TdrLimitCount TdrLimitTime TdrDebugMode) do call
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.101|                    :killkey
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.102|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\GraphicsDrivers
::X|EN|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.103|                    %%V - OPTY.bat line 1924
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.001|  Ce que c est    : Six valeurs sous GraphicsDrivers qui modifient le
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.002|                    temps que Windows attend avant de conclure que le GPU
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.003|                    ne repond plus, et ce qu il en fait ensuite. Les six
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.004|                    sont livrees absentes, et le code ne supprime que
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.005|                    celles qu il trouve reellement.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.006|
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.007|  Effet reel      : Supprime celles des six qui existent, retablissant le
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.008|                    chien de garde de deux secondes et le comportement
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.009|                    reinitialiser-puis-recuperer d origine.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.010|
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.011|  Gain            : Aucune image par seconde, et cette carte se lit a
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.012|                    l envers : le reglage d ou viennent ces valeurs est
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.013|                    vendu comme un correctif de stabilite alors qu il en
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.014|                    est le contraire. TdrLevel=0 n empeche pas votre GPU
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.015|                    de se bloquer, il supprime la recuperation. Le meme
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.016|                    blocage, qui aurait ete un clignotement noir de deux
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.017|                    secondes suivi d une notification, devient une machine
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.018|                    sur laquelle vous maintenez le bouton d alimentation.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.019|                    Supprimer les six vous rend le clignotement.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.020|
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.021|  Cout            : Rien sur du materiel grand public en usage grand
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.022|                    public.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.023|
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.024|  Defaut Windows  : Les six absentes - comportement effectif : TdrLevel 3,
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.025|                    TdrDelay 2 secondes, TdrDdiDelay 5 secondes,
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.026|                    TdrLimitCount 5 sur une fenetre TdrLimitTime de 60
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.027|                    secondes.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.028|
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.029|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.030|    DELETE               : Les six supprimees : le comportement d origine
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.031|                           revient. Un GPU qui cesse de repondre pendant
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.032|                           deux secondes est reinitialise, le pilote est
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.033|                           relance a chaud, l ecran clignote en noir et
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.034|                           vous recevez la notification « le pilote
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.035|                           d affichage a cesse de fonctionner et a
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.036|                           recupere ». L application survit le plus
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.037|                           souvent ; la machine, toujours.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.038|    TdrLevel = 0 / 3     : 3 est le defaut effectif : detecter le
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.039|                           depassement et recuperer. 0 coupe entierement
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.040|                           la detection - et c est la valeur dont parle
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.041|                           toute cette carte, parce qu elle est vendue
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.042|                           comme un correctif alors qu elle en est l exact
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.043|                           contraire. Elle n empeche pas le GPU de se
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.044|                           bloquer : elle supprime la recuperation, si
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.045|                           bien que le meme blocage, qui aurait ete un
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.046|                           clignotement de deux secondes, devient une
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.047|                           machine gelee qu on redemarre a la prise. (1
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.048|                           provoque un ecran bleu au depassement, 2 est un
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.049|                           mode de recuperation VGA hérité ; ni l un ni
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.050|                           l autre ne s ecrit a la main.)
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.051|    TdrDelay = 2 (default) or raised :
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.052|        Nombre de secondes que le GPU peut passer sur un seul paquet
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.053|        planifie avant que le chien de garde ne se declenche. Defaut : 2.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.054|        Le monter a 8, 10 ou 60 est le contournement standard et legitime
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.055|        pour les longs noyaux de calcul - et le contournement standard et
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.056|        illegitime pour masquer un overclock instable, ou il transforme un
::X|FR|rad.gpu.tdr.killkeys.057|        clignotement recuperable en un gel plus long qui finit pareil.
