:: ---- rad.gpu.preemption.killkey (cleanup) ------------------------
::P|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.001|RESTORE GPU PREEMPTION TO ITS DEFAULT STATE
::T|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.002|Deletes a registry value some GPU tuning scripts add while claiming it cuts scheduling overhead - there is no published measurement showing any gain either way, so this just returns the machine to what a fresh Windows install looks like.
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.001|RETABLIR LA PREEMPTION GPU A SON ETAT D ORIGINE
::T|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.002|Supprime une valeur de registre que certains scripts de reglage GPU ajoutent en pretendant reduire le cout d ordonnancement : aucune mesure publiee ne montre le moindre gain dans un sens ou l autre, donc ceci ne fait que remettre la machine dans l etat d une installation Windows neuve.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.001|  What it is      : EnablePreemption under GraphicsDrivers\Scheduler, a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.002|                    value that appears on machines that have been through
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.003|                    a GPU tuning script. Windows does not create this key.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.004|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.005|  Actual effect   : Deletes the value if it is there, leaving the GPU
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.006|                    scheduler on the driver's own preemption behaviour.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.007|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.008|  Gain            : Returning the machine to the state a fresh install is
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.009|                    in - nothing more, and it is worth being blunt about
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.010|                    that. This is not a Microsoft-documented value, and
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.011|                    there is no published measurement showing that setting
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.012|                    it to 0 gains frames or that deleting it costs any.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.013|                    The reason to delete is that absence is what Windows
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.014|                    ships, not that removal buys you something. If it did,
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.015|                    this card would say a number.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.016|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.017|  Cost            : None. Nothing on a normal machine reads this key
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.018|                    deliberately.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.019|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.020|  Windows default : Absent.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.021|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.022|  Possible values:
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.023|    DELETE               : The value is removed and the GPU scheduler is
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.024|                           left on whatever preemption granularity the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.025|                           driver reports it can do - which is the only
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.026|                           state a fresh Windows install has ever been in,
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.027|                           because Windows does not create this key.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.028|    0                    : What tuning scripts write, with the claim that
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.029|                           it removes scheduling overhead. Preemption is
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.030|                           the mechanism that lets the GPU interrupt a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.031|                           long draw or compute job mid-flight so
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.032|                           something else - the desktop, a higher-priority
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.033|                           queue, the watchdog's reset - can get in. If
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.034|                           the value does what those scripts claim, the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.035|                           trade is a stuttering desktop under GPU load
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.036|                           and a TDR reset with less to work with. I have
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.037|                           found no published measurement showing it gains
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.038|                           anything, so treat the claim as unsupported in
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.039|                           both directions and just delete it.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.040|    1                    : Setting 1 is not the restore either, even
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.041|                           though it reads like 'preemption on'. Windows
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.042|                           ships this key absent, so 1 is a written
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.043|                           override that happens to agree with the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.044|                           driver's own default - and it would keep
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.045|                           overriding it if a future driver wanted
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.046|                           something else. Deletion is the restore.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.047|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.048|  Why these profiles : All five delete, and there is genuinely no per-
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.049|                       profile argument to make: the value is script
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.050|                       residue on every kind of machine equally, and the
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.051|                       correct state is the one Windows ships. Inventing a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.052|                       reason for the server or the laptop to keep it
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.053|                       would be inventing a difference out of nothing.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.054|                       Answer 5 does the same thing as answers 1 to 4
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.055|                       here, which is exactly what you would expect from a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.056|                       card whose whole content is 'put it back how it
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.057|                       came'.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.058|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.059|  Unverified      : The existing card asserted that without preemption a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.060|                    long shader cannot be interrupted, the desktop
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.061|                    stutters and TDR recovery has nothing to work with.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.062|                    That is the mechanism preemption provides in general,
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.063|                    but I have not verified that EnablePreemption=0 on a
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.064|                    current WDDM 3.x driver actually disables it - the key
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.065|                    is undocumented and drivers may ignore it. I have
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.066|                    therefore stated it conditionally rather than as fact.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.067|                    The deletion itself is correct regardless, since
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.068|                    absence is the shipped state.
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.069|
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.070|  Target          : call :killkey HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Gr
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.071|                    aphicsDrivers\Scheduler EnablePreemption - OPTY.bat
::X|EN|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.072|                    line 1927
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.001|  Ce que c est    : EnablePreemption, sous GraphicsDrivers\Scheduler : une
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.002|                    valeur qui apparait sur les machines passees entre les
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.003|                    mains d un script de reglage GPU. Windows ne cree pas
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.004|                    cette cle.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.005|
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.006|  Effet reel      : Supprime la valeur si elle est presente, laissant
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.007|                    l ordonnanceur GPU sur le comportement de preemption
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.008|                    propre au pilote.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.009|
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.010|  Gain            : Remettre la machine dans l etat d une installation
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.011|                    neuve, rien de plus, et autant le dire franchement. Ce
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.012|                    n est pas une valeur documentee par Microsoft, et
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.013|                    aucune mesure publiee ne montre que la mettre a 0
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.014|                    rapporte des images par seconde, ni que la supprimer
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.015|                    en coute. La raison de supprimer, c est que l absence
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.016|                    est ce que livre Windows, pas que le retrait vous
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.017|                    rapporte quelque chose. Si c etait le cas, cette carte
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.018|                    donnerait un chiffre.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.019|
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.020|  Cout            : Aucun. Rien, sur une machine normale, ne lit cette cle
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.021|                    volontairement.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.022|
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.023|  Defaut Windows  : Absente.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.024|
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.025|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.026|    DELETE               : La valeur est supprimee et l ordonnanceur GPU
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.027|                           reste sur la granularite de preemption que le
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.028|                           pilote declare savoir faire - le seul etat
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.029|                           qu une installation neuve de Windows ait jamais
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.030|                           connu, puisque Windows ne cree pas cette cle.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.031|    0                    : Ce qu ecrivent les scripts de reglage, avec
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.032|                           l argument que cela supprime du cout
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.033|                           d ordonnancement. La preemption est le
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.034|                           mecanisme qui permet au GPU d interrompre un
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.035|                           long travail de dessin ou de calcul en cours de
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.036|                           route pour laisser passer autre chose : le
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.037|                           bureau, une file plus prioritaire, la
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.038|                           reinitialisation du chien de garde. Si la
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.039|                           valeur fait bien ce que ces scripts pretendent,
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.040|                           la contrepartie est un bureau qui saccade sous
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.041|                           charge GPU et un TDR qui a moins de prise. Je
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.042|                           n ai trouve aucune mesure publiee montrant un
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.043|                           gain quelconque : considerez l affirmation
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.044|                           comme non etayee dans les deux sens, et
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.045|                           contentez-vous de supprimer la valeur.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.046|    1                    : Ecrire 1 n est pas non plus le retour a
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.047|                           l origine, meme si cela se lit comme «
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.048|                           preemption activee ». Windows livre cette cle
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.049|                           absente : 1 est donc un forcage ecrit qui se
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.050|                           trouve coincider avec le defaut du pilote - et
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.051|                           qui continuerait de le forcer si un pilote
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.052|                           futur voulait autre chose. C est la suppression
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.053|                           qui restaure.
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.054|
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.055|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq suppriment, et il n y a sincerement
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.056|                         aucun argument par profil a faire valoir : la
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.057|                         valeur est un residu de script, de la meme facon
::X|FR|rad.gpu.preemption.killkey.058|                         sur tous les types de machines, et le bon etat
