:: ---- mouse.accel.off (preference) -----------------------------------
::P|mouse.accel.off|0|SKIP|1|1|1|
::T|EN|mouse.accel.off.001|TURN OFF MOUSE POINTER ACCELERATION
::T|EN|mouse.accel.off.002|Makes the desktop cursor move a consistent, repeatable distance per swipe instead of speed-scaled - helps precision in cursor-driven games and creative apps, does nothing for most modern shooters since they already bypass this via raw input.
::T|FR|mouse.accel.off.001|DESACTIVER L ACCELERATION DU POINTEUR DE SOURIS
::T|FR|mouse.accel.off.002|Rend le mouvement du curseur de bureau constant et reproductible au lieu d etre mis a l echelle selon la vitesse : utile en precision pour les jeux pilotes au curseur et les logiciels de creation, sans effet sur la plupart des jeux de tir modernes qui contournent deja ce reglage via le raw input.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.001|  What it is      : Four REG_SZ strings under HKCU\Control Panel\Mouse.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.002|                    MouseSpeed is the state of the 'Enhance pointer
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.003|                    precision' checkbox. MouseThreshold1 and
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.004|                    MouseThreshold2 are the legacy acceleration knees
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.005|                    Control Panel writes alongside it. MouseSensitivity is
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.006|                    the 1-20 pointer-speed slider, 10 being the middle
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.007|                    notch.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.008|
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.009|  Actual effect   : MouseSpeed=0 with both thresholds at 0 unticks Enhance
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.010|                    pointer precision: the OS stops running mouse counts
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.011|                    through the ballistics curve, so the same physical
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.012|                    movement always yields the same cursor distance.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.013|                    MouseSensitivity stays 10, the only notch that passes
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.014|                    counts through with no multiplier. All four are
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.015|                    written as REG_SZ, which is how a stock profile stores
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.016|                    them.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.017|
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.018|  Gain            : On the desktop cursor, and in anything that reads the
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.019|                    OS cursor position, movement becomes linear and
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.020|                    repeatable. In games that use raw input — essentially
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.021|                    every modern shooter — there is no measurable gain,
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.022|                    because raw input never passes through pointer
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.023|                    ballistics at all: the flick was already 1:1 before
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.024|                    you touched anything. The real beneficiaries are
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.025|                    cursor-driven games (RTS, MOBA, older titles, in-game
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.026|                    menus), drawing and photo editing.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.027|
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.028|  Cost            : Crossing a large or high-resolution screen takes more
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.029|                    desk travel, noticeably so below about 800 DPI.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.030|                    Trackpads suffer most — the surface is small and
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.031|                    acceleration is what makes it usable. If you have
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.032|                    lived with acceleration for years, the desktop feels
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.033|                    wrong for a day or two. Fully reversible from menu 4
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.034|                    -> 3 -> 2.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.035|
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.036|  Windows default : MouseSpeed=1, MouseThreshold1=6, MouseThreshold2=10,
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.037|                    MouseSensitivity=10 — all four present as REG_SZ.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.038|                    Enhance pointer precision is ticked on a fresh
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.039|                    install.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.040|
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.041|  Possible values:
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.042|    MouseSpeed=0         : Unchecks 'Enhance pointer precision'. Mouse
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.043|                           counts are no longer run through the pointer-
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.044|                           ballistics curve, so a given physical movement
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.045|                           always produces the same cursor distance
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.046|                           regardless of how fast you moved. Written
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.047|                           together with MouseThreshold1=0 and
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.048|                           MouseThreshold2=0, which is what Control Panel
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.049|                           writes when you untick the box.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.050|    MouseSpeed=1         : The shipped state: 'Enhance pointer precision'
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.051|                           ticked. Fast movements are multiplied, slow
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.052|                           ones are not, so the pointer covers a big
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.053|                           screen quickly and still lands on a single
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.054|                           pixel when you creep. Written together with
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.055|                           MouseThreshold1=6 and MouseThreshold2=10.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.056|    MouseSpeed=2         : Accepted by the API and a leftover from the
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.057|                           pre-XP acceleration model, where crossing
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.058|                           MouseThreshold2 quadrupled movement instead of
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.059|                           doubling it. Nothing in Windows 11 writes it,
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.060|                           and no UI exposes it. Any value above 0 reads
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.061|                           back as 'the box is ticked'.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.062|    MouseThreshold1 / MouseThreshold2 (6 and 10, or 0 and 0) :
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.063|        The two knees of the legacy acceleration model, expressed in
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.064|        mickeys per mouse report: past the first the movement doubled,
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.065|        past the second it doubled again. Control Panel writes 6 and 10
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.066|        when the box is ticked, 0 and 0 when it is not, so in practice
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.067|        they travel with MouseSpeed rather than doing work of their own.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.068|    MouseSensitivity=10  : The middle notch of the 1-20 pointer-speed
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.069|                           slider, and the only one with a multiplier of
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.070|                           exactly 1: every count the mouse reports
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.071|                           becomes cursor movement, nothing added, nothing
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.072|                           dropped. This card always writes 10.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.073|    MouseSensitivity=1 to 9 :
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.074|        Scales movement down by a fraction. Because the scaling happens on
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.075|        integer counts, part of the movement is discarded rather than
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.076|        stored, so small slow movements can be lost entirely. This is why
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.077|        a low slider setting feels imprecise rather than simply slow —
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.078|        lower your mouse DPI instead.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.079|    MouseSensitivity=11 to 20 :
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.080|        Scales movement up. The cursor jumps by more than one pixel per
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.081|        count, so there are screen pixels it can no longer land on.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.082|        Raising mouse DPI achieves the same speed without the skipping.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.083|    SKIP                 : Leaves all four values exactly as they are,
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.084|                           whatever a driver utility, another tweaker or
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.085|                           the user's own Control Panel visit put there.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.086|
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.087|  Why these profiles : Only gaming differs, and I am not going to invent
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.088|                       distinctions for the other four. 1 writes 0 because
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.089|                       a linear desktop cursor is what muscle memory and
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.090|                       cursor-driven games want. 3 office, 4 laptop and 5
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.091|                       Windows all write the stock 1 / 6 / 10 for the same
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.092|                       reason: acceleration is precisely what makes a
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.093|                       small trackpad and a low-DPI office mouse
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.094|                       comfortable, and it costs no power, no CPU and no
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.095|                       latency, so there is no argument for changing it —
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.096|                       note in particular that 4 laptop picks the default
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.097|                       on comfort grounds, not battery grounds, because
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.098|                       none of these values affect power draw at all. 2
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.099|                       server is SKIP: a headless box has no pointer to
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.100|                       tune, and writing HKCU values for an account that
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.101|                       never opens an interactive session is noise.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.102|
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.103|  Unverified      : Three things I cannot substantiate. (a)
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.104|                    MouseThreshold1/2 are the XP-era doubling knees; on
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.105|                    Windows 11 the actual ballistics work is done by
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.106|                    SmoothMouseXCurve/YCurve, so 6/10 versus 0/0 most
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.107|                    likely has no effect of its own and MouseSpeed alone
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.108|                    flips the checkbox — I did not measure this. (b) The
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.109|                    exact multiplier behind each MouseSensitivity notch is
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.110|                    a lookup table I did not verify; what is solid is that
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.111|                    10 is x1, below 10 scales down and above 10 skips
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.112|                    pixels. (c) The old card's claim that 'a REG_DWORD
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.113|                    written here is ignored by Windows' is inherited, not
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.114|                    something I tested — what is certain is that a stock
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.115|                    profile stores all four as REG_SZ.
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.116|
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.117|  Target          : HKCU\Control Panel\Mouse — values MouseSpeed,
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.118|                    MouseThreshold1, MouseThreshold2, MouseSensitivity,
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.119|                    all REG_SZ. Written by the shared :ask_mouse_accel
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.120|                    (called from :setup_system and :gaming_restore) as
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.121|                    MouseSensitivity=10 plus MouseSpeed / MouseThreshold1 /
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.122|                    MouseThreshold2 per PROFVAL. The SmoothMouse curves and
::X|EN|mouse.accel.off.123|                    the live-apply are separate cards asked right after.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.001|  Ce que c est    : Quatre chaînes REG_SZ sous HKCU\Control Panel\Mouse.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.002|                    MouseSpeed est l état de la case « Améliorer la
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.003|                    précision du pointeur ». MouseThreshold1 et
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.004|                    MouseThreshold2 sont les seuils d accélération hérités
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.005|                    que le Panneau de configuration écrit avec elle.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.006|                    MouseSensitivity est le curseur de vitesse 1 à 20,
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.007|                    dont 10 est le cran du milieu.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.008|
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.009|  Effet reel      : MouseSpeed=0 avec les deux seuils à 0 décoche «
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.010|                    Améliorer la précision du pointeur » : le système
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.011|                    cesse de faire passer les impulsions par la courbe de
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.012|                    balistique, donc un même geste physique donne toujours
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.013|                    la même distance à l écran. MouseSensitivity reste à
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.014|                    10, le seul cran qui laisse passer les impulsions sans
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.015|                    multiplicateur. Les quatre sont écrites en REG_SZ, la
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.016|                    forme utilisée par un profil d origine.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.017|
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.018|  Gain            : Sur le curseur du bureau, et dans tout ce qui lit la
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.019|                    position du curseur système, le déplacement devient
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.020|                    linéaire et reproductible. Dans les jeux qui utilisent
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.021|                    le raw input — c est-à-dire à peu près tous les FPS
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.022|                    modernes — le gain est nul : le raw input ne passe
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.023|                    jamais par la balistique du pointeur, votre flick
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.024|                    était déjà en 1:1 avant que vous ne touchiez à quoi
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.025|                    que ce soit. Les vrais bénéficiaires sont les jeux
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.026|                    pilotés au curseur (RTS, MOBA, titres anciens, menus
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.027|                    in-game), le dessin et la retouche photo.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.028|
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.029|  Cout            : Traverser un grand écran ou une dalle haute résolution
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.030|                    demande plus de course sur le bureau, nettement en
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.031|                    dessous de 800 DPI environ. Les pavés tactiles sont
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.032|                    les plus pénalisés : la surface est petite et c est
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.033|                    justement l accélération qui les rend utilisables. Et
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.034|                    si vous vivez avec l accélération depuis des années,
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.035|                    le bureau paraît faux pendant un jour ou deux.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.036|                    Entièrement réversible depuis le menu 4 -> 3 -> 2.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.037|
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.038|  Defaut Windows  : MouseSpeed=1, MouseThreshold1=6, MouseThreshold2=10,
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.039|                    MouseSensitivity=10, les quatre présentes en REG_SZ.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.040|                    La case « Améliorer la précision du pointeur » est
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.041|                    cochée sur une installation neuve.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.042|
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.043|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.044|    MouseSpeed=0         : Décoche « Améliorer la précision du pointeur ».
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.045|                           Les impulsions de la souris ne passent plus par
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.046|                           la courbe de balistique : un déplacement
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.047|                           physique donné produit toujours la même
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.048|                           distance à l écran, quelle que soit la vitesse
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.049|                           du geste. S écrit avec MouseThreshold1=0 et
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.050|                           MouseThreshold2=0, exactement ce qu écrit le
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.051|                           Panneau de configuration quand on décoche la
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.052|                           case.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.053|    MouseSpeed=1         : L état d origine : case « Améliorer la
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.054|                           précision du pointeur » cochée. Les gestes
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.055|                           rapides sont multipliés, les lents ne le sont
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.056|                           pas : le pointeur traverse un grand écran vite
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.057|                           tout en restant capable de viser un pixel quand
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.058|                           on avance doucement. S écrit avec
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.059|                           MouseThreshold1=6 et MouseThreshold2=10.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.060|    MouseSpeed=2         : Accepté par l API, vestige du modèle
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.061|                           d accélération d avant XP où franchir
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.062|                           MouseThreshold2 quadruplait le mouvement au
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.063|                           lieu de le doubler. Rien dans Windows 11 ne
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.064|                           l écrit et aucune interface ne l expose. Toute
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.065|                           valeur supérieure à 0 se relit comme « la case
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.066|                           est cochée ».
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.067|    MouseThreshold1 / MouseThreshold2 (6 and 10, or 0 and 0) :
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.068|        Les deux coudes du modèle d accélération hérité, exprimés en
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.069|        mickeys par rapport de la souris : passé le premier le mouvement
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.070|        doublait, passé le second il doublait encore. Le Panneau de
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.071|        configuration écrit 6 et 10 quand la case est cochée, 0 et 0 sinon
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.072|        : en pratique ils suivent MouseSpeed plutôt que d agir par eux-
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.073|        mêmes.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.074|    MouseSensitivity=10  : Le cran du milieu du curseur de vitesse 1 à 20,
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.075|                           et le seul dont le multiplicateur vaut
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.076|                           exactement 1 : chaque impulsion remontée par la
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.077|                           souris devient du mouvement, rien d ajouté,
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.078|                           rien de perdu. Cette carte écrit toujours 10.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.079|    MouseSensitivity=1 to 9 :
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.080|        Réduit le mouvement d une fraction. Comme la mise à l échelle
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.081|        porte sur des impulsions entières, une partie du déplacement est
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.082|        jetée plutôt que conservée : les petits gestes lents peuvent
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.083|        disparaître complètement. C est pour ça qu un curseur bas donne
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.084|        une impression d imprécision et pas seulement de lenteur — baissez
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.085|        plutôt le DPI de la souris.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.086|    MouseSensitivity=11 to 20 :
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.087|        Amplifie le mouvement. Le curseur saute de plus d un pixel par
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.088|        impulsion : il existe des pixels de l écran sur lesquels il ne
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.089|        peut plus se poser. Monter le DPI de la souris donne la même
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.090|        vitesse sans les sauts.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.091|    SKIP                 : Laisse les quatre valeurs telles quelles, quoi
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.092|                           qu y aient mis un utilitaire de pilote, un
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.093|                           autre optimiseur ou l utilisateur lui-même
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.094|                           depuis le Panneau de configuration.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.095|
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.096|  Pourquoi ces profils : Seul le profil jeu diffère, et je ne vais pas
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.097|                         inventer des différences pour les quatre autres.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.098|                         Le 1 écrit 0 : un curseur de bureau linéaire est
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.099|                         ce que réclament la mémoire musculaire et les
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.100|                         jeux pilotés au curseur. Les profils 3
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.101|                         bureautique, 4 portable et 5 Windows écrivent
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.102|                         tous les valeurs d origine 1 / 6 / 10, pour la
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.103|                         même raison : l accélération est exactement ce
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.104|                         qui rend confortables un petit pavé tactile et
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.105|                         une souris bureautique bas DPI, et elle ne coûte
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.106|                         ni watt, ni CPU, ni latence. À noter pour le 4
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.107|                         portable : il choisit la valeur par défaut pour
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.108|                         le confort, pas pour l autonomie — aucun de ces
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.109|                         réglages n a le moindre effet sur la
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.110|                         consommation. Le 2 serveur est SKIP : une machine
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.111|                         sans écran n a pas de pointeur à régler, et
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.112|                         écrire des valeurs HKCU pour un compte qui
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.113|                         n ouvre jamais de session interactive ne sert à
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.114|                         rien.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.115|
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.116|  Non verifie (en)  : Three things I cannot substantiate. (a)
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.117|                      MouseThreshold1/2 are the XP-era doubling knees; on
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.118|                      Windows 11 the actual ballistics work is done by
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.119|                      SmoothMouseXCurve/YCurve, so 6/10 versus 0/0 most
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.120|                      likely has no effect of its own and MouseSpeed alone
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.121|                      flips the checkbox — I did not measure this. (b) The
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.122|                      exact multiplier behind each MouseSensitivity notch
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.123|                      is a lookup table I did not verify; what is solid is
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.124|                      that 10 is x1, below 10 scales down and above 10
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.125|                      skips pixels. (c) The old card s claim that  a
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.126|                      REG_DWORD written here is ignored by Windows  is
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.127|                      inherited, not something I tested — what is certain
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.128|                      is that a stock profile stores all four as REG_SZ.
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.129|
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.130|  Cible           : HKCU\Control Panel\Mouse — values MouseSpeed,
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.131|                    MouseThreshold1, MouseThreshold2, MouseSensitivity,
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.132|                    all REG_SZ. Written by the shared :ask_mouse_accel
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.133|                    (called from :setup_system and :gaming_restore) as
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.134|                    MouseSensitivity=10 plus MouseSpeed / MouseThreshold1 /
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.135|                    MouseThreshold2 per PROFVAL. The SmoothMouse curves and
::X|FR|mouse.accel.off.136|                    the live-apply are separate cards asked right after.
::
:: ---- mouse.smoothcurve.delete (repair) --------------------------
::P|mouse.smoothcurve.delete|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.001|CLEAN UP CORRUPTED MOUSE ACCELERATION CURVES
::T|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.002|Deletes the two mouse acceleration curve values only if they are not the standard 40-byte size - this is cleanup for a bug an older version of this same script caused, not a fix for anything on a machine that never ran that old version.
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.001|NETTOYER LES COURBES D ACCELERATION DE SOURIS CORROMPUES
::T|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.002|Supprime les deux valeurs de courbe d acceleration de la souris seulement si elles ne font pas la taille standard de 40 octets : c est un rattrapage d un bug cause par une ancienne version de ce meme script, pas la correction d un probleme sur une machine qui n a jamais connu cette vieille version.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.001|  What it is      : SmoothMouseXCurve and SmoothMouseYCurve are the
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.002|                    REG_BINARY acceleration lookup tables under
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.003|                    HKCU\Control Panel\Mouse — the curves the pointer-
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.004|                    ballistics code uses when Enhance pointer precision is
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.005|                    on. A stock pair is 40 bytes each, which reg query
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.006|                    prints as 80 hex characters. OPTY v03.x used to write
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.007|                    truncated 17 and 20 byte versions.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.008|
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.009|  Actual effect   : reg query prints the blob, and the guard keeps the
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.010|                    value only when the hex string is exactly 80
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.011|                    characters long. Anything else is deleted — the short
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.012|                    blobs an old OPTY left behind, and also any blob
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.013|                    longer than 40 bytes. With the value gone, Windows
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.014|                    falls back to its built-in curve, so pointer behaviour
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.015|                    is correct from that moment on.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.016|
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.017|  Gain            : It removes a malformed value that an earlier version
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.018|                    of this same script created. Whether a truncated curve
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.019|                    ever produced a symptom you could feel is not
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.020|                    established — do not expect the pointer to change. On
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.021|                    a machine that never ran v03.x, nothing is deleted at
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.022|                    all. The honest description is tidying up after an old
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.023|                    bug of ours, not a fix for a pointer problem you have.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.024|
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.025|  Cost            : A hand-tuned custom curve is destroyed if it is not
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.026|                    exactly 40 bytes. Custom curves are 40 bytes because
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.027|                    that is the format, so this is close to theoretical —
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.028|                    but it is an exact-length test, not a 'shorter than'
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.029|                    test, and that distinction should be stated rather
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.030|                    than glossed over.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.031|
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.032|  Windows default : Both values present at 40 bytes each. Absent is also a
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.033|                    valid state: Windows has a built-in fallback and works
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.034|                    fine without them.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.035|
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.036|  Possible values:
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.037|    blob is exactly 40 bytes (80 hex characters) :
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.038|        Kept, untouched. This is the stock length and also the length of
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.039|        any legitimately customised curve, so the guard makes the whole
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.040|        operation a no-op on a healthy machine.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.041|    blob is shorter than 40 bytes :
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.042|        Deleted. This is the case the repair exists for: OPTY v03.x wrote
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.043|        truncated 17 and 20 byte blobs. A short blob is not a valid curve
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.044|        structure under any reading.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.045|    blob is longer than 40 bytes :
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.046|        Also deleted — and this is a correction to the old card, which
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.047|        claimed the test was 'shorter than 40 bytes'. The guard is an
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.048|        exact-length test: anything that is not exactly 80 hex characters
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.049|        goes. In practice no known writer produces a longer curve, but the
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.050|        behaviour should be described accurately.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.051|    value absent         : A perfectly valid state. Windows has a built-in
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.052|                           fallback curve, so pointer behaviour is correct
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.053|                           with the values gone. The registry values
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.054|                           reappear the next time something writes them —
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.055|                           the Pointer Options tab does when you tick or
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.056|                           untick Enhance pointer precision.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.057|    SKIP                 : Leaves whatever is there, including a truncated
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.058|                           blob an old OPTY wrote.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.059|
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.060|  Why these profiles : All five identical, and that is the correct answer
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.061|                       rather than a gap in the analysis. The guard makes
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.062|                       this a no-op on a healthy machine, so no profile
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.063|                       benefits from leaving a malformed value in place —
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.064|                       including 5 Windows, since deleting a truncated
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.065|                       blob moves toward shipped behaviour, not away from
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.066|                       it. 2 server runs it too: the check costs one reg
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.067|                       query and a headless box can have inherited the
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.068|                       same bad value from an old run.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.069|
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.070|  Known problems  : The only writer of truncated curves I can name is OPTY
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.071|                    v03.x itself. I cannot name an application and a
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.072|                    symptom caused by them, so this card claims none.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.073|
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.074|  Unverified      : Two claims I could not verify and will not repeat as
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.075|                    fact. The old card said Windows 'regenerates a correct
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.076|                    default curve at the next logon'; what I can support
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.077|                    is that the built-in fallback curve is used when the
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.078|                    values are absent — whether the registry values are
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.079|                    physically re-created at logon, or only when something
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.080|                    writes them, I did not test. And I have no
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.081|                    reproduction of a truncated curve producing observable
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.082|                    pointer misbehaviour, so the 'repairs erratic pointer
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.083|                    behaviour' line from the old card is unsupported.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.084|
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.085|  Target          : HKCU\Control Panel\Mouse — values SmoothMouseXCurve
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.086|                    and SmoothMouseYCurve (REG_BINARY). Guarded delete in
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.087|                    :mousecurve / :curvecheck, OPTY.bat lines 2348-2362.
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.088|                    The guard is `if "%CV:~80,1%"=="" if not
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.089|                    "%CV:~79,1%"=="" goto :eof` — keep only when the hex
::X|EN|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.090|                    string is exactly 80 characters.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.001|  Ce que c est    : SmoothMouseXCurve et SmoothMouseYCurve sont les tables
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.002|                    d accélération REG_BINARY sous HKCU\Control
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.003|                    Panel\Mouse — les courbes qu utilise le code de
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.004|                    balistique du pointeur quand « Améliorer la précision
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.005|                    du pointeur » est actif. Une paire d origine fait 40
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.006|                    octets chacune, que reg query affiche en 80 caractères
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.007|                    hexadécimaux. OPTY v03.x en écrivait des versions
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.008|                    tronquées de 17 et 20 octets.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.009|
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.010|  Effet reel      : reg query affiche le blob, et le garde-fou ne conserve
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.011|                    la valeur que si la chaîne hexadécimale fait
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.012|                    exactement 80 caractères. Tout le reste est supprimé :
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.013|                    les blobs courts laissés par une ancienne version
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.014|                    d OPTY, mais aussi tout blob de plus de 40 octets. La
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.015|                    valeur disparue, Windows retombe sur sa courbe interne
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.016|                    : le pointeur se comporte correctement dès cet
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.017|                    instant.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.018|
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.019|  Gain            : Cela retire une valeur malformée qu une version
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.020|                    antérieure de ce même script a créée. Qu une courbe
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.021|                    tronquée ait jamais produit un symptôme perceptible
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.022|                    n est pas établi — n attendez aucun changement au
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.023|                    pointeur. Sur une machine qui n a jamais vu la v03.x,
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.024|                    rien n est supprimé. La description honnête, c est un
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.025|                    rangement après un de nos anciens bugs, pas la
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.026|                    correction d un problème de pointeur que vous auriez.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.027|
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.028|  Cout            : Une courbe personnalisée faite main est détruite si
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.029|                    elle ne fait pas exactement 40 octets. Les courbes
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.030|                    personnalisées font 40 octets parce que c est le
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.031|                    format, donc le risque est quasi théorique — mais le
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.032|                    test porte sur une longueur exacte, pas sur «
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.033|                    inférieur à », et cette nuance mérite d être dite
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.034|                    plutôt que passée sous silence.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.035|
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.036|  Defaut Windows  : Les deux valeurs présentes, 40 octets chacune.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.037|                    L absence est également un état valide : Windows
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.038|                    dispose d un repli interne et fonctionne très bien
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.039|                    sans elles.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.040|
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.041|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.042|    blob is exactly 40 bytes (80 hex characters) :
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.043|        Conservé, intact. C est la longueur d origine, et aussi celle de
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.044|        toute courbe personnalisée légitime : le garde-fou rend donc
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.045|        l opération totalement neutre sur une machine saine.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.046|    blob is shorter than 40 bytes :
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.047|        Supprimé. C est le cas pour lequel la réparation existe : OPTY
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.048|        v03.x écrivait des blobs tronqués de 17 et 20 octets. Un blob
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.049|        court n est une structure de courbe valide sous aucun angle.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.050|    blob is longer than 40 bytes :
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.051|        Supprimé également — et c est une correction de l ancienne carte,
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.052|        qui annonçait un test « inférieur à 40 octets ». Le garde-fou
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.053|        teste une longueur exacte : tout ce qui ne fait pas exactement 80
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.054|        caractères hexadécimaux dégage. En pratique aucun producteur connu
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.055|        n écrit de courbe plus longue, mais le comportement mérite d être
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.056|        décrit exactement.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.057|    value absent         : Un état parfaitement valide. Windows dispose
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.058|                           d une courbe de repli interne : le pointeur se
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.059|                           comporte correctement même sans ces valeurs.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.060|                           Elles réapparaissent dès que quelque chose les
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.061|                           réécrit — l onglet Options du pointeur le fait
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.062|                           quand on coche ou décoche « Améliorer la
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.063|                           précision du pointeur ».
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.064|    SKIP                 : Laisse ce qui s y trouve, y compris un blob
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.065|                           tronqué écrit par une ancienne version d OPTY.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.066|
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.067|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq sont identiques, et c est la bonne
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.068|                         réponse, pas un trou dans l analyse. Le garde-fou
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.069|                         rend l opération neutre sur une machine saine :
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.070|                         aucun profil ne gagne à laisser une valeur
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.071|                         malformée en place, y compris le 5 Windows,
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.072|                         puisque supprimer un blob tronqué rapproche du
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.073|                         comportement d origine au lieu de l en éloigner.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.074|                         Le 2 serveur l exécute aussi : le contrôle coûte
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.075|                         un reg query, et une machine sans écran a pu
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.076|                         hériter de la même mauvaise valeur lors d un
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.077|                         ancien passage.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.078|
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.079|  Problemes connus : Le seul producteur de courbes tronquées que je peux
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.080|                     nommer est OPTY v03.x lui-même. Je ne peux citer ni
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.081|                     application ni symptôme causé par elles, donc cette
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.082|                     carte n en revendique aucun.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.083|
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.084|  Non verifie (en)  : Two claims I could not verify and will not repeat as
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.085|                      fact. The old card said Windows  regenerates a
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.086|                      correct default curve at the next logon ; what I can
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.087|                      support is that the built-in fallback curve is used
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.088|                      when the values are absent — whether the registry
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.089|                      values are physically re-created at logon, or only
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.090|                      when something writes them, I did not test. And I
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.091|                      have no reproduction of a truncated curve producing
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.092|                      observable pointer misbehaviour, so the  repairs
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.093|                      erratic pointer behaviour  line from the old card is
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.094|                      unsupported.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.095|
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.096|  Cible           : HKCU\Control Panel\Mouse — values SmoothMouseXCurve
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.097|                    and SmoothMouseYCurve (REG_BINARY). Guarded delete in
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.098|                    :mousecurve / :curvecheck, OPTY.bat lines 2348-2362.
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.099|                    The guard is `if "%CV:~80,1%"=="" if not
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.100|                    "%CV:~79,1%"=="" goto :eof` — keep only when the hex
::X|FR|mouse.smoothcurve.delete.101|                    string is exactly 80 characters.
