:: ---- rad.defender.policy.purge (repair) -------------------------
::P|rad.defender.policy.purge|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.001|REMOVE HIDDEN POLICIES THAT DISABLE DEFENDER
::T|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.002|Deletes six leftover policy values that debloat scripts use to force Microsoft Defender off and grey out its switch - if Tamper Protection is on this only fixes the cosmetic managed by your organisation message, but if it is off it genuinely turns real-time protection back on.
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.001|RETIRER LES STRATEGIES CACHEES QUI DESACTIVENT DEFENDER
::T|FR|rad.defender.policy.purge.002|Supprime six valeurs de strategie laissees par des scripts de debloat pour forcer l extinction de Microsoft Defender et griser son bouton : si la protection contre les falsifications est active, cela ne corrige que le message cosmetique gere par votre organisation, mais si elle est coupee, cela reactive vraiment la protection en temps reel.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.001|  What it is      : Six policy REG_DWORDs that debloat scripts write to
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.002|                    keep Microsoft Defender permanently off:
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.003|                    DisableAntiSpyware and DisableAntiVirus under
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.004|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender,
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.005|                    plus DisableRealtimeMonitoring,
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.006|                    DisableBehaviorMonitoring, DisableOnAccessProtection
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.007|                    and DisableScanOnRealtimeEnable under its Real-Time
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.008|                    Protection subkey. Being policy, they sit above the
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.009|                    Windows Security app and grey its switches out.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.010|
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.011|  Actual effect   : Deletes all six values. The keys stay (empty keys are
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.012|                    harmless and deleting user-owned keys is not this
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.013|                    script's job). Windows Security stops reporting a
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.014|                    managed state and its real-time protection switch
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.015|                    becomes usable again. No reboot: Defender picks the
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.016|                    change up on the fly.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.017|
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.018|  Gain            : Honestly, it depends on one thing you cannot see from
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.019|                    here. If Tamper Protection is ON - the default on a
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.020|                    normal consumer Windows 11 - Defender was already
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.021|                    ignoring these values, real-time scanning was already
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.022|                    running, and the only measurable change is cosmetic:
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.023|                    Windows Security drops the "your settings are managed
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.024|                    by your organisation" banner and the greyed-out switch
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.025|                    comes back. If Tamper Protection is OFF, the gain is
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.026|                    real and large: real-time protection was genuinely
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.027|                    disabled and starts working again. There is no
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.028|                    performance gain in either direction - this costs
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.029|                    performance, it does not buy it.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.030|
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.031|  Cost            : Real-time scanning costs disk I/O. Compiling,
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.032|                    extracting large archives and first-launch
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.033|                    shader/asset work get slower, typically a few percent,
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.034|                    more on a slow disk. The answer to that is a folder
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.035|                    exclusion, not a disabled antivirus. If you run a
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.036|                    third-party AV it registers with Windows Security and
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.037|                    Defender drops into passive mode by itself - you will
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.038|                    not end up with two scanners fighting.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.039|
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.040|  Windows default : All six absent. Windows Setup never writes any of
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.041|                    them; every one you find came from a script or a GPO.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.042|
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.043|  Possible values:
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.044|    1                    : Policy says "switch this component off".
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.045|                           DisableAntiSpyware=1 was the old master kill
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.046|                           switch for the whole Defender engine; Microsoft
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.047|                           deprecated it and machines with Tamper
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.048|                           Protection on ignore it outright. The four
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.049|                           Real-Time Protection values still bite when
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.050|                           Tamper Protection is off:
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.051|                           DisableRealtimeMonitoring stops on-access
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.052|                           scanning entirely, DisableBehaviorMonitoring
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.053|                           stops the behavioural engine,
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.054|                           DisableOnAccessProtection stops scanning on
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.055|                           file open/close, DisableScanOnRealtimeEnable
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.056|                           stops the catch-up scan that runs when real-
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.057|                           time protection is re-armed. Whatever the value
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.058|                           does or does not do, its presence makes Windows
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.059|                           Security grey the switch out and print "managed
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.060|                           by your organisation".
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.061|    0                    : The trap. Writing 0 does NOT restore the
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.062|                           default - it creates a third state: a policy
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.063|                           that says "forced on". The component runs, but
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.064|                           the switch stays greyed out in Windows Security
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.065|                           and the machine still reports itself as
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.066|                           managed. Anything that later needs to toggle
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.067|                           real-time protection (a third-party AV
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.068|                           installer, a Defender update, you) is blocked
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.069|                           by your own policy.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.070|    DELETE               : The real default: no policy at all. Defender
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.071|                           obeys the Windows Security app, the switches
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.072|                           are usable, and the state you set in the UI is
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.073|                           the state you get. This is what the script
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.074|                           writes for every profile.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.075|
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.076|  Why these profiles : All five profiles delete, profile 5 included,
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.077|                       because absent is genuinely what Windows ships.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.078|                       There is no machine role where a hidden policy that
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.079|                       silently disables the antivirus is the right state,
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.080|                       and the box that seeds torrents and serves Plex to
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.081|                       the internet is the last one that should have it.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.082|                       If you deliberately run a third-party AV you still
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.083|                       want these gone: your AV takes Defender out of the
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.084|                       way through its own passive-mode registration, not
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.085|                       through these keys, and leaving them behind means
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.086|                       Defender cannot come back if you ever uninstall the
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.087|                       other product. This is a table with no variation in
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.088|                       it, and inventing one would be dishonest.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.089|
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.090|  Known problems  : Nothing known breaks by deleting them. The breakage
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.091|                    runs the other way: with these values left in place
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.092|                    and Tamper Protection off, Windows Security shows a
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.093|                    permanent red "Virus & threat protection is off" state
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.094|                    that no button in the UI can clear, and third-party AV
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.095|                    installers occasionally abort because they cannot
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.096|                    query Defender's status.
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.097|
::X|EN|rad.defender.policy.purge.098|  Unverified      : With Tamper Protection ON, Defender already ignores
