:: ---- net.nic.rss.queues (preference) --------------------------------
::P|net.nic.rss.queues|1/ENUMMAX|1/ENUMMAX|1/ENUMMAX|1/ENUMMAX|SKIP|
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.001|RECEIVE SIDE SCALING WITH A REAL QUEUE COUNT
::T|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.002|Spreads incoming network traffic across several CPU cores instead of one, using the exact queue count your card actually supports; usually not noticeable on a modern multi-core CPU.
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.001|RSS AVEC UN NOMBRE DE FILES REELLEMENT SUPPORTE
::T|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.002|Repartit le trafic reseau entrant sur plusieurs coeurs CPU au lieu d un seul, en utilisant le nombre de files reellement supporte par votre carte ; generalement peu perceptible sur un CPU moderne multi-coeurs.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.001|  What it is      : RSS spreads incoming packet processing across several
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.002|                    CPU cores instead of pinning it all to one.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.003|                    *NumRssQueues is how many receive queues the card is
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.004|                    told to use.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.005|
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.006|  Actual effect   : Writes *RSS=1, then reads the values your driver
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.007|                    actually enumerates and writes the highest one. Most
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.008|                    scripts hardcode 4 or 8; if the card only enumerates 1
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.009|                    and 2 the driver silently ignores that and the script
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.010|                    reports a success that never happened.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.011|
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.012|  Gain            : On a card with more than one queue, high-throughput
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.013|                    traffic stops saturating a single core - visible
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.014|                    during large downloads or heavy LAN transfers,
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.015|                    particularly on older or low-core CPUs. On a 1 Gbit
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.016|                    link with a modern 8-core CPU the difference is
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.017|                    usually not measurable, and this card will not claim
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.018|                    otherwise.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.019|
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.020|  Cost            : Slightly more cross-core work for the same traffic and
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.021|                    marginally higher power draw. No meaningful latency
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.022|                    penalty.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.023|
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.024|  Windows default : Driver-supplied. *RSS ships enabled on essentially all
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.025|                    multi-queue adapters; the shipped *NumRssQueues varies
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.026|                    by chipset and is often 2. Your card's real default is
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.027|                    readable at Ndi\Params\*NumRssQueues\default.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.028|
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.029|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.030|    *RSS=0               : Every received packet is processed on the
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.031|                           single CPU the adapter's interrupt is
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.032|                           affinitised to. That core saturates before the
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.033|                           link does on a fast connection.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.034|    *RSS=1               : Receive processing is hashed across several
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.035|                           cores, one queue per core. Shipped enabled on
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.036|                           essentially every multi-queue adapter.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.037|    *NumRssQueues=1      : RSS is on but there is a single queue, so it
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.038|                           behaves exactly like RSS off.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.039|    *NumRssQueues=2, 4, 8, 16 :
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.040|        That many receive queues, if the driver enumerates the value. A
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.041|        number the driver does not enumerate is written to the registry,
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.042|        accepted, and ignored by the driver - which is why scripts that
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.043|        hardcode 8 report a success that never happened.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.044|    ENUMMAX              : OPTY's literal: the highest value actually
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.045|                           present under Ndi\Params\*NumRssQueues\Enum on
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.046|                           your own card. On an Intel I211 that is 2, not
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.047|                           8.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.048|    SKIP                 : Neither keyword is written; both stay at the
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.049|                           driver's shipped values.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.050|
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.051|  Why these profiles : Four identical columns, and the reason is that the
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.052|                       value written is not a fixed number - it is
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.053|                       whatever your own driver enumerates. A single-queue
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.054|                       card gets a no-op, a multi-queue card gets its own
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.055|                       ceiling, so there is nothing left for a usage
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.056|                       profile to differ about. The Windows column is SKIP
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.057|                       because OPTY writes the highest ENUMERATED value
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.058|                       rather than the driver's default, and on some
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.059|                       adapters that is above factory.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.060|
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.061|  Unverified      : The shipped *NumRssQueues for a given adapter cannot
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.062|                    be asserted here - it is per chipset. OPTY writes the
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.063|                    highest ENUMERATED value, not the driver's default, so
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.064|                    on some adapters this raises the queue count above
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.065|                    what the card shipped with.
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.066|
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.067|  Target          : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*RSS" "1", then call
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.068|                    :nicenummax "%NICKEY%" "*NumRssQueues" which reads the
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.069|                    highest REG_SZ entry under
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.070|                    Ndi\Params\*NumRssQueues\Enum into NENUMMAX, then call
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.071|                    :nicset with that value (OPTY.bat ~2858-2860, helper
::X|EN|net.nic.rss.queues.072|                    :nicenummax ~3483)
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.001|  Ce que c est    : Le RSS repartit le traitement des paquets entrants sur
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.002|                    plusieurs coeurs au lieu de tout concentrer sur un
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.003|                    seul. *NumRssQueues indique combien de files de
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.004|                    reception la carte doit utiliser.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.005|
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.006|  Effet reel      : Ecrit *RSS=1, puis lit les valeurs que votre pilote
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.007|                    enumere reellement et ecrit la plus elevee. La plupart
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.008|                    des scripts codent 4 ou 8 en dur ; si la carte
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.009|                    n enumere que 1 et 2, le pilote ignore l ecriture en
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.010|                    silence et le script annonce une reussite qui n a
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.011|                    jamais eu lieu.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.012|
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.013|  Gain            : Sur une carte a plusieurs files, un gros debit cesse
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.014|                    de saturer un seul coeur : cela se voit pendant les
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.015|                    gros telechargements ou les transferts LAN intensifs,
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.016|                    surtout sur un CPU ancien ou a peu de coeurs. Sur une
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.017|                    liaison 1 Gb/s avec un huit coeurs moderne, la
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.018|                    difference n est generalement pas mesurable, et cette
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.019|                    fiche ne pretendra pas le contraire.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.020|
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.021|  Cout            : Un peu plus de travail reparti entre les coeurs pour
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.022|                    le meme trafic, et une consommation marginalement plus
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.023|                    elevee. Aucune penalite de latence significative.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.024|
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.025|  Defaut Windows  : Valeur fournie par le pilote. *RSS est livre actif sur
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.026|                    pratiquement toutes les cartes multi-files ; la valeur
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.027|                    d origine de *NumRssQueues depend du chipset et vaut
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.028|                    souvent 2. La vraie valeur de votre carte se lit dans
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.029|                    Ndi\Params\*NumRssQueues\default.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.030|
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.031|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.032|    *RSS=0               : Chaque paquet recu est traite sur l unique CPU
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.033|                           auquel l interruption de la carte est affectee.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.034|                           Sur une connexion rapide, ce coeur sature avant
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.035|                           le lien.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.036|    *RSS=1               : Le traitement en reception est reparti par
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.037|                           hachage sur plusieurs coeurs, une file par
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.038|                           coeur. Livre actif sur pratiquement toutes les
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.039|                           cartes multi-files.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.040|    *NumRssQueues=1      : Le RSS est actif mais il n y a qu une file : le
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.041|                           comportement est exactement celui du RSS
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.042|                           desactive.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.043|    *NumRssQueues=2, 4, 8, 16 :
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.044|        Autant de files de reception, si le pilote enumere la valeur. Un
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.045|        nombre qu il n enumere pas est ecrit dans le registre, accepte,
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.046|        puis ignore par le pilote - c est pour cela que les scripts qui
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.047|        codent 8 en dur annoncent une reussite qui n a jamais eu lieu.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.048|    ENUMMAX              : Le litteral d OPTY : la valeur la plus elevee
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.049|                           reellement presente dans
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.050|                           Ndi\Params\*NumRssQueues\Enum sur votre propre
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.051|                           carte. Sur une Intel I211, c est 2 et non 8.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.052|    SKIP                 : Aucun des deux mots-cles n est ecrit ; les deux
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.053|                           restent aux valeurs livrees par le pilote.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.054|
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.055|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes identiques, et la raison est que
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.056|                         la valeur ecrite n est pas un nombre fixe : c est
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.057|                         ce que votre propre pilote enumere. Une carte a
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.058|                         file unique recoit un non-evenement, une carte
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.059|                         multi-files recoit son propre plafond - il ne
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.060|                         reste donc rien qu un profil d usage puisse faire
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.061|                         varier. La colonne Windows est SKIP parce qu OPTY
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.062|                         ecrit la valeur la plus elevee ENUMEREE et non
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.063|                         celle par defaut du pilote, ce qui depasse
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.064|                         l usine sur certaines cartes.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.065|
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.066|  Non verifie (en)  : The shipped *NumRssQueues for a given adapter cannot
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.067|                      be asserted here - it is per chipset. OPTY writes
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.068|                      the highest ENUMERATED value, not the driver s
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.069|                      default, so on some adapters this raises the queue
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.070|                      count above what the card shipped with.
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.071|
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.072|  Cible           : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*RSS" "1", then call
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.073|                    :nicenummax "%NICKEY%" "*NumRssQueues" which reads the
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.074|                    highest REG_SZ entry under
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.075|                    Ndi\Params\*NumRssQueues\Enum into NENUMMAX, then call
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.076|                    :nicset with that value (OPTY.bat ~2858-2860, helper
::X|FR|net.nic.rss.queues.077|                    :nicenummax ~3483)
::
:: ---- net.nic.jumbo.apply (repair) -------------------------------
::P|net.nic.jumbo.apply|1514|1514|1514|1514|SKIP|
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.001|JUMBO FRAMES OFF (STANDARD 1514-BYTE FRAMES)
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.002|Keeps the network card on standard frame size, avoiding a classic mismatch problem when not every device on the path supports jumbo frames; only costs you something if you run a dedicated jumbo-frame storage network.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.001|TRAMES JUMBO DESACTIVEES (TAILLE STANDARD 1514)
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.002|Garde la carte reseau sur la taille de trame standard, ce qui evite un probleme classique quand tous les equipements du reseau ne supportent pas les trames jumbo ; ne coute quelque chose que si vous avez un reseau de stockage dedie en trames jumbo.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.001|  What it is      : Jumbo frames send up to 9000 bytes per Ethernet frame
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.002|                    instead of the standard 1500. *JumboPacket=1514 is the
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.003|                    off setting on Intel and Realtek drivers.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.004|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.005|  Actual effect   : Writes 1514, keeping the adapter on standard-size
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.006|                    frames. On a normally configured PC this re-asserts
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.007|                    what the driver already shipped.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.008|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.009|  Gain            : Avoids a classic self-inflicted problem: jumbo frames
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.010|                    only work if every device and switch on the path
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.011|                    agrees. On a machine already at 1514 there is no gain
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.012|                    at all, and this card says so.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.013|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.014|  Cost            : If you run a NAS on a dedicated storage VLAN where
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.015|                    everything is set to 9000 bytes, this undoes it and
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.016|                    you lose the throughput and CPU efficiency that setup
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.017|                    exists for. That is the only real scenario.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.018|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.019|  Windows default : 1514 (jumbo disabled) on Intel and Realtek wired
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.020|                    drivers, and on effectively every current Windows NIC
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.021|                    driver.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.022|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.023|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.024|    1514                 : Standard Ethernet frame: a 1500-byte MTU plus
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.025|                           the 14-byte header. This is the off position
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.026|                           for jumbo frames on Intel and Realtek drivers.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.027|    4088 or 4096         : Mid-size jumbo, exact value vendor-dependent.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.028|                           Only useful if every hop is configured for it.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.029|    9014 or 9000         : Full jumbo. Works only if every switch port,
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.030|                           router and peer on the path agrees. Enable it
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.031|                           on one PC and traffic to the internet, whose
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.032|                           path is 1500 bytes, fragments and throughput
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.033|                           drops.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.034|    SKIP                 : The keyword is not written; the adapter keeps
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.035|                           the driver's own frame size setting.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.036|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.037|  Why these profiles : Four identical columns at 1514. Anything leaving
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.038|                       your LAN meets a 1500-byte path and fragments, so
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.039|                       jumbo frames only pay on a closed storage network -
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.040|                       and none of these four profiles describes one. The
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.041|                       server profile here means torrents, Plex and game
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.042|                       servers over the internet, not a 10G iSCSI fabric.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.043|                       Windows is SKIP because 1514 is the driver's value
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.044|                       and this path does not read the driver's default.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.045|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.046|  Target          : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*JumboPacket" "1514" - REG_SZ
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.apply.047|                    in the adapter class key (OPTY.bat ~2863)
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.001|  Ce que c est    : Les trames jumbo transportent jusqu a 9000 octets par
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.002|                    trame Ethernet au lieu des 1500 standard.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.003|                    *JumboPacket=1514, c est la position desactive chez
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.004|                    Intel et Realtek.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.005|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.006|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 1514, ce qui garde la carte sur des trames de
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.007|                    taille standard. Sur un PC configure normalement, cela
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.008|                    reaffirme ce que le pilote livrait deja.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.009|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.010|  Gain            : Evite un probleme classique qu on se cree soi-meme :
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.011|                    les trames jumbo ne marchent que si tous les
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.012|                    equipements du chemin sont d accord. Sur une machine
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.013|                    deja a 1514, il n y a aucun gain, et cette fiche le
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.014|                    dit.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.015|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.016|  Cout            : Si vous avez un NAS sur un VLAN de stockage dedie ou
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.017|                    tout est regle a 9000 octets, ceci l annule et vous
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.018|                    perdez le debit et l efficacite CPU pour lesquels
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.019|                    cette installation existe. C est le seul cas reel.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.020|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.021|  Defaut Windows  : 1514 (jumbo desactive) sur les pilotes filaires Intel
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.022|                    et Realtek, et sur pratiquement tous les pilotes
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.023|                    reseau Windows actuels.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.024|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.025|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.026|    1514                 : Trame Ethernet standard : un MTU de 1500 octets
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.027|                           plus l en-tete de 14 octets. C est la position
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.028|                           desactive des trames jumbo chez Intel et
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.029|                           Realtek.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.030|    4088 or 4096         : Jumbo de taille intermediaire, valeur exacte
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.031|                           selon le fabricant. Utile seulement si chaque
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.032|                           equipement du chemin est configure pareil.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.033|    9014 or 9000         : Jumbo complet. Ne fonctionne que si chaque port
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.034|                           de switch, routeur et machine d en face est
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.035|                           d accord. Active sur un seul PC, le trafic vers
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.036|                           Internet, dont le chemin est a 1500 octets, se
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.037|                           fragmente et le debit s effondre.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.038|    SKIP                 : Le mot-cle n est pas ecrit ; la carte garde le
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.039|                           reglage de taille de trame du pilote.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.040|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.041|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes identiques a 1514. Tout ce qui
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.042|                         sort de votre reseau local rencontre un chemin a
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.043|                         1500 octets et se fragmente : les trames jumbo ne
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.044|                         paient que sur un reseau de stockage ferme, et
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.045|                         aucun de ces quatre profils n en decrit un. Ici,
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.046|                         le profil serveur signifie torrents, Plex et
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.047|                         serveurs de jeu sur Internet, pas une
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.048|                         infrastructure iSCSI 10G. Windows est SKIP parce
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.049|                         que 1514 est la valeur du pilote et que ce chemin
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.050|                         ne lit pas la valeur par defaut du pilote.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.051|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.052|  Cible           : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*JumboPacket" "1514" - REG_SZ
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.apply.053|                    in the adapter class key (OPTY.bat ~2863)
::
:: ---- net.nic.buffers.raise (preference) -----------------------------
::P|net.nic.buffers.raise|1024|1024|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.001|RAISE THE NETWORK CARD BUFFER SIZE
::T|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.002|Gives the network card more room to hold incoming and outgoing packets during a traffic burst, so fewer get dropped; only matters under heavy load like big downloads or torrents, invisible otherwise.
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.001|AUGMENTER LES TAMPONS DE LA CARTE RESEAU
::T|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.002|Donne a la carte reseau plus de place pour stocker les paquets en rafale afin d en perdre moins ; ne compte que sous forte charge comme un gros telechargement ou un torrent, invisible le reste du temps.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.001|  What it is      : Descriptor rings are the queues the card uses to hand
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.002|                    packets to the driver and take packets from it. More
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.003|                    entries means more headroom before a burst of traffic
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.004|                    has to be dropped.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.005|
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.006|  Actual effect   : Requests 1024 for both *ReceiveBuffers and
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.007|                    *TransmitBuffers, then clamps to the driver's own
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.008|                    maximum and rounds down to its step - so the value
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.009|                    actually written is frequently far below 1024, and the
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.010|                    log says which.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.011|
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.012|  Gain            : Fewer dropped packets under burst load. The cases
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.013|                    where it matters are a saturated link, a busy LAN
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.014|                    transfer, or a torrent client at full tilt. On an idle
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.015|                    or lightly loaded machine you will not measure
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.016|                    anything at all.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.017|
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.018|  Cost            : A little more non-paged pool permanently reserved by
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.019|                    the driver - kilobytes to low megabytes. In theory
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.020|                    deeper queues add a little buffering latency under
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.021|                    sustained saturation; at these sizes it is not
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.022|                    something you will feel.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.023|
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.024|  Windows default : Driver- and chipset-dependent. An Intel I211 for
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.025|                    instance ships 256 receive and 512 transmit. Your
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.026|                    card's real default is at
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.027|                    Ndi\Params\*ReceiveBuffers\default.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.028|
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.029|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.030|    1024                 : The requested ring size. The value actually
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.031|                           written is clamped to Ndi\Params\<keyword>\max
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.032|                           and rounded down to \step, so a card whose
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.033|                           maximum is 512 gets 512 - and the log line says
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.034|                           what was written rather than what was asked
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.035|                           for.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.036|    256 or 512           : Common shipped sizes. An Intel I211 for
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.037|                           instance ships 256 receive and 512 transmit
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.038|                           descriptors.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.039|    80 or 128            : Small rings. Less non-paged pool reserved, less
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.040|                           headroom: a burst arriving faster than the
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.041|                           driver drains the ring is dropped, and TCP
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.042|                           retransmits.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.043|    SKIP                 : Neither keyword is written; the driver's ring
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.044|                           sizes stay as shipped and no extra non-paged
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.045|                           pool is reserved.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.046|
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.047|  Why these profiles : Gaming and server say 1024 because both actually
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.048|                       saturate the link: a Steam download and a torrent
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.049|                       client at full tilt are precisely the burst load a
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.050|                       deeper ring absorbs. Office and laptop say SKIP
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.051|                       because they never reach the drop threshold, so the
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.052|                       only thing they would collect is a permanently
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.053|                       reserved slice of non-paged pool - memory that
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.054|                       never pages out, which matters more on the laptop
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.055|                       than anywhere else. Windows says SKIP because the
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.056|                       shipped size is per chipset and this path does not
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.057|                       read it.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.058|
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.059|  Unverified      : Per-adapter shipped buffer values vary too much
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.060|                    between chipsets to state one here. OPTY reads the
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.061|                    driver's max and step at runtime but does not read the
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.062|                    driver's default, so it cannot report how far above
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.063|                    factory the written value is.
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.064|
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.065|  Target          : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*ReceiveBuffers" "1024" and
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.066|                    "*TransmitBuffers" "1024", clamped to
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.067|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword>\max by :nicclamp and rounded down
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.068|                    to \step by :nicround (OPTY.bat ~2866-2867, helpers
::X|EN|net.nic.buffers.raise.069|                    ~3470-3480)
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.001|  Ce que c est    : Les anneaux de descripteurs sont les files que la
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.002|                    carte utilise pour passer les paquets au pilote et en
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.003|                    recuperer. Plus il y a d entrees, plus il y a de marge
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.004|                    avant qu une rafale doive etre jetee.
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.005|
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.006|  Effet reel      : Demande 1024 pour *ReceiveBuffers et *TransmitBuffers,
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.007|                    puis borne au maximum du pilote et arrondit vers le
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.008|                    bas selon son pas. La valeur reellement ecrite est
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.009|                    donc souvent tres inferieure a 1024, et le journal
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.010|                    indique laquelle.
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.011|
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.012|  Gain            : Moins de paquets perdus en rafale. Les cas ou cela
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.013|                    compte : une liaison saturee, un gros transfert LAN,
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.014|                    ou un client torrent a fond. Sur une machine au repos
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.015|                    ou peu chargee, vous ne mesurerez strictement rien.
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.016|
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.017|  Cout            : Un peu plus de memoire non paginee reservee en
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.018|                    permanence par le pilote, de quelques Ko a quelques
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.019|                    Mo. En theorie, des files plus profondes ajoutent un
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.020|                    peu de latence de mise en tampon en saturation
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.021|                    prolongee ; a ces tailles-la, cela ne se sent pas.
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.022|
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.023|  Defaut Windows  : Depend du pilote et du chipset. Une Intel I211, par
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.024|                    exemple, est livree a 256 en reception et 512 en
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.025|                    emission. La vraie valeur de votre carte est dans
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.026|                    Ndi\Params\*ReceiveBuffers\default.
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.027|
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.028|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.029|    1024                 : La taille d anneau demandee. La valeur
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.030|                           reellement ecrite est bornee a Ndi\Params\<mot-
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.031|                           cle>\max et arrondie vers le bas selon \step :
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.032|                           une carte dont le maximum est 512 recoit 512,
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.033|                           et le journal indique ce qui a ete ecrit et non
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.034|                           ce qui a ete demande.
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.035|    256 or 512           : Tailles livrees courantes. Une Intel I211, par
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.036|                           exemple, est livree avec 256 descripteurs en
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.037|                           reception et 512 en emission.
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.038|    80 or 128            : Petits anneaux. Moins de pool non pagine
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.039|                           reserve, moins de marge : une rafale qui arrive
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.040|                           plus vite que le pilote ne vide l anneau est
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.041|                           jetee, et TCP retransmet.
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.042|    SKIP                 : Aucun des deux mots-cles n est ecrit ; les
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.043|                           tailles d anneau du pilote restent celles
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.044|                           d origine et aucun pool non pagine
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.045|                           supplementaire n est reserve.
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.046|
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.047|  Pourquoi ces profils : Gaming et serveur passent a 1024 parce que les
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.048|                         deux saturent reellement le lien : un
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.049|                         telechargement Steam et un client torrent a fond
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.050|                         sont exactement la charge en rafale qu un anneau
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.051|                         plus profond absorbe. Bureautique et portable
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.052|                         restent sur SKIP parce qu ils n atteignent jamais
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.053|                         le seuil de perte : la seule chose qu ils
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.054|                         recolteraient serait une part de pool non pagine
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.055|                         reservee en permanence - de la memoire qui ne
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.056|                         sort jamais, ce qui pese davantage sur un
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.057|                         portable qu ailleurs. Windows reste sur SKIP
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.058|                         parce que la taille livree depend du chipset et
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.059|                         que ce chemin ne la lit pas.
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.060|
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.061|  Non verifie (en)  : Per-adapter shipped buffer values vary too much
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.062|                      between chipsets to state one here. OPTY reads the
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.063|                      driver s max and step at runtime but does not read
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.064|                      the driver s default, so it cannot report how far
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.065|                      above factory the written value is.
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.066|
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.067|  Cible           : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*ReceiveBuffers" "1024" and
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.068|                    "*TransmitBuffers" "1024", clamped to
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.069|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword>\max by :nicclamp and rounded down
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.070|                    to \step by :nicround (OPTY.bat ~2866-2867, helpers
::X|FR|net.nic.buffers.raise.071|                    ~3470-3480)
::
:: ---- net.nic.eee.off (preference) -----------------------------------
::P|net.nic.eee.off|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.001|ENERGY EFFICIENT ETHERNET OFF
::T|EN|net.nic.eee.off.002|Stops the network port from dozing between packets, which can fix an unstable or flaky link; if your connection is already stable there is no gain, only a small continuous power cost.
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.001|ETHERNET A ECONOMIE D ENERGIE DESACTIVE
::T|FR|net.nic.eee.off.002|Empeche le port reseau de somnoler entre les paquets, ce qui peut corriger un lien instable ; si votre connexion est deja stable, il n y a aucun gain, seulement un petit cout d energie permanent.
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.001|  What it is      : 802.3az Energy Efficient Ethernet lets the PHY sleep
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.002|                    between frames. Vendors spell it differently, and OPTY
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.003|                    writes 0 to whichever keyword the driver exposes.
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.004|
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.005|  Actual effect   : The port stops advertising and entering Low Power
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.006|                    Idle, so the link stays fully awake and there is no
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.007|                    wake-from-idle transition between frames.
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.008|
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.009|  Gain            : This is a link-stability setting, not a ping fix. If
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.010|                    your link renegotiates, drops for a second, or trains
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.011|                    at the wrong speed with a particular switch, EEE is a
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.012|                    real suspect and turning it off is a real fix. If your
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.013|                    link is stable the honest gain is zero - during a game
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.014|                    the link is never idle long enough to enter Low Power
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.015|                    Idle in the first place, so there is nothing for the
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.016|                    wake transition to delay.
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.017|
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.018|  Cost            : About 0.5 W per port, continuously. And this step is
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.019|                    deliberately NOT undone by the network Restore: the
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.020|                    restore filter excludes the EEE and Wake keywords so
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.021|                    it never silently re-enables what you turned off.
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.022|                    Undoing it means Device Manager, Advanced tab, by
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.023|                    hand.
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.024|
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.025|  Windows default : Enabled on drivers that expose it - Intel's EEE and
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.026|                    Realtek's Green Ethernet both ship on.
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.027|
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.028|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.029|    0                    : The PHY stops advertising 802.3az and never
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.030|                           enters Low Power Idle. The link stays fully
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.031|                           awake between frames, so there is no wake-from-
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.032|                           idle transition and no renegotiation triggered
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.033|                           by one.
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.034|    1                    : Energy Efficient Ethernet enabled, which is how
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.035|                           every driver that exposes it ships. The PHY
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.036|                           drops to Low Power Idle when there is no
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.037|                           traffic and wakes on the next frame. Vendors
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.038|                           spell the keyword differently: *EEE,
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.039|                           EEELinkAdvertisement, AdvancedEEE,
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.040|                           EnableGreenEthernet.
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.041|    SKIP                 : No keyword is written; EEE stays as the driver
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.042|                           shipped it, and the automatic undo path stays
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.043|                           intact for everything else.
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.044|
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.045|  Why these profiles : Five identical SKIP columns, and that is the honest
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.046|                       answer rather than a shrug. This is diagnosed by
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.047|                       symptom, not by usage: the machine that should say
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.048|                       yes is the one whose link actually flaps or trains
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.049|                       at the wrong speed with a specific switch, and that
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.050|                       machine can equally be a gaming PC, an office PC or
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.051|                       a laptop. The laptop has one extra reason to leave
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.052|                       it alone - it is a continuous battery cost with no
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.053|                       counterpart. Say yes only if you have seen the
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.054|                       fault yourself.
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.055|
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.056|  Unverified      : The 0.5 W per port figure is the commonly cited
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.057|                    802.3az saving for a gigabit copper PHY, not a
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.058|                    measurement taken on this machine.
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.059|
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.060|  Target          : call :nicset for EEELinkAdvertisement=0, *EEE=0,
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.061|                    EnableGreenEthernet=0 and AdvancedEEE=0 - whichever
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.062|                    the driver exposes (OPTY.bat ~2869-2873). Deliberately
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.063|                    excluded from the :net_restore keyword filter, so the
::X|EN|net.nic.eee.off.064|                    network Restore never undoes it.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.001|  Ce que c est    : L Energy Efficient Ethernet (802.3az) laisse la puce
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.002|                    PHY dormir entre les trames. Chaque fabricant
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.003|                    l appelle autrement, et OPTY ecrit 0 dans le mot-cle
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.004|                    que le pilote expose.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.005|
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.006|  Effet reel      : Le port cesse d annoncer et d entrer en veille basse
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.007|                    consommation : le lien reste pleinement eveille, sans
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.008|                    transition de reveil entre les trames.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.009|
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.010|  Gain            : C est un reglage de stabilite du lien, pas un remede
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.011|                    au ping. Si votre lien renegocie, tombe une seconde ou
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.012|                    se cale a la mauvaise vitesse avec un commutateur
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.013|                    precis, l EEE est un vrai suspect et le couper est une
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.014|                    vraie solution. Si votre lien est stable, le gain
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.015|                    honnete est nul : pendant une partie, le lien n est
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.016|                    jamais inactif assez longtemps pour entrer en veille,
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.017|                    il n y a donc aucune transition de reveil a retarder.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.018|
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.019|  Cout            : Environ 0,5 W par port, en continu. Et cette etape
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.020|                    n est volontairement PAS annulee par la restauration
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.021|                    reseau : le filtre de restauration exclut les mots-
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.022|                    cles EEE et Wake pour ne jamais reactiver en douce ce
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.023|                    que vous aviez coupe. L annuler demande de passer par
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.024|                    le Gestionnaire de peripheriques, onglet Avance, a la
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.025|                    main.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.026|
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.027|  Defaut Windows  : Active sur les pilotes qui l exposent - l EEE d Intel
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.028|                    comme le Green Ethernet de Realtek sont actifs
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.029|                    d origine.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.030|
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.031|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.032|    0                    : La puce PHY cesse d annoncer 802.3az et n entre
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.033|                           jamais en veille basse consommation. Le lien
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.034|                           reste pleinement eveille entre les trames :
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.035|                           plus de transition de reveil, ni de
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.036|                           renegociation declenchee par une telle
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.037|                           transition.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.038|    1                    : Ethernet a economie d energie active, ce qui
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.039|                           est l etat livre par tous les pilotes qui
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.040|                           l exposent. La PHY passe en veille basse
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.041|                           consommation quand il n y a pas de trafic et se
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.042|                           reveille a la trame suivante. Chaque fabricant
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.043|                           nomme le mot-cle autrement : *EEE,
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.044|                           EEELinkAdvertisement, AdvancedEEE,
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.045|                           EnableGreenEthernet.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.046|    SKIP                 : Aucun mot-cle n est ecrit ; l EEE reste tel que
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.047|                           le pilote l a livre, et le chemin d annulation
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.048|                           automatique reste intact pour le reste.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.049|
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.050|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques a SKIP, et c est une
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.051|                         reponse assumee, pas une derobade. Cela se
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.052|                         diagnostique par symptome et non par usage : la
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.053|                         machine qui doit dire oui est celle dont le lien
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.054|                         tombe ou se cale a la mauvaise vitesse avec un
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.055|                         commutateur precis, et ce peut etre aussi bien un
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.056|                         PC de jeu qu un poste bureautique ou un portable.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.057|                         Le portable a une raison de plus de ne pas y
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.058|                         toucher : c est un cout de batterie continu sans
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.059|                         contrepartie. Dites oui uniquement si vous avez
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.060|                         constate la panne.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.061|
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.062|  Non verifie (en)  : The 0.5 W per port figure is the commonly cited
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.063|                      802.3az saving for a gigabit copper PHY, not a
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.064|                      measurement taken on this machine.
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.065|
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.066|  Cible           : call :nicset for EEELinkAdvertisement=0, *EEE=0,
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.067|                    EnableGreenEthernet=0 and AdvancedEEE=0 - whichever
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.068|                    the driver exposes (OPTY.bat ~2869-2873). Deliberately
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.069|                    excluded from the :net_restore keyword filter, so the
::X|FR|net.nic.eee.off.070|                    network Restore never undoes it.
::
:: ---- net.nic.flowcontrol.profile (preference) -----------------------
::P|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile|3|3|3|3|SKIP|
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.001|ETHERNET FLOW CONTROL (802.3X PAUSE FRAMES)
::T|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.002|Controls whether your switch can briefly pause your upload instead of dropping packets when its buffer fills; most home networks never trigger this, so the effect is usually exactly zero either way.
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.001|CONTROLE DE FLUX ETHERNET (TRAMES PAUSE 802.3X)
::T|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.002|Determine si votre switch peut brievement mettre votre envoi en pause au lieu de jeter des paquets quand sa memoire se remplit ; la plupart des reseaux domestiques ne declenchent jamais ce mecanisme, donc l effet est en general exactement nul.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.001|  What it is      : Flow control lets a switch send a PAUSE frame telling
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.002|                    your card to stop transmitting for a moment when the
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.003|                    switch's buffer fills. OPTY writes 3 for the Balanced
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.004|                    and Throughput profiles and 0 for the Low latency
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.005|                    profile.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.006|
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.007|  Actual effect   : With it on, a PAUSE frame can stall your upload for up
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.008|                    to about 33.6 ms at gigabit. With it off, the switch
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.009|                    drops those packets instead and TCP retransmits them.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.010|
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.011|  Gain            : Only if something on your LAN actually sends PAUSE
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.012|                    frames, and most consumer switches and routers never
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.013|                    do. When one does, turning it off replaces an
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.014|                    occasional multi-millisecond upload stall with dropped
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.015|                    packets, which a game tolerates better than a stall.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.016|                    If nothing sends PAUSE frames the measured effect is
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.017|                    exactly zero, and the script says so instead of
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.018|                    claiming a win.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.019|
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.020|  Cost            : Under saturation you trade a brief pause for real
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.021|                    packet loss, which hurts bulk uploads and backups. And
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.022|                    the network Restore does not cover *FlowControl, so if
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.023|                    you write 0 you undo it by hand in Device Manager.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.024|
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.025|  Windows default : Driver-supplied, commonly 3 (Rx and Tx Enabled) on
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.026|                    Intel adapters. Your card's real default is at
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.027|                    Ndi\Params\*FlowControl\default.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.028|
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.029|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.030|    0                    : Disabled. The card neither sends nor honours
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.031|                           802.3x PAUSE frames. A switch whose buffer
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.032|                           fills drops your packets instead of stalling
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.033|                           you, and TCP retransmits them.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.034|    1                    : Tx Enabled. The card sends PAUSE frames when
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.035|                           its own receive ring fills, but ignores
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.036|                           incoming ones. This is the surgical option if
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.037|                           what you want is never to have your upload
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.038|                           stalled - OPTY does not offer it, but it is a
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.039|                           valid keyword value.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.040|    2                    : Rx Enabled. The card honours incoming PAUSE
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.041|                           frames but never sends them.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.042|    3                    : Rx and Tx Enabled, the usual shipped value on
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.043|                           Intel adapters. A received PAUSE frame can
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.044|                           stall your transmit for up to about 33.6 ms at
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.045|                           gigabit.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.046|    SKIP                 : The keyword is not written; it stays at
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.047|                           Ndi\Params\*FlowControl\default, and the
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.048|                           missing automatic undo never becomes a problem.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.049|
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.050|  Why these profiles : Four identical columns at 3, gaming included,
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.051|                       because the low-latency choice has no mechanism
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.052|                       unless a switch on your LAN really sends PAUSE
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.053|                       frames - and if none does, writing 0 buys nothing
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.054|                       and costs you the automatic undo. The server column
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.055|                       is the least ambiguous of the four: it does bulk
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.056|                       uploads, where a brief pause is cheaper than
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.057|                       retransmitting. Windows is SKIP because 3 is the
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.058|                       driver's value rather than a Windows one. Choose 0
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.059|                       only if you administer a managed switch and can see
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.060|                       it sending pause frames.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.061|
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.062|  Unverified      : Whether your LAN sends PAUSE frames at all cannot be
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.063|                    determined from a batch script. The only honest signal
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.064|                    is the adapter's own Pause Frames Received counter,
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.065|                    which the driver does not expose through any
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.066|                    scriptable interface - so OPTY deliberately makes no
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.067|                    claim about whether the change helped.
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.068|
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.069|  Target          : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*FlowControl" - 3 in the
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.070|                    Balanced branch and in :net_prof_thr, 0 in
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.071|                    :net_prof_lat (OPTY.bat ~2898, ~2903, ~2916). Excluded
::X|EN|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.072|                    from the :net_restore keyword filter.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.001|  Ce que c est    : Le controle de flux permet a un switch d envoyer une
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.002|                    trame PAUSE demandant a votre carte d arreter
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.003|                    d emettre un instant quand sa memoire tampon se
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.004|                    remplit. OPTY ecrit 3 pour les profils Equilibre et
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.005|                    Debit, et 0 pour le profil Faible latence.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.006|
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.007|  Effet reel      : Active, une trame PAUSE peut bloquer votre envoi
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.008|                    jusqu a environ 33,6 ms en gigabit. Desactive, le
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.009|                    switch jette ces paquets a la place et TCP les
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.010|                    retransmet.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.011|
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.012|  Gain            : Seulement si quelque chose sur votre reseau local
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.013|                    envoie reellement des trames PAUSE, ce que la plupart
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.014|                    des switchs et box grand public ne font jamais. Quand
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.015|                    c est le cas, le desactiver remplace un blocage
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.016|                    d envoi de quelques millisecondes par des paquets
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.017|                    perdus, ce qu un jeu supporte mieux qu un blocage. Si
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.018|                    rien n envoie de PAUSE, l effet mesure est exactement
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.019|                    nul, et le script le dit au lieu de s attribuer un
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.020|                    gain.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.021|
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.022|  Cout            : En saturation, vous echangez une breve pause contre de
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.023|                    vraies pertes de paquets, ce qui penalise les gros
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.024|                    envois et les sauvegardes. Et la restauration reseau
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.025|                    ne couvre pas *FlowControl : si vous ecrivez 0,
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.026|                    l annulation se fait a la main dans le Gestionnaire de
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.027|                    peripheriques.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.028|
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.029|  Defaut Windows  : Valeur fournie par le pilote, souvent 3 (Rx et Tx
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.030|                    actives) sur les cartes Intel. La vraie valeur de
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.031|                    votre carte est dans Ndi\Params\*FlowControl\default.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.032|
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.033|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.034|    0                    : Desactive. La carte n envoie ni n honore les
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.035|                           trames PAUSE 802.3x. Un switch dont la memoire
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.036|                           tampon se remplit jette vos paquets au lieu de
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.037|                           vous bloquer, et TCP les retransmet.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.038|    1                    : Tx Enabled. La carte envoie des trames PAUSE
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.039|                           quand son propre anneau de reception se
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.040|                           remplit, mais ignore celles qu elle recoit.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.041|                           C est l option chirurgicale si ce que vous
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.042|                           voulez est que votre envoi ne soit jamais
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.043|                           bloque - OPTY ne la propose pas, mais c est une
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.044|                           valeur valide du mot-cle.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.045|    2                    : Rx Enabled. La carte honore les trames PAUSE
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.046|                           recues mais n en envoie jamais.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.047|    3                    : Rx et Tx Enabled, la valeur livree habituelle
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.048|                           sur les cartes Intel. Une trame PAUSE recue
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.049|                           peut bloquer votre emission jusqu a environ
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.050|                           33,6 ms en gigabit.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.051|    SKIP                 : Le mot-cle n est pas ecrit ; il reste a
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.052|                           Ndi\Params\*FlowControl\default, et l absence
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.053|                           d annulation automatique ne devient jamais un
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.054|                           probleme.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.055|
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.056|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes identiques a 3, gaming compris,
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.057|                         parce que le choix Faible latence n a aucun
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.058|                         mecanisme tant qu un switch de votre reseau
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.059|                         n envoie pas reellement des trames PAUSE - et si
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.060|                         aucun ne le fait, ecrire 0 n apporte rien et vous
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.061|                         coute l annulation automatique. La colonne
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.062|                         serveur est la moins ambigue des quatre : elle
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.063|                         fait de gros envois, ou une breve pause coute
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.064|                         moins cher qu une retransmission. Windows est
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.065|                         SKIP parce que 3 est une valeur du pilote et non
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.066|                         de Windows. Ne choisissez 0 que si vous
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.067|                         administrez un switch manageable et que vous le
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.068|                         voyez envoyer des trames de pause.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.069|
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.070|  Non verifie (en)  : Whether your LAN sends PAUSE frames at all cannot be
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.071|                      determined from a batch script. The only honest
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.072|                      signal is the adapter s own Pause Frames Received
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.073|                      counter, which the driver does not expose through
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.074|                      any scriptable interface - so OPTY deliberately
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.075|                      makes no claim about whether the change helped.
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.076|
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.077|  Cible           : call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*FlowControl" - 3 in the
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.078|                    Balanced branch and in :net_prof_thr, 0 in
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.079|                    :net_prof_lat (OPTY.bat ~2898, ~2903, ~2916). Excluded
::X|FR|net.nic.flowcontrol.profile.080|                    from the :net_restore keyword filter.
::
:: ---- net.nti.profile.choice (preference) ----------------------------
::P|net.nti.profile.choice|10|0xffffffff|10|10|10|
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.001|NETWORK THROTTLING CAP BY PROFILE
::T|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.002|Keeps the audio-protecting network cap at its default of 10 for most profiles, but lifts it for the server profile so big transfers can use the full connection even while media plays, at the cost of a small audio-glitch risk.
::T|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.001|PLAFOND RESEAU AUDIO SELON LE PROFIL
::T|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.002|Garde le plafond reseau qui protege l audio a sa valeur d origine de 10 pour la plupart des profils, mais le leve pour le profil serveur afin que les gros transferts utilisent toute la connexion meme pendant une lecture, au prix d un petit risque de coupures audio.
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.001|  What it is      : An MMCSS setting that caps non-multimedia network
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.002|                    processing at roughly 10 packets per millisecond -
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.003|                    about 120 Mbit/s - WHILE multimedia is playing, so
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.004|                    audio threads are not starved by network interrupt
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.005|                    work. It does nothing when nothing is playing.
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.006|
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.007|  Actual effect   : The balanced and low-latency branches write 10, the
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.008|                    Windows default, rather than skipping the write. The
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.009|                    throughput branch writes 0xFFFFFFFF, lifting the cap
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.010|                    so a big download can use the whole link even while
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.011|                    audio plays.
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.012|
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.013|  Gain            : For 10: none over a stock machine, since it is the
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.014|                    default - the value exists to repair machines where a
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.015|                    guide wrote 0xFFFFFFFF. For 0xFFFFFFFF: full download
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.016|                    speed while music or video plays, genuinely visible on
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.017|                    a connection above about 120 Mbit/s and invisible
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.018|                    below it.
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.019|
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.020|  Cost            : For 0xFFFFFFFF: risk of audio crackle under heavy
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.021|                    network load, and you remove the protection MMCSS
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.022|                    exists for. That matters far more for a DAW or a
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.023|                    streamer than for background music.
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.024|
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.025|  Windows default : 10 (0x0000000a).
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.026|
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.027|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.028|    10 (0x0000000a)      : Shipped value. While an MMCSS multimedia stream
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.029|                           is registered on this machine, non-multimedia
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.030|                           network processing is capped at 10 packets per
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.031|                           millisecond - about 10000 packets per second,
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.032|                           roughly 120 Mbit/s with full-size frames. With
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.033|                           no audio or video playing, the cap does
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.034|                           nothing.
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.035|    4294967295 (0xFFFFFFFF) :
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.036|        The cap is lifted entirely, so a download can use the whole link
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.037|        even while audio plays. Network DPC work can then take time from
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.038|        the audio thread, which is the protection you are giving up.
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.039|    any other N          : The cap becomes N packets per millisecond.
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.040|                           Below 10 it is tighter than shipped, above 10
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.041|                           it is looser without being removed.
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.042|
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.043|  Why these profiles : Only the server column differs, and for a
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.044|                       mechanical reason rather than a taste. The cap
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.045|                       engages only while an MMCSS multimedia stream is
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.046|                       registered on THIS machine, and a box that hosts
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.047|                       torrents, Plex and game servers is serving media to
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.048|                       other machines rather than playing it locally - so
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.049|                       it rarely hits the cap at all, and on the occasions
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.050|                       it does, throughput is exactly what it exists for.
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.051|                       Gaming, office and laptop keep 10 because all three
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.052|                       play audio constantly and crackle under load is
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.053|                       precisely what the cap prevents. Windows keeps 10
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.054|                       because 10 is what ships. Honest test either way: a
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.055|                       large download with audio playing, before and
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.056|                       after.
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.057|
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.058|  Target          : call :regset "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.059|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile"
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.060|                    "NetworkThrottlingIndex" REG_DWORD <10 or 4294967295>
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.061|                    - 10 in the Balanced and Low latency branches,
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.062|                    4294967295 in :net_prof_thr (OPTY.bat ~2899, ~2911,
::X|EN|net.nti.profile.choice.063|                    ~2917)
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.001|  Ce que c est    : Un reglage MMCSS qui plafonne le traitement reseau non
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.002|                    multimedia a environ 10 paquets par milliseconde, soit
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.003|                    a peu pres 120 Mbit/s, PENDANT qu un flux multimedia
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.004|                    joue, pour que les threads audio ne soient pas
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.005|                    etouffes par le travail d interruption reseau. Sans
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.006|                    lecture, il ne fait rien.
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.007|
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.008|  Effet reel      : Les branches Equilibre et Faible latence ecrivent 10,
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.009|                    la valeur Windows, au lieu de sauter l ecriture. La
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.010|                    branche Debit ecrit 0xFFFFFFFF, ce qui leve le plafond
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.011|                    : un gros telechargement peut alors utiliser tout le
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.012|                    lien meme pendant une lecture audio.
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.013|
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.014|  Gain            : Pour 10 : aucun par rapport a une machine d origine,
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.015|                    puisque c est la valeur d usine - elle existe pour
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.016|                    reparer les PC ou un guide a mis 0xFFFFFFFF. Pour
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.017|                    0xFFFFFFFF : pleine vitesse de telechargement pendant
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.018|                    qu une musique ou une video joue, reellement visible
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.019|                    au-dela d environ 120 Mbit/s et invisible en dessous.
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.020|
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.021|  Cout            : Pour 0xFFFFFFFF : risque de craquements audio sous
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.022|                    forte charge reseau, et vous retirez la protection qui
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.023|                    justifie l existence de MMCSS. Cela pese bien plus
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.024|                    pour une station audio ou un streameur que pour une
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.025|                    musique de fond.
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.026|
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.027|  Defaut Windows  : 10 (0x0000000a).
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.028|
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.029|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.030|    10 (0x0000000a)      : Valeur d origine. Tant qu un flux multimedia
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.031|                           MMCSS est enregistre sur cette machine, le
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.032|                           traitement reseau non multimedia est plafonne a
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.033|                           10 paquets par milliseconde - environ 10000
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.034|                           paquets par seconde, a peu pres 120 Mbit/s avec
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.035|                           des trames pleines. Sans lecture audio ou
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.036|                           video, le plafond ne fait rien.
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.037|    4294967295 (0xFFFFFFFF) :
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.038|        Le plafond est totalement leve : un telechargement peut utiliser
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.039|        tout le lien meme pendant une lecture audio. Le travail reseau en
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.040|        DPC peut alors prendre du temps au thread audio - c est la
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.041|        protection a laquelle vous renoncez.
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.042|    any other N          : Le plafond passe a N paquets par milliseconde.
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.043|                           En dessous de 10 il est plus serre que
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.044|                           d origine, au-dessus il est plus lache sans
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.045|                           etre supprime.
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.046|
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.047|  Pourquoi ces profils : Seule la colonne serveur differe, et pour une
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.048|                         raison mecanique et non de gout. Le plafond ne
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.049|                         s active que tant qu un flux multimedia MMCSS est
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.050|                         enregistre sur CETTE machine : une machine qui
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.051|                         heberge torrents, Plex et serveurs de jeu diffuse
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.052|                         du media vers d autres postes plutot que d en
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.053|                         lire localement, elle atteint donc rarement le
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.054|                         plafond, et les rares fois ou elle le fait, le
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.055|                         debit est justement sa raison d etre. Gaming,
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.056|                         bureautique et portable gardent 10 parce que tous
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.057|                         trois jouent de l audio en permanence et que les
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.058|                         craquements sous charge sont exactement ce que le
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.059|                         plafond evite. Windows garde 10 parce que 10 est
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.060|                         ce qui est livre. Test honnete dans les deux sens
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.061|                         : un gros telechargement avec de l audio, avant
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.062|                         et apres.
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.063|
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.064|  Cible           : call :regset "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.065|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile"
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.066|                    "NetworkThrottlingIndex" REG_DWORD <10 or 4294967295>
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.067|                    - 10 in the Balanced and Low latency branches,
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.068|                    4294967295 in :net_prof_thr (OPTY.bat ~2899, ~2911,
::X|FR|net.nti.profile.choice.069|                    ~2917)
