:: ---- net.tcp.globals.assert.profile (repair) --------------------
::P|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile|APPLY|APPLY|APPLY|APPLY|APPLY|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.001|TCP SETTINGS RE-CONFIRMED AFTER A NETWORK PROFILE
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.002|Re-writes three standard Windows TCP settings after applying a network profile; no effect on an untouched PC, but a real throughput fix if an old tweak script had disabled receive-window autotuning.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.001|REGLAGES TCP REAFFIRMES APRES UN PROFIL RESEAU
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.002|Reecrit trois reglages TCP standards de Windows apres l application d un profil reseau ; aucun effet sur un PC intact, mais une vraie amelioration de debit si un ancien script avait desactive l ajustement automatique de la fenetre de reception.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.001|  What it is      : The same three machine-wide TCP settings, written
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.002|                    again at the end of applying a network profile so the
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.003|                    profile and the stack agree: receive-window
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.004|                    autotuning, Window Scaling Heuristics, and RSS at the
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.005|                    TCP layer.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.006|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.007|  Actual effect   : Three netsh calls, machine-wide and immediate, no
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.008|                    reboot. On a stock machine all three are no-ops - the
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.009|                    step's real value is undoing a previous tweak, most
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.010|                    often autotuninglevel=disabled.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.011|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.012|  Gain            : On a machine a previous tweak script mangled, putting
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.013|                    autotuning back to normal is a large and easily
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.014|                    measurable throughput fix on high-latency or high-
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.015|                    bandwidth paths. On an untouched machine all three
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.016|                    writes change nothing, and the honest answer is that
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.017|                    you will measure no difference.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.018|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.019|  Cost            : Practically none, because none of the three departs
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.020|                    from what Windows ships. If a specific site or VPN
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.021|                    turns flaky afterwards, netsh int tcp set heuristics
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.022|                    default is the single line to put back.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.023|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.024|  Windows default : autotuninglevel=normal, rss=enabled and Window Scaling
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.025|                    Heuristics disabled are all Windows defaults - WSH has
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.026|                    shipped disabled since Windows 8.1. netsh int tcp set
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.027|                    heuristics default hands the decision back to Windows.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.028|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.029|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.030|    autotuninglevel=normal :
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.031|        The receive window scales up to what the path needs. Shipped
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.032|        value, and the one optimizer guides most often replace.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.033|    autotuninglevel=disabled, highlyrestricted, restricted :
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.034|        Progressively smaller ceilings on the receive window. On a long or
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.035|        fast path each one is a throughput cut, invisible locally and
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.036|        severe over distance.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.037|    autotuninglevel=experimental :
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.038|        An extreme ceiling. Wastes non-paged pool with no benefit on any
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.039|        home path.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.040|    heuristics=disabled  : Windows stops second-guessing your autotuning
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.041|                           level from stored per-path history.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.042|    heuristics=enabled   : Windows may silently reduce the window scale
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.043|                           factor on paths it decides are problematic.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.044|    heuristics=default   : Hands the decision back to Windows built-in
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.045|                           policy - which is disabled, so the behaviour is
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.046|                           the same.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.047|    rss=enabled / disabled / default :
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.048|        Enabled spreads TCP receive processing over several cores,
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.049|        disabled pins it to one, default hands the choice to Windows,
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.050|        whose policy is enabled.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.051|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.052|  Why these profiles : Five identical columns. These are the shipped
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.053|                       values, so no usage profile has a reason to want
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.054|                       anything else, and the Windows column wants them
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.055|                       too because writing them is how a mangled machine
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.056|                       gets back to shipped. Earlier drafts of this card
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.057|                       claimed heuristics=disabled was a departure from
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.058|                       stock and built a gain and cost argument on it -
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.059|                       that was wrong, and nothing here rests on it any
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.060|                       more.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.061|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.062|  Unverified      : That Window Scaling Heuristics ships disabled rests on
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.063|                    documented Windows 8.1-and-later behaviour plus a live
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.064|                    Windows 11 25H2 reading, not on a Microsoft statement
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.065|                    for 25H2. netsh reports it separately from the global
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.066|                    parameters, which is why earlier versions of this card
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.067|                    guessed the opposite.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.068|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.069|  Target          : netsh int tcp set global autotuninglevel=normal ;
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.070|                    netsh int tcp set heuristics disabled ; netsh int tcp
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.071|                    set global rss=enabled - run at :net_prof_done, after
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.072|                    a network profile has been applied (OPTY.bat
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.073|                    ~2923-2925)
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.001|  Ce que c est    : Les memes trois reglages TCP valables pour toute la
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.002|                    machine, reecrits a la fin de l application d un
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.003|                    profil reseau pour que le profil et la pile soient
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.004|                    d accord : ajustement automatique de la fenetre de
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.005|                    reception, heuristiques de mise a l echelle, et RSS au
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.006|                    niveau TCP.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.007|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.008|  Effet reel      : Trois commandes netsh, valables pour toute la machine,
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.009|                    effet immediat, sans redemarrage. Sur une machine
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.010|                    d origine, les trois ne changent rien : la vraie
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.011|                    valeur de l etape est d annuler un tweak precedent, le
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.012|                    plus souvent autotuninglevel=disabled.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.013|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.014|  Gain            : Sur une machine massacree par un ancien script de
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.015|                    tweak, remettre l autotuning sur normal est un gain de
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.016|                    debit important et facilement mesurable sur les
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.017|                    chemins a forte latence ou a haut debit. Sur une
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.018|                    machine intacte, les trois ecritures ne changent rien,
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.019|                    et il faut le dire franchement : vous ne mesurerez
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.020|                    aucune difference.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.021|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.022|  Cout            : Quasiment aucun, puisque aucune des trois valeurs ne
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.023|                    s ecarte de ce que Windows livre. Si un site precis ou
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.024|                    un VPN devient capricieux ensuite, netsh int tcp set
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.025|                    heuristics default est la seule ligne a remettre.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.026|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.027|  Defaut Windows  : autotuninglevel=normal, rss=enabled et les
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.028|                    heuristiques de mise a l echelle desactivees sont
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.029|                    toutes des valeurs Windows d origine - les
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.030|                    heuristiques sont livrees desactivees depuis Windows
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.031|                    8.1. netsh int tcp set heuristics default rend la
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.032|                    decision a Windows.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.033|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.034|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.035|    autotuninglevel=normal :
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.036|        La fenetre de reception grandit jusqu a ce que le chemin exige.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.037|        Valeur d origine, et celle que les guides d optimisation
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.038|        remplacent le plus souvent.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.039|    autotuninglevel=disabled, highlyrestricted, restricted :
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.040|        Des plafonds de plus en plus bas sur la fenetre de reception. Sur
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.041|        un chemin long ou rapide, chacun est une coupe de debit, invisible
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.042|        en local et severe a distance.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.043|    autotuninglevel=experimental :
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.044|        Un plafond extreme. Gaspille du pool non pagine sans aucun
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.045|        benefice sur un chemin domestique.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.046|    heuristics=disabled  : Windows cesse de remettre en cause votre niveau
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.047|                           d autotuning a partir de son historique par
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.048|                           chemin.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.049|    heuristics=enabled   : Windows peut reduire en silence le facteur
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.050|                           d echelle sur les chemins qu il juge
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.051|                           problematiques.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.052|    heuristics=default   : Rend la decision a la politique interne de
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.053|                           Windows, qui est disabled : le comportement est
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.054|                           donc identique.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.055|    rss=enabled / disabled / default :
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.056|        Enabled repartit le traitement TCP en reception sur plusieurs
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.057|        coeurs, disabled le fixe sur un seul, default laisse le choix a
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.058|        Windows, dont la politique est enabled.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.059|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.060|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques. Ce sont les valeurs
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.061|                         livrees : aucun profil d usage n a de raison d en
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.062|                         vouloir d autres, et la colonne Windows les veut
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.063|                         aussi puisque les ecrire est precisement la
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.064|                         maniere de ramener une machine trafiquee a l etat
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.065|                         d origine. Des versions anterieures de cette
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.066|                         fiche affirmaient que heuristics=disabled etait
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.067|                         un ecart par rapport au standard et
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.068|                         construisaient tout un raisonnement dessus :
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.069|                         c etait faux, et plus rien ici n en depend.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.070|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.071|  Non verifie (en)  : That Window Scaling Heuristics ships disabled rests
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.072|                      on documented Windows 8.1-and-later behaviour plus a
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.073|                      live Windows 11 25H2 reading, not on a Microsoft
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.074|                      statement for 25H2. netsh reports it separately from
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.075|                      the global parameters, which is why earlier versions
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.076|                      of this card guessed the opposite.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.077|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.078|  Cible           : netsh int tcp set global autotuninglevel=normal ;
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.079|                    netsh int tcp set heuristics disabled ; netsh int tcp
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.080|                    set global rss=enabled - run at :net_prof_done, after
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.081|                    a network profile has been applied (OPTY.bat
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.assert.profile.082|                    ~2923-2925)
::
:: ---- net.nic.restart.profile (preference) ---------------------------
::P|net.nic.restart.profile|RESTART|SKIP|RESTART|RESTART|SKIP|
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.001|RESTART THE NETWORK ADAPTER NOW
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.002|Applies the new network settings immediately instead of waiting for the next reboot, at the cost of a 2 to 4 second link drop that can interrupt downloads, VPN, remote sessions or online games.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.001|REDEMARRER LA CARTE RESEAU MAINTENANT
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.002|Applique les nouveaux reglages reseau tout de suite au lieu d attendre le prochain demarrage, au prix d une coupure de 2 a 4 secondes qui peut interrompre un telechargement, un VPN, une session distante ou une partie en ligne.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.001|  What it is      : pnputil /restart-device on the adapter, so NDIS re-
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.002|                    reads all the keywords just written instead of waiting
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.003|                    for the next boot.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.004|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.005|  Actual effect   : The adapter goes down and comes back up in roughly 2
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.006|                    to 4 seconds, and the new settings become active
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.007|                    immediately.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.008|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.009|  Gain            : You do not have to reboot to test the profile. That is
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.010|                    the entire benefit - the settings themselves are
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.011|                    identical either way.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.012|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.013|  Cost            : The link drops. That kills an in-flight download
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.014|                    without resume, drops a VPN, ends an RDP or SSH
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.015|                    session, and disconnects you from an online game or a
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.016|                    call. A Wi-Fi adapter also has to re-associate.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.017|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.018|  Windows default : Not a setting - it is an action.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.019|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.020|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.021|    RESTART              : pnputil stops and restarts the adapter device.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.022|                           NDIS re-reads every keyword during
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.023|                           initialisation, so the settings become live.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.024|                           The link is down for roughly 2 to 4 seconds.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.025|    SKIP                 : Nothing happens now. The same keywords are read
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.026|                           at the next boot, with exactly the same result
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.027|                           - the settings are identical either way.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.028|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.029|  Why these profiles : The server column is SKIP, and it is the one that
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.030|                       carries the argument: that machine is usually
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.031|                       administered over the network, is seeding or
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.032|                       serving while you work on it, and a 2 to 4 second
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.033|                       blackout costs real sessions - it can wait for a
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.034|                       scheduled reboot. Gaming, office and laptop are
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.035|                       machines you are sitting in front of, so restarting
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.036|                       now is free and saves you a reboot. The Windows
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.037|                       column is SKIP because Windows never restarts an
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.038|                       adapter on its own.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.039|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.040|  Target          : the restart prompt at the end of :net_restore, which
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.profile.041|                    calls :nicrestart -> pnputil /restart-device "%NPNP%"
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.001|  Ce que c est    : pnputil /restart-device sur la carte, pour que NDIS
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.002|                    relise tous les mots-cles qui viennent d etre ecrits
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.003|                    au lieu d attendre le prochain demarrage.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.004|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.005|  Effet reel      : La carte tombe et remonte en 2 a 4 secondes environ,
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.006|                    et les nouveaux reglages deviennent actifs tout de
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.007|                    suite.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.008|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.009|  Gain            : Pas besoin de redemarrer pour tester le profil. C est
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.010|                    tout l interet : les reglages sont identiques dans les
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.011|                    deux cas.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.012|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.013|  Cout            : Le lien tombe. Cela tue un telechargement en cours
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.014|                    sans reprise, coupe un VPN, met fin a une session RDP
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.015|                    ou SSH, et vous deconnecte d un jeu en ligne ou d un
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.016|                    appel. Une carte Wi-Fi doit en plus se reassocier.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.017|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.018|  Defaut Windows  : Ce n est pas un reglage, c est une action.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.019|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.020|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.021|    RESTART              : pnputil arrete et relance le peripherique
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.022|                           reseau. NDIS relit tous les mots-cles pendant
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.023|                           l initialisation, les reglages deviennent donc
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.024|                           actifs. Le lien tombe environ 2 a 4 secondes.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.025|    SKIP                 : Rien ne se passe maintenant. Les memes mots-
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.026|                           cles sont lus au prochain demarrage, avec
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.027|                           exactement le meme resultat : les reglages sont
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.028|                           identiques dans les deux cas.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.029|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.030|  Pourquoi ces profils : La colonne serveur est a SKIP, et c est elle qui
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.031|                         porte le raisonnement : cette machine est
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.032|                         generalement administree a distance, elle partage
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.033|                         ou sert pendant que vous travaillez dessus, et un
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.034|                         trou de 2 a 4 secondes coute de vraies sessions -
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.035|                         elle peut attendre un redemarrage planifie.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.036|                         Gaming, bureautique et portable sont des machines
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.037|                         devant lesquelles vous etes assis : redemarrer la
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.038|                         carte est gratuit et vous evite un reboot. La
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.039|                         colonne Windows est a SKIP parce que Windows ne
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.040|                         redemarre jamais une carte de lui-meme.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.041|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.042|  Cible           : le prompt de redemarrage a la fin de :net_restore, qui
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.profile.043|                    appelle :nicrestart -> pnputil /restart-device "%NPNP%"
::
:: ---- net.nic.restore.profile (repair) ---------------------------
::P|net.nic.restore.profile|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|APPLY|
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.001|RESTORE THE NETWORK CARD'S OWN FACTORY DEFAULTS
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.002|Undoes the network profile by reading each setting's true default straight from your own driver, giving a clean way back to factory NIC behaviour without reinstalling it; you simply lose whatever profile you had applied.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.001|RETABLIR LES VALEURS D USINE DE LA CARTE RESEAU
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.002|Annule le profil reseau en relisant la vraie valeur par defaut de chaque reglage directement dans votre pilote, ce qui offre un retour propre au comportement d usine sans reinstaller le pilote ; vous perdez simplement le profil applique.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.001|  What it is      : The undo for the network profile. For each of the 14
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.002|                    keywords OPTY writes, it reads
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.003|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default from the driver itself
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.004|                    and writes that back - never a hardcoded number.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.005|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.006|  Actual effect   : Interrupt moderation, ITR, LSO, all checksum offloads,
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.007|                    RSS, queue count, jumbo and both buffer sizes go back
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.008|                    to exactly what your driver shipped with. The TCP
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.009|                    globals are handed back to Windows and an adapter
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.010|                    restart is offered.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.011|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.012|  Gain            : A clean way back to factory NIC behaviour without
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.013|                    reinstalling the driver - useful when you are
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.014|                    debugging a network problem and want the adapter out
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.015|                    of the equation. It is correct on any chipset by
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.016|                    construction, because it reads the values from your
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.017|                    own machine.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.018|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.019|  Cost            : You lose the profile you applied. Note what it
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.020|                    deliberately does NOT touch: Wake-on-LAN, Energy
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.021|                    Efficient Ethernet and ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown are
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.022|                    excluded from the filter, because an earlier version
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.023|                    restored those too and silently switched back on
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.024|                    things people had turned off on purpose. The side
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.025|                    effect is that if OPTY turned EEE or Flow Control off,
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.026|                    this leaves them off.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.027|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.028|  Windows default : By construction, this IS the per-driver default.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.029|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.030|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.031|    APPLY                : All 14 keywords go back to the value your own
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.032|                           driver publishes as its default: interrupt
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.033|                           moderation and ITR, both LSO keywords, all five
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.034|                           checksum offloads, RSS and the queue count,
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.035|                           jumbo frames and both buffer sizes.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.036|    SKIP                 : The profile you applied stays exactly as it is.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.037|    no default published : There is no third choice to make: if the driver
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.038|                           publishes no default for a keyword, the helper
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.039|                           leaves that keyword alone rather than inventing
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.040|                           a number.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.041|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.042|  Why these profiles : The four usage columns are SKIP for an obvious
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.043|                       reason - running the undo during a setup pass just
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.044|                       cancels what you came to do. The Windows column is
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.045|                       APPLY, and this is the only step in the whole
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.046|                       network section that can honestly claim to produce
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.047|                       the shipped state, because it reads that state
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.048|                       rather than assuming it. Say yes outside a setup
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.049|                       run: when troubleshooting, when handing the PC
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.050|                       over, or when the profile did not help.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.051|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.052|  Target          : :net_restore - reg query "%NICKEY%\Ndi\Params"
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.053|                    filtered by findstr /i /e to the 14 keywords
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.054|                    :net_apply writes, then call :nicdefault for each,
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.055|                    which reads Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default and writes it
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.profile.056|                    back as REG_SZ (OPTY.bat ~2985-2995)
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.001|  Ce que c est    : L annulation du profil reseau. Pour chacun des 14
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.002|                    mots-cles qu OPTY ecrit, il lit Ndi\Params\<mot-
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.003|                    cle>\default dans le pilote lui-meme et reecrit cette
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.004|                    valeur - jamais un chiffre en dur.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.005|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.006|  Effet reel      : Moderation d interruptions, ITR, LSO, toutes les
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.007|                    sommes de controle, RSS, nombre de files, jumbo et les
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.008|                    deux tailles de tampons reviennent exactement a ce que
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.009|                    livrait votre pilote. Les reglages TCP globaux sont
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.010|                    rendus a Windows et un redemarrage de la carte est
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.011|                    propose.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.012|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.013|  Gain            : Un retour propre au comportement d usine de la carte
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.014|                    sans reinstaller le pilote - pratique quand vous
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.015|                    deboguez un souci reseau et voulez sortir la carte de
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.016|                    l equation. C est correct sur n importe quel chipset
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.017|                    par construction, puisque les valeurs sont lues sur
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.018|                    votre propre machine.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.019|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.020|  Cout            : Vous perdez le profil applique. A noter, ce qu il ne
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.021|                    touche volontairement PAS : Wake-on-LAN, l Ethernet a
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.022|                    economie d energie et ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown sont
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.023|                    exclus du filtre, parce qu une version precedente les
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.024|                    retablissait aussi et rallumait en douce des choses
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.025|                    coupees expres. Effet de bord : si OPTY a desactive
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.026|                    l EEE ou le controle de flux, ils restent desactives.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.027|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.028|  Defaut Windows  : Par construction, c est la valeur d usine propre a
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.029|                    chaque pilote.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.030|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.031|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.032|    APPLY                : Les 14 mots-cles reviennent a la valeur que
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.033|                           votre propre pilote publie comme defaut :
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.034|                           moderation d interruptions et ITR, les deux
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.035|                           mots-cles LSO, les cinq sommes de controle, le
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.036|                           RSS et le nombre de files, les trames jumbo et
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.037|                           les deux tailles de tampons.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.038|    SKIP                 : Le profil que vous avez applique reste
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.039|                           exactement en place.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.040|    no default published : Il n y a pas de troisieme choix a faire : si le
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.041|                           pilote ne publie aucun defaut pour un mot-cle,
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.042|                           la fonction laisse ce mot-cle tranquille au
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.043|                           lieu d inventer un nombre.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.044|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.045|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les quatre colonnes d usage sont a SKIP pour une
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.046|                         raison evidente : lancer l annulation pendant une
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.047|                         passe de configuration ne fait qu annuler ce que
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.048|                         vous etes venu faire. La colonne Windows est a
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.049|                         APPLY, et c est la seule etape de toute la
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.050|                         section reseau qui puisse honnetement pretendre
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.051|                         produire l etat livre, puisqu elle le lit au lieu
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.052|                         de le supposer. Dites oui en dehors d une
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.053|                         configuration : en depannage, avant de ceder le
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.054|                         PC, ou si le profil n a rien apporte.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.055|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.056|  Cible           : :net_restore - reg query "%NICKEY%\Ndi\Params"
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.057|                    filtered by findstr /i /e to the 14 keywords
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.058|                    :net_apply writes, then call :nicdefault for each,
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.059|                    which reads Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default and writes it
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.profile.060|                    back as REG_SZ (OPTY.bat ~2985-2995)
::
:: ---- net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword (repair) -------------------
::P|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|APPLY|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.001|RESTORE THE THREE TCP GLOBALS
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.002|Puts the three network auto-tuning settings back the way Windows ships them - mostly a formality since two of them already behave that way, but the right move if a site, VPN or proxy turned flaky after the original tweak.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.001|RETABLIR LES TROIS REGLAGES TCP GLOBAUX
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.002|Remet les trois reglages d auto-tuning reseau comme a la sortie de Windows - surtout une formalite car deux d entre eux se comportent deja ainsi, mais le bon geste si un site, un VPN ou un proxy est devenu capricieux apres le reglage d origine.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.001|  What it is      : The undo for the TCP settings, run at the end of the
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.002|                    NIC restore: autotuninglevel=normal,
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.003|                    heuristics=default, rss=default.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.004|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.005|  Actual effect   : Three netsh calls. Two of them stop asserting a value
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.006|                    at all and let Windows pick; the third pins autotuning
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.007|                    to the level Windows would have picked anyway.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.008|                    Machine-wide and immediate, no reboot.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.009|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.010|  Gain            : The right move if a particular site, VPN or corporate
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.011|                    proxy turned flaky after the TCP assert step. Be
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.012|                    honest about the size of it: since Window Scaling
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.013|                    Heuristics ships disabled, default and disabled land
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.014|                    on the same behaviour, so for that line this is a
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.015|                    formality rather than a fix.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.016|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.017|  Cost            : Nothing is lost. If you had deliberately chosen a non-
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.018|                    default autotuning level to work around a broken
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.019|                    router, you would set it again by hand.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.020|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.021|  Windows default : autotuninglevel=normal is the shipped level, so the
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.022|                    literal write is equivalent. For heuristics and rss
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.023|                    the keyword default is correct by construction.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.024|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.025|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.026|    autotuninglevel=normal :
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.027|        Written as a literal rather than as the keyword default, because
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.028|        normal IS the shipped level - the two are equivalent here.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.029|    heuristics=default   : Stops asserting a value and hands the decision
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.030|                           to Windows built-in policy, which is disabled -
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.031|                           so the observable behaviour is identical to
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.032|                           heuristics=disabled. For this one line the
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.033|                           restore is a formality, not a reversal.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.034|    rss=default          : Same idea for RSS, and the Windows policy is
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.035|                           enabled - so this too lands where the assert
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.036|                           step left it.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.037|    SKIP                 : The three globals stay wherever the assert step
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.038|                           left them, which on a stock machine is the same
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.039|                           place.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.040|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.041|  Why these profiles : Four SKIP columns because this is the undo half of
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.042|                       the assert step - running it during a setup pass
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.043|                       just cancels it. The Windows column is APPLY:
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.044|                       handing the decision back with the keyword default
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.045|                       is the only form of restore that stays correct even
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.046|                       if a future Windows release changes its mind about
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.047|                       what the default is.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.048|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.049|  Target          : netsh int tcp set global autotuninglevel=normal ;
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.050|                    netsh int tcp set heuristics default ; netsh int tcp
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.051|                    set global rss=default - the three lines at the end of
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.052|                    :net_restore (OPTY.bat ~2999-3001)
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.001| Ce que c est : L annulation des reglages TCP, executee a la fin de la
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.002|                    restauration de la carte : autotuninglevel=normal,
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.003|                    heuristics=default, rss=default.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.004|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.005|  Effet reel      : Trois commandes netsh. Deux cessent completement
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.006| d imposer une valeur et laissent Windows choisir, la
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.007| troisieme fixe l autotuning sur le niveau que Windows
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.008|                    aurait choisi de toute facon. Effet immediat sur toute
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.009|                    la machine, sans redemarrage.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.010|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.011|  Gain            : Le bon geste si un site precis, un VPN ou un proxy
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.012| d entreprise est devenu capricieux apres l etape
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.013| d application TCP. Soyons honnetes sur l ampleur :
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.014| puisque les heuristiques de mise a l echelle sont
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.015|                    livrees desactivees, default et disabled aboutissent
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.016| au meme comportement - pour cette ligne, c est une
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.017| formalite plutot qu un correctif.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.018|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.019| Cout : Rien n est perdu. Si vous aviez choisi volontairement
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.020| un autre niveau d autotuning pour contourner un
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.021|                    routeur capricieux, il faudra le remettre a la main.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.022|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.023|  Defaut Windows  : autotuninglevel=normal est bien le niveau livre,
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.024| l ecriture litterale est donc equivalente. Pour les
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.025|                    heuristiques et le RSS, le mot-cle default est correct
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.026|                    par construction.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.027|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.028|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.029|    autotuninglevel=normal :
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.030| Ecrit comme valeur litterale plutot qu avec le mot-cle default,
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.031|        parce que normal EST le niveau livre : les deux sont equivalents
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.032|        ici.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.033| heuristics=default : Cesse d imposer une valeur et rend la decision
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.034|                           a la politique interne de Windows, qui est
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.035|                           disabled : le comportement observable est
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.036|                           identique a heuristics=disabled. Pour cette
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.037|                           ligne, la restauration est une formalite et non
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.restore.keyword.038|                           un retour en arriere.
