:: ---- net.tcp.globals.handback (repair) --------------------------
::P|net.tcp.globals.handback|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|APPLY|
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.001|HAND THE TCP SETTINGS BACK TO WINDOWS
::T|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.002|Returns network auto-tuning to Windows own factory behavior without you needing to know the exact values - handy before troubleshooting a network issue, so you are debugging stock Windows rather than a leftover tweak.
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.001|RENDRE LES REGLAGES TCP A WINDOWS
::T|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.002|Remet l auto-tuning reseau au comportement d origine de Windows sans que vous ayez besoin de connaitre les valeurs exactes - pratique avant de diagnostiquer un probleme reseau, pour deboguer Windows tel quel plutot qu un reglage laisse par un tweak precedent.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.001|  What it is      : The undo side of the TCP assert. Heuristics and global
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.002|                    RSS are set to the keyword default, meaning Windows
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.003|                    decides; autotuning is written as the literal value
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.004|                    normal rather than as default.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.005|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.006|  Actual effect   : Three netsh calls. Two of them stop asserting a value
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.007|                    at all and let Windows pick, the third pins autotuning
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.008|                    to the level Windows would have picked anyway.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.009|                    Immediate, no reboot.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.010|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.011|  Gain            : Puts the machine back to shipped TCP behaviour without
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.012|                    you needing to know what the shipped values are.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.013|                    Useful before troubleshooting anything network-
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.014|                    related, so you are debugging Windows rather than
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.015|                    debugging a previous tweak.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.016|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.017|  Cost            : Nothing is lost. If you had deliberately chosen a non-
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.018|                    default autotuning level to work around a broken
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.019|                    router, you would set it again.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.020|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.021|  Windows default : autotuninglevel=normal is the shipped level, so the
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.022|                    literal write is equivalent here. For heuristics and
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.023|                    rss the keyword default is correct by construction.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.024|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.025|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.026|    the keyword default  : netsh stops asserting a value for that
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.027|                           parameter and lets the Windows built-in policy
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.028|                           decide. It stays correct even if a future
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.029|                           Windows release changes what the default is,
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.030|                           because no number is baked in.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.031|    a literal value such as normal or enabled :
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.032|        netsh pins the parameter to that exact setting. Correct today if
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.033|        it matches what Windows ships, and silently wrong the day it does
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.034|        not.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.035|    autotuninglevel written as normal rather than default :
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.036|        A deliberate asymmetry: normal is the shipped level and writing it
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.037|        literally makes the log readable, since default would print no
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.038|        value for the reader to check.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.039|    SKIP                 : The three globals keep whatever the assert step
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.040|                           wrote.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.041|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.042|  Why these profiles : Four SKIP columns, because handing the globals back
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.043|                       in the middle of a setup run just cancels the
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.044|                       assert step you ran a moment earlier. The Windows
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.045|                       column is APPLY, and it is worth understanding why
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.046|                       this is the better shaped restore: two of the three
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.047|                       lines assert nothing at all, so they cannot become
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.048|                       wrong. Run it before reporting a network problem to
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.049|                       an ISP or a support desk - they will ask whether
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.050|                       the stack is stock.
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.051|
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.052|  Target          : the same three lines as the NIC restore path: netsh
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.053|                    int tcp set global autotuninglevel=normal ; netsh int
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.054|                    tcp set heuristics default ; netsh int tcp set global
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.055|                    rss=default (OPTY.bat ~2999-3001). This card explains
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.056|                    why one of the three is written as a literal and the
::X|EN|net.tcp.globals.handback.057|                    other two as the keyword default.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.001| Ce que c est : Le pendant annulation de l application TCP. Les
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.002|                    heuristiques et le RSS global passent sur le mot-cle
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.003| default, c est-a-dire que Windows decide ;
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.004| l autotuning, lui, est ecrit avec la valeur litterale
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.005|                    normal et non default.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.006|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.007|  Effet reel      : Trois commandes netsh. Deux cessent completement
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.008| d imposer une valeur et laissent Windows choisir, la
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.009| troisieme fixe l autotuning sur le niveau que Windows
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.010|                    aurait choisi de toute facon. Effet immediat, sans
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.011|                    redemarrage.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.012|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.013| Gain : Remet la machine dans le comportement TCP d origine
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.014|                    sans que vous ayez besoin de connaitre ces valeurs.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.015|                    Pratique avant de diagnostiquer quoi que ce soit de
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.016|                    reseau : vous deboguez Windows et non un tweak passe.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.017|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.018| Cout : Rien n est perdu. Si vous aviez volontairement choisi
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.019| un autre niveau d autotuning pour contourner un
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.020|                    routeur capricieux, il faudra le remettre.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.021|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.022|  Defaut Windows  : autotuninglevel=normal est bien le niveau livre,
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.023| l ecriture litterale est donc equivalente ici. Pour
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.024|                    les heuristiques et le RSS, le mot-cle default est
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.025|                    correct par construction.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.026|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.027|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.028| the keyword default : netsh cesse d imposer une valeur pour ce
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.029|                           parametre et laisse decider la politique
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.030|                           interne de Windows. Cela reste juste meme si
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.031|                           une future version de Windows change son
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.032| defaut, puisque aucun nombre n est fige.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.033|    a literal value such as normal or enabled :
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.034|        netsh fixe le parametre exactement sur ce reglage. Correct
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.035| aujourd hui s il correspond a ce que Windows livre, et
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.036| silencieusement faux le jour ou ce n est plus le cas.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.037|    autotuninglevel written as normal rather than default :
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.038| Une asymetrie assumee : normal est le niveau livre, et l ecrire
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.039| litteralement rend le journal lisible, la ou default n afficherait
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.040|        aucune valeur a verifier.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.041|    SKIP                 : Les trois reglages globaux gardent ce que
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.042| l etape d application a ecrit.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.043|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.044|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes a SKIP, parce que rendre les
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.045| reglages globaux au milieu d une passe de
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.046| configuration ne fait qu annuler l etape
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.047| d application lancee quelques instants plus tot.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.048|                         La colonne Windows est a APPLY, et il vaut la
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.049|                         peine de comprendre pourquoi cette forme de
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.050|                         restauration est la meilleure : deux des trois
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.051| lignes n affirment plus rien du tout, elles ne
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.052|                         peuvent donc pas devenir fausses. Lancez-la avant
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.053|                         de signaler un probleme reseau a votre FAI ou a
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.054|                         un support : ils vous demanderont si la pile est
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.055| d origine.
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.056|
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.057|  Cible           : the same three lines as the NIC restore path: netsh
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.058|                    int tcp set global autotuninglevel=normal ; netsh int
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.059|                    tcp set heuristics default ; netsh int tcp set global
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.060|                    rss=default (OPTY.bat ~2999-3001). This card explains
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.061|                    why one of the three is written as a literal and the
::X|FR|net.tcp.globals.handback.062|                    other two as the keyword default.
::
:: ---- net.nic.offloads.explain (repair) --------------------------
::P|net.nic.offloads.explain|1/3|1/3|1/3|1/3|SKIP|
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.001|RE-ENABLE NETWORK CARD HARDWARE OFFLOADS
::T|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.002|Lets the network card handle its own checksums and packet splitting instead of the CPU - this is the setting most tweak guides tell you to turn off, but doing that only adds CPU work for no real benefit on modern hardware.
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.001|REACTIVER LES DECHARGEMENTS MATERIELS DE LA CARTE RESEAU
::T|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.002|Laisse la carte reseau gerer elle-meme ses sommes de controle et le decoupage des paquets plutot que le CPU - c est le reglage que la plupart des guides de bidouille demandent de couper, mais le couper ne fait qu ajouter du travail au CPU sans benefice reel sur du materiel recent.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.001|  What it is      : Seven driver keywords treated as one step: Large Send
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.002|                    Offload v2 for IPv4 and IPv6, plus the TCP, UDP and IP
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.003|                    checksum offloads. They move segmentation and checksum
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.004|                    work off your CPU and into the network chip.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.005|
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.006|  Actual effect   : Writes LSOv2 = 1 and every checksum offload = 3. This
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.007|                    deliberately reverses the standard optimizer-blog
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.008|                    advice to turn offloads off.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.009|
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.010|  Gain            : On a fresh machine, usually none - these ship enabled,
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.011|                    so the write confirms rather than changes anything. On
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.012|                    a machine where a previous tweak disabled them, you
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.013|                    get back the work the card was supposed to be doing,
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.014|                    which shows up as lower CPU during large transfers.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.015|
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.016|  Cost            : A genuinely buggy NIC driver can produce bad checksums
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.017|                    with offload on, which looks like connections that
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.018|                    stall or fail. That was common a decade ago and is
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.019|                    rare now, and the network Restore puts them back if
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.020|                    you turn out to have such a driver.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.021|
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.022|  Windows default : Driver-supplied. On practically every current NIC
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.023|                    driver LSOv2 ships Enabled and the checksum offloads
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.024|                    ship at 3, so this normally re-asserts the factory
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.025|                    value.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.026|
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.027|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.028|    LSOv2 = 1            : The chip performs TCP segmentation. One large
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.029|                           buffer crosses the stack instead of dozens of
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.030|                           MTU-sized packets, so CPU per transferred byte
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.031|                           drops on bulk sends.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.032|    LSOv2 = 0            : The CPU does the segmentation. Identical bytes
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.033|                           on the wire, more CPU to produce them - which
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.034|                           is what the tweak guides ask for.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.035|    checksum offload = 3 : Rx and Tx Enabled: the chip computes checksums
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.036|                           on send and verifies them on receive. The
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.037|                           shipped value on practically every current
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.038|                           driver.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.039|    checksum offload = 2 or 1 :
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.040|        Only one direction is offloaded; the other stays on the CPU.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.041|        Rarely a deliberate choice - usually the residue of a half-applied
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.042|        tweak.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.043|    checksum offload = 0 : Both directions on the CPU. A legitimate
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.044|                           diagnostic step when chasing packet corruption
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.045|                           from a suspect driver, and a poor permanent
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.046|                           setting.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.047|    SKIP                 : Nothing is written; the seven keywords keep the
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.048|                           driver's own values.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.049|
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.050|  Why these profiles : Four identical columns, and the reason is worth
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.051|                       stating plainly: the claim that offloads hurt
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.052|                       gaming does not survive the fact that game traffic
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.053|                       is small UDP datagrams which never go through LSO
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.054|                       at all. There is no latency being traded, so gaming
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.055|                       and office get the same answer. Windows is SKIP
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.056|                       because 1 and 3 belong to the driver, not to
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.057|                       Windows, and only the restore step reads the real
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.058|                       values. Say no only if you have a documented driver
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.059|                       bug.
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.060|
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.061|  Target          : the same seven :nicset calls as the offloads step -
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.062|                    *LsoV2IPv4, *LsoV2IPv6 = 1 and
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.063|                    *TCPChecksumOffloadIPv4/IPv6,
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.064|                    *UDPChecksumOffloadIPv4/IPv6, *IPChecksumOffloadIPv4 =
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.065|                    3, REG_SZ in the adapter class key (OPTY.bat
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.066|                    ~2849-2856). This card is the why, and reverses the
::X|EN|net.nic.offloads.explain.067|                    standard tweak-blog advice.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.001|  Ce que c est    : Sept mots-cles du pilote traites comme une seule etape
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.002|                    : Large Send Offload v2 en IPv4 et IPv6, plus les
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.003|                    sommes de controle TCP, UDP et IP. Ils deplacent le
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.004|                    decoupage des segments et le calcul des sommes de
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.005|                    controle du CPU vers la puce reseau.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.006|
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.007|  Effet reel      : Ecrit LSOv2 = 1 et toutes les sommes de controle = 3.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.008| C est volontairement l inverse du conseil classique
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.009| des blogs d optimisation, qui veut qu on les
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.010|                    desactive.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.011|
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.012|  Gain            : Sur une machine neuve, en general aucun : ils sont
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.013| livres actifs, l ecriture confirme donc au lieu de
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.014|                    changer quoi que ce soit. Sur une machine ou un tweak
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.015|                    precedent les avait coupes, vous recuperez le travail
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.016|                    que la carte etait censee faire, ce qui se voit a un
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.017|                    CPU plus bas pendant les gros transferts.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.018|
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.019|  Cout            : Un pilote reellement bogue peut produire de mauvaises
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.020|                    sommes de controle avec le dechargement actif, ce qui
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.021| donne des connexions qui bloquent ou echouent. C etait
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.022| courant il y a dix ans, c est rare aujourd hui, et la
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.023|                    restauration reseau les remet en arriere si vous
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.024|                    tombez sur un tel pilote.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.025|
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.026|  Defaut Windows  : Valeur fournie par le pilote. Sur pratiquement tous
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.027|                    les pilotes actuels, LSOv2 est livre actif et les
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.028|                    sommes de controle a 3 : on reaffirme donc en general
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.029| la valeur d usine.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.030|
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.031|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.032|    LSOv2 = 1            : La puce assure la segmentation TCP. Un gros
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.033|                           tampon traverse la pile au lieu de dizaines de
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.034|                           paquets a la taille du MTU : le CPU par octet
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.035|                           transfere baisse sur les gros envois.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.036|    LSOv2 = 0            : Le CPU fait la segmentation. Les memes octets
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.037|                           sur le cable, plus de CPU pour les produire -
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.038| c est ce que demandent les guides de bidouille.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.039|    checksum offload = 3 : Rx et Tx Enabled : la puce calcule les sommes
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.040| de controle a l emission et les verifie a la
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.041|                           reception. Valeur livree sur pratiquement tous
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.042|                           les pilotes actuels.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.043|    checksum offload = 2 or 1 :
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.044| Un seul sens est decharge, l autre reste sur le CPU. Rarement un
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.045| choix delibere : le plus souvent le residu d un tweak applique a
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.046|        moitie.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.047|    checksum offload = 0 : Les deux sens sur le CPU. Etape de diagnostic
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.048|                           legitime quand on traque une corruption de
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.049|                           paquets due a un pilote suspect, mauvais
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.050|                           reglage permanent.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.051| SKIP : Rien n est ecrit ; les sept mots-cles gardent
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.052|                           les valeurs propres du pilote.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.053|
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.054|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes identiques, et la raison merite
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.055| d etre dite clairement : l idee que les
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.056|                         dechargements nuisent au jeu ne resiste pas au
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.057| fait que le trafic d un jeu, ce sont de petits
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.058|                         datagrammes UDP qui ne passent jamais par le LSO.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.059| Aucune latence n est echangee : gaming et
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.060|                         bureautique recoivent donc la meme reponse.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.061|                         Windows est a SKIP parce que 1 et 3 appartiennent
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.062| au pilote et non a Windows, et que seule l etape
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.063|                         de restauration lit les vraies valeurs. Dites non
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.064|                         uniquement si vous avez un bug de pilote
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.065|                         documente.
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.066|
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.067|  Cible           : the same seven :nicset calls as the offloads step -
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.068|                    *LsoV2IPv4, *LsoV2IPv6 = 1 and
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.069|                    *TCPChecksumOffloadIPv4/IPv6,
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.070|                    *UDPChecksumOffloadIPv4/IPv6, *IPChecksumOffloadIPv4 =
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.071|                    3, REG_SZ in the adapter class key (OPTY.bat
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.072|                    ~2849-2856). This card is the why, and reverses the
::X|FR|net.nic.offloads.explain.073|                    standard tweak-blog advice.
::
:: ---- net.nic.jumbo.explain (repair) -----------------------------
::P|net.nic.jumbo.explain|1514|1514|1514|1514|SKIP|
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.001|KEEP STANDARD NETWORK FRAME SIZE
::T|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.002|Turns off oversized jumbo frames, which only work if every device on your network - switch, router, the other machine - is configured to match; fixes stalled transfers caused by a mismatch, but undoes a deliberately built jumbo-frame network if you have one.
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.001|GARDER LA TAILLE DE TRAME RESEAU STANDARD
::T|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.002|Desactive les trames jumbo surdimensionnees, qui ne servent que si tous les equipements du reseau - switch, routeur, autre machine - sont regles pareil ; corrige des transferts qui bloquent a cause d un reglage non assorti, mais annule un reseau jumbo construit volontairement si vous en avez un.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.001|  What it is      : Jumbo frames let a NIC send Ethernet frames far larger
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.002|                    than the standard 1514 bytes. They only help when
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.003|                    every device along the path - switch, router, the
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.004|                    other machine - is configured for them too.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.005|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.006|  Actual effect   : Writes *JumboPacket=1514, the standard frame size,
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.007|                    meaning jumbo frames off. It takes effect at the next
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.008|                    adapter restart or reboot.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.009|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.010|  Gain            : On a machine where jumbo frames were turned on without
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.011|                    a matching switch and NAS, this fixes real symptoms:
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.012|                    large transfers that stall, or that work on the LAN
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.013|                    but not to the internet. On a machine already at 1514,
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.014|                    nothing changes and there is honestly no gain.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.015|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.016|  Cost            : If you have a deliberately built jumbo-frame LAN - a
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.017|                    10G NAS, an iSCSI or virtualization setup where every
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.018|                    switch port is configured for a 9000-byte MTU - this
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.019|                    undoes it and you lose the throughput that setup
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.020|                    exists for.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.021|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.022|  Windows default : 1514 (jumbo disabled) on effectively every Windows NIC
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.023|                    driver, so this normally re-asserts the factory value.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.024|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.025|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.026|    1514                 : Standard frame: 1500 bytes of payload plus a
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.027|                           14-byte Ethernet header. Everything that leaves
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.028|                           your LAN uses this size anyway.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.029|    4088 or 4096         : Intermediate jumbo sizes offered by some
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.030|                           drivers. Same all-or-nothing requirement as
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.031|                           full jumbo - every hop must agree.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.032|    9014 or 9000         : Full jumbo. On a path where one hop is still at
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.033|                           1500, large transfers fragment or stall - the
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.034|                           classic symptom of a jumbo setting applied to
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.035|                           one machine only.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.036|    SKIP                 : Nothing is written; the adapter keeps the
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.037|                           driver's frame size setting.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.038|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.039|  Why these profiles : Four identical columns at 1514. The distinction
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.040|                       that would matter here is not gaming versus office,
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.041|                       it is whether you built a closed jumbo network -
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.042|                       and none of the four profiles describes one.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.043|                       Windows is SKIP because the value belongs to the
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.044|                       driver and this path does not read the driver's
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.045|                       default. Say no only if you knowingly run a jumbo
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.046|                       storage or virtualization network.
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.047|
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.048|  Target          : the same call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*JumboPacket" "1514"
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.049|                    as the jumbo step (OPTY.bat ~2863). This card explains
::X|EN|net.nic.jumbo.explain.050|                    what the value means and when refusing it is correct.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.001|  Ce que c est    : Les trames jumbo permettent a une carte reseau
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.002| d envoyer des trames Ethernet bien plus grandes que
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.003| les 1514 octets standard. Elles n apportent quelque
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.004|                    chose que si tous les equipements du chemin - switch,
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.005| routeur, machine d en face - sont configures pareil.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.006|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.007|  Effet reel      : Ecrit *JumboPacket=1514, soit la taille de trame
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.008| standard, c est-a-dire jumbo desactive. L effet arrive
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.009|                    au prochain redemarrage de la carte ou de la machine.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.010|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.011|  Gain            : Sur une machine ou les trames jumbo avaient ete
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.012|                    activees sans switch ni NAS assortis, cela regle des
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.013|                    symptomes bien reels : gros transferts qui bloquent,
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.014|                    ou qui marchent en local mais pas vers Internet. Sur
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.015|                    une machine deja a 1514, rien ne change et,
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.016| honnetement, il n y a aucun gain.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.017|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.018|  Cout            : Si vous avez monte volontairement un reseau en trames
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.019|                    jumbo - NAS 10G, iSCSI, virtualisation, avec chaque
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.020|                    port du switch regle sur un MTU de 9000 - ceci
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.021| l annule et vous perdez le debit pour lequel cette
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.022|                    installation existe.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.023|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.024|  Defaut Windows  : 1514 (jumbo desactive) sur pratiquement tous les
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.025|                    pilotes reseau Windows : on reaffirme donc en general
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.026| la valeur d usine.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.027|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.028|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.029|    1514                 : Trame standard : 1500 octets de charge utile
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.030|                           plus un en-tete Ethernet de 14 octets. De toute
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.031|                           facon, tout ce qui sort de votre reseau local
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.032|                           utilise cette taille.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.033|    4088 or 4096         : Tailles jumbo intermediaires proposees par
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.034|                           certains pilotes. Meme exigence du tout ou rien
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.035|                           que le jumbo complet : chaque equipement du
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.036| chemin doit etre d accord.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.037|    9014 or 9000         : Jumbo complet. Sur un chemin ou un equipement
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.038|                           reste a 1500, les gros transferts se
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.039|                           fragmentent ou bloquent : le symptome classique
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.040| d un reglage jumbo applique a une seule
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.041|                           machine.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.042| SKIP : Rien n est ecrit ; la carte garde le reglage de
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.043|                           taille de trame du pilote.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.044|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.045|  Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes identiques a 1514. La distinction
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.046| qui compterait ici n est pas gaming contre
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.047| bureautique, c est de savoir si vous avez monte
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.048|                         un reseau jumbo ferme - et aucun des quatre
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.049| profils n en decrit un. Windows est a SKIP parce
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.050|                         que la valeur appartient au pilote et que ce
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.051|                         chemin ne lit pas son defaut. Dites non
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.052|                         uniquement si vous exploitez sciemment un reseau
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.053|                         de stockage ou de virtualisation en jumbo.
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.054|
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.055|  Cible           : the same call :nicset "%NICKEY%" "*JumboPacket" "1514"
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.056|                    as the jumbo step (OPTY.bat ~2863). This card explains
::X|FR|net.nic.jumbo.explain.057|                    what the value means and when refusing it is correct.
::
:: ---- net.nic.restart.pnputil (risky) ---------------------------
::P|net.nic.restart.pnputil|RESTART|SKIP|RESTART|RESTART|SKIP|
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.001|RESTART THE NETWORK ADAPTER TO APPLY CHANGES NOW
::T|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.002|Makes the settings above take effect immediately instead of at your next reboot, at the cost of a brief 2-4 second network blip that can drop a VPN, an online game or a remote desktop session.
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.001|REDEMARRER LA CARTE RESEAU POUR APPLIQUER TOUT DE SUITE
::T|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.002|Fait prendre effet immediatement aux reglages ci-dessus au lieu d attendre le prochain redemarrage, au prix d une breve coupure reseau de 2 a 4 secondes qui peut couper un VPN, une partie en ligne ou une session de bureau a distance.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.001|  What it is      : NDIS only re-reads the adapter's keyword values when
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.002|                    the device initialises, so every setting written above
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.003|                    does nothing until the adapter restarts or the machine
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.004|                    reboots.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.005|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.006|  Actual effect   : pnputil /restart-device stops and restarts the network
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.007|                    adapter. The link goes down for roughly 2 to 4 seconds
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.008|                    and comes back with the new settings live.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.009|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.010|  Gain            : The settings take effect now rather than at your next
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.011|                    reboot. That is the entire benefit - the settings
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.012|                    themselves are identical either way, which is why
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.013|                    declining costs you nothing.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.014|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.015|  Cost            : For those few seconds the machine has no network:
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.016|                    downloads without resume support die, VPN tunnels
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.017|                    drop, online game sessions disconnect, remote desktop
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.018|                    sessions break. If you are connected to this machine
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.019|                    over RDP you may not get back in until it reconnects.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.020|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.021|  Windows default : Not applicable - this is an operation, not a setting.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.022|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.023|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.024|    RESTART              : The device is stopped and started again. NDIS
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.025|                           re-reads every keyword during initialisation,
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.026|                           which is the only way the values written above
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.027|                           become active without a reboot. Link down
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.028|                           roughly 2 to 4 seconds, longer on Wi-Fi because
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.029|                           the adapter must re-associate.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.030|    SKIP                 : The keywords sit in the registry and are read
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.031|                           at the next boot. Same end state, no
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.032|                           interruption now.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.033|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.034|  Why these profiles : The server column is SKIP and carries the whole
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.035|                       argument: that machine is usually reached over the
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.036|                       network, and it is seeding, serving or transcoding
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.037|                       for someone else while you work on it - a blackout
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.038|                       costs sessions that a scheduled reboot would not.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.039|                       The other three are machines you are sitting in
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.040|                       front of, where the interruption is yours alone and
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.041|                       lasts seconds. Windows is SKIP because restarting a
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.042|                       device is an action Windows never takes on its own.
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.043|
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.044|  Target          : call :nicrestart "%NICKEY%" - reads NetCfgInstanceId
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.045|                    from the class key, looks up PnPInstanceId under
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.046|                    Control\Network\{4d36e972-...}\<GUID>\Connection, then
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.047|                    runs pnputil /restart-device on it (OPTY.bat helper
::X|EN|net.nic.restart.pnputil.048|                    ~3500-3510)
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.001| Ce que c est : NDIS ne relit les mots-cles de la carte qu au moment
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.002| ou le peripherique s initialise : tous les reglages
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.003|                    ecrits plus haut ne font donc rien tant que la carte
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.004| n a pas redemarre ou que la machine n a pas reboote.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.005|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.006|  Effet reel      : pnputil /restart-device arrete et relance la carte
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.007|                    reseau. Le lien tombe environ 2 a 4 secondes puis
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.008|                    revient avec les nouveaux reglages actifs.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.009|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.010|  Gain            : Les reglages prennent effet maintenant au lieu du
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.011| prochain redemarrage. C est tout le benefice : les
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.012| reglages sont identiques dans les deux cas, et c est
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.013|                    pour cela que refuser ne coute rien.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.014|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.015| Cout : Pendant ces quelques secondes, la machine n a plus de
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.016|                    reseau : les telechargements sans reprise meurent, les
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.017|                    tunnels VPN tombent, les parties en ligne se
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.018|                    deconnectent, les sessions de bureau a distance se
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.019|                    coupent. Si vous etes connecte a cette machine en RDP,
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.020|                    vous risquez de ne pas pouvoir revenir avant sa
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.021|                    reconnexion.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.022|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.023| Defaut Windows : Sans objet - c est une operation, pas un reglage.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.024|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.025|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.026|    RESTART              : Le peripherique est arrete puis relance. NDIS
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.027|                           relit tous les mots-cles pendant
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.028| l initialisation, seul moyen de rendre actives
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.029|                           les valeurs ecrites plus haut sans redemarrer.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.030|                           Lien coupe environ 2 a 4 secondes, davantage en
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.031|                           Wi-Fi car la carte doit se reassocier.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.032|    SKIP                 : Les mots-cles restent dans le registre et sont
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.033|                           lus au prochain demarrage. Meme etat final,
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.034|                           aucune interruption maintenant.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.035|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.036|  Pourquoi ces profils : La colonne serveur est a SKIP et porte tout le
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.037|                         raisonnement : cette machine est generalement
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.038|                         jointe par le reseau, et elle partage, sert ou
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.039| transcode pour quelqu un d autre pendant que vous
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.040|                         travaillez dessus - une coupure lui coute des
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.041| sessions qu un redemarrage planifie ne couterait
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.042|                         pas. Les trois autres sont des machines devant
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.043| lesquelles vous etes assis : l interruption n est
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.044|                         que la votre et dure quelques secondes. Windows
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.045|                         est a SKIP parce que redemarrer un peripherique
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.046|                         est une action que Windows ne prend jamais de
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.047|                         lui-meme.
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.048|
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.049|  Cible           : call :nicrestart "%NICKEY%" - reads NetCfgInstanceId
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.050|                    from the class key, looks up PnPInstanceId under
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.051|                    Control\Network\{4d36e972-...}\<GUID>\Connection, then
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.052|                    runs pnputil /restart-device on it (OPTY.bat helper
::X|FR|net.nic.restart.pnputil.053|                    ~3500-3510)
::
:: ---- net.nic.restore.driver14 (repair) --------------------------
::P|net.nic.restore.driver14|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|APPLY|
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.001|RESTORE THE NETWORK CARD TO ITS OWN DRIVER DEFAULTS
::T|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.002|Puts all fourteen adapter settings OPTY changed back to whatever your specific driver ships as default - reads the real values off your hardware instead of guessing, though it deliberately leaves Wake-on-LAN and energy-saving settings alone.
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.001|RESTAURER LA CARTE RESEAU AUX VALEURS D USINE DE SON PILOTE
::T|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.002|Remet les quatorze reglages de la carte modifies par OPTY a ce que votre pilote precis livre comme defaut - lit les vraies valeurs sur votre materiel au lieu de deviner, mais laisse volontairement de cote le Wake-on-LAN et les reglages d economie d energie.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.001|  What it is      : Puts back the 14 adapter keywords OPTY writes, reading
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.002|                    each factory value out of the driver's own
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.003|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default rather than assuming
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.004|                    values for your chipset.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.005|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.006|  Actual effect   : Rewrites interrupt moderation and ITR, the LSO and
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.007|                    checksum offloads, RSS and the queue count, jumbo
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.008|                    frames and the receive and transmit buffers to what
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.009|                    the driver ships. It then hands the TCP globals back
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.010|                    to Windows and offers an adapter restart.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.011|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.012|  Gain            : A guaranteed way back to the manufacturer's tuning on
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.013|                    any card, without you needing to know those values. It
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.014|                    is correct on any chipset by construction, because it
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.015|                    reads them from your own machine.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.016|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.017|  Cost            : Two things it does NOT restore, on purpose: 802.3az
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.018|                    EEE and *FlowControl. The filter deliberately excludes
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.019|                    Wake-on-LAN, EEE and ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown so Restore
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.020|                    does not re-enable settings you turned off outside
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.021|                    OPTY. The side effect is that if OPTY turned EEE or
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.022|                    Flow Control off, this leaves them off - undo those
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.023|                    two in Device Manager, Advanced tab.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.024|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.025|  Windows default : Not applicable - it reads the real per-adapter default
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.026|                    out of the driver instead of asserting one.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.027|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.028|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.029|    APPLY                : Each of the 14 keywords is rewritten with the
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.030|                           value that keyword's own
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.031|                           Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default holds on this
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.032|                           machine - correct on an Intel, a Realtek, a
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.033|                           Killer or anything else, and still correct
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.034|                           after a driver update.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.035|    SKIP                 : Nothing is rewritten and the applied profile
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.036|                           stands.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.037|    keyword excluded from the filter :
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.038|        Wake-on-LAN, the EEE keywords and ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown are never
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.039|        restored, so a setting you turned off outside OPTY is not switched
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.040|        back on behind your back. The price is that OPTY's own EEE and
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.041|        Flow Control writes are not undone either.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.042|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.043|  Why these profiles : The four usage columns are SKIP because restoring
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.044|                       in the middle of a setup run undoes what you came
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.045|                       for. The Windows column is APPLY, and this is the
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.046|                       step that gives that column meaning at all in the
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.047|                       NIC section: everywhere else the Windows answer has
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.048|                       to be SKIP, because OPTY hardcodes vendor values
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.049|                       rather than reading them. Here it reads them, so
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.050|                       APPLY genuinely produces the shipped state.
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.051|
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.052|  Target          : the :net_restore loop over the 14 keywords the apply
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.053|                    path writes, each restored by call :nicdefault from
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.054|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default; the filter excludes
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.055|                    Wake-on-LAN, EEE and ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown by design
::X|EN|net.nic.restore.driver14.056|                    (OPTY.bat ~2985-2995, helper :nicdefault ~3489-3499)
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.001| Ce que c est : Remet les 14 mots-cles de la carte qu OPTY ecrit, en
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.002| lisant chaque valeur d usine dans le Ndi\Params\<mot-
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.003|                    cle>\default du pilote lui-meme, au lieu de supposer
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.004|                    des valeurs pour votre chipset.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.005|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.006| Effet reel : Reecrit la moderation d interruptions et ITR, les
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.007|                    dechargements LSO et checksum, le RSS et le nombre de
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.008| files, les trames jumbo et les tampons d emission et
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.009|                    de reception avec ce que livre le pilote. Rend ensuite
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.010|                    les reglages TCP globaux a Windows et propose un
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.011|                    redemarrage de la carte.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.012|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.013|  Gain            : Un retour garanti au reglage du fabricant sur
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.014| n importe quelle carte, sans que vous ayez besoin de
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.015| connaitre ces valeurs. C est correct sur tous les
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.016|                    chipsets par construction, puisque les valeurs sont
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.017|                    lues sur votre propre machine.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.018|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.019| Cout : Deux choses qu il ne restaure PAS, volontairement :
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.020| l EEE 802.3az et *FlowControl. Le filtre exclut
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.021|                    sciemment Wake-on-LAN, EEE et ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.022|                    pour que la restauration ne reactive pas des reglages
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.023| que vous aviez coupes en dehors d OPTY. Effet de bord
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.024| : si OPTY a desactive l EEE ou le controle de flux,
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.025|                    ils restent desactives - a annuler dans le
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.026|                    Gestionnaire de peripheriques, onglet Avance.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.027|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.028|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet - il lit la vraie valeur par defaut de
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.029| votre carte dans le pilote au lieu d en affirmer une.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.030|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.031|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.032|    APPLY                : Chacun des 14 mots-cles est reecrit avec la
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.033|                           valeur que contient son propre Ndi\Params\<mot-
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.034|                           cle>\default sur cette machine - juste sur une
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.035| Intel, une Realtek, une Killer ou n importe
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.036| quoi d autre, et toujours juste apres une mise
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.037|                           a jour de pilote.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.038| SKIP : Rien n est reecrit et le profil applique reste
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.039|                           en place.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.040|    keyword excluded from the filter :
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.041|        Wake-on-LAN, les mots-cles EEE et ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown ne sont
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.042| jamais restaures, pour qu un reglage coupe en dehors d OPTY ne
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.043|        soit pas rallume dans votre dos. Le prix a payer : les ecritures
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.044| EEE et controle de flux d OPTY ne sont pas annulees non plus.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.045|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.046| Pourquoi ces profils : Les quatre colonnes d usage sont a SKIP parce que
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.047| restaurer au milieu d une configuration annule ce
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.048|                         pour quoi vous etes venu. La colonne Windows est
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.049| a APPLY, et c est cette etape qui donne un sens a
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.050|                         cette colonne dans toute la section reseau :
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.051|                         partout ailleurs, la reponse Windows doit etre
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.052| SKIP, parce qu OPTY code des valeurs de fabricant
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.053|                         en dur au lieu de les lire. Ici il les lit, donc
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.054| APPLY produit reellement l etat livre.
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.055|
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.056|  Cible           : the :net_restore loop over the 14 keywords the apply
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.057|                    path writes, each restored by call :nicdefault from
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.058|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default; the filter excludes
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.059|                    Wake-on-LAN, EEE and ReduceSpeedOnPowerDown by design
::X|FR|net.nic.restore.driver14.060|                    (OPTY.bat ~2985-2995, helper :nicdefault ~3489-3499)
::
:: ---- net.helper.nicset.regsz (preference) ---------------------------
::P|net.helper.nicset.regsz|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.001|HOW NETWORK CARD SETTINGS ARE ACTUALLY WRITTEN
::T|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.002|Explains the internal mechanism OPTY uses to write every adapter setting (REG_SZ, clamped to what your driver actually supports) - informational only, nothing here is a choice you make.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.001|COMMENT LES REGLAGES DE LA CARTE RESEAU SONT VRAIMENT ECRITS
::T|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.002|Explique le mecanisme interne qu utilise OPTY pour ecrire chaque reglage de carte reseau (REG_SZ, borne a ce que votre pilote accepte reellement) - purement informatif, rien ici n est un choix a faire.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.001|  What it is      : The writer behind every adapter setting: a reg add of
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.002|                    one NDIS keyword into the adapter's class key, as
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.003|                    REG_SZ.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.004|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.005|  Actual effect   : REG_SZ is the type the Ndi\Params schema describes and
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.006|                    the type virtually every Windows NIC driver's advanced
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.007|                    properties are stored in, so it is the safe choice. A
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.008|                    REG_DWORD written to the same value name may or may
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.009|                    not be honoured depending on the driver - which is one
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.010|                    reason scripts that write DWORDs sometimes report
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.011|                    success while nothing changes. The helper also clamps
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.012|                    the requested value to Ndi\Params\<keyword>\max,
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.013|                    rounds it down to \step, skips keywords your driver
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.014|                    does not expose, and always writes rather than
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.015|                    skipping an already-correct value.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.016|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.017|  Gain            : The settings you choose reach the driver in the form
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.018|                    its own parameter schema describes. The log
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.019|                    distinguishes SET (the value was absent), written (it
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.020|                    was already correct) and FIXED, so you can tell
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.021|                    whether your machine needed the change at all.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.022|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.023|  Cost            : None on its own. The clamping means the value you
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.024|                    asked for is often not the value written - 1024
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.025|                    buffers on a card whose maximum is 512 becomes 512,
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.026|                    and the log says so rather than pretending.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.027|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.028|  Windows default : Not applicable - this is a generic primitive. Every
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.029|                    real default lives at Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default on
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.030|                    your own machine.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.031|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.032|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.033|    REG_SZ               : The type the Ndi\Params schema describes and
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.034|                           the type virtually every Windows NIC driver's
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.035|                           advanced properties are stored in. Always the
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.036|                           safe choice, which is why every OPTY keyword
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.037|                           write uses it.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.038|    REG_DWORD            : May or may not be honoured depending on the
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.039|                           driver. NDIS reads keywords through
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.040|                           NdisReadConfiguration, which returns an integer
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.041|                           from either type, and some drivers do store
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.042|                           keywords as DWORD - so this is not a universal
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.043|                           silent no-op, but it is one reason scripts that
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.044|                           write DWORDs sometimes report success while
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.045|                           nothing changes.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.046|    the clamped value    : What is actually written when the request
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.047|                           exceeds Ndi\Params\<keyword>\max or is not a
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.048|                           multiple of \step. 1024 buffers on a card whose
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.049|                           maximum is 512 becomes 512, and the log says
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.050|                           512.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.051|    keyword absent from Ndi\Params :
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.052|        Nothing is written at all and the helper returns immediately, so a
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.053|        keyword your driver does not expose is skipped rather than
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.054|        invented.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.055|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.056|  Why these profiles : Nothing to decide here, so five identical columns.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.057|                       It is worth reading because it explains why OPTY's
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.058|                       numbers sometimes differ from the ones you asked
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.059|                       for, and why other scripts' identical-looking
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.060|                       settings can have no effect on your particular
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.061|                       driver.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.062|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.063|  Unverified      : Whether a given driver honours a REG_DWORD keyword is
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.064|                    driver-specific and was not tested here. The claim
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.065|                    made is only that REG_SZ is the type the Ndi\Params
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.066|                    schema describes and that it is always accepted.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.067|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.068|  Target          : :nicset - reg add "<class key>" /v "<keyword>" /t
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.069|                    REG_SZ /d "<value>" /f, after checking the keyword
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.070|                    exists under Ndi\Params, clamping to \max via
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.071|                    :nicclamp and rounding down to \step via :nicround;
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.072|                    the old value is read only to label the log line SET,
::X|EN|net.helper.nicset.regsz.073|                    written or FIXED (OPTY.bat ~3437-3465)
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.001| Ce que c est : La fonction d ecriture derriere chaque reglage de
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.002| carte reseau : un reg add d un mot-cle NDIS dans la
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.003|                    cle de classe de la carte, en REG_SZ.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.004|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.005|  Effet reel      : REG_SZ est le type que decrit le schema Ndi\Params et
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.006|                    celui dans lequel sont stockees les proprietes
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.007|                    avancees de pratiquement tous les pilotes reseau
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.008| Windows : c est donc le choix sur. Un REG_DWORD ecrit
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.009|                    sur le meme nom de valeur peut etre pris en compte ou
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.010| non selon le pilote - c est une des raisons pour
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.011|                    lesquelles des scripts qui ecrivent des DWORD
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.012|                    annoncent parfois une reussite sans que rien ne
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.013|                    change. La fonction borne aussi la valeur demandee a
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.014| Ndi\Params\<mot-cle>\max, l arrondit vers le bas selon
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.015| \step, ignore les mots-cles que votre pilote n expose
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.016|                    pas, et ecrit toujours au lieu de sauter une valeur
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.017|                    deja correcte.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.018|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.019|  Gain            : Les reglages que vous choisissez atteignent le pilote
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.020|                    sous la forme que decrit son propre schema de
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.021|                    parametres. Le journal distingue SET (la valeur etait
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.022|                    absente), written (elle etait deja correcte) et FIXED
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.023|                    : vous pouvez donc savoir si votre machine avait
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.024|                    vraiment besoin du changement.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.025|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.026|  Cout            : Aucun en soi. Le bornage fait que la valeur demandee
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.027| n est souvent pas celle ecrite : 1024 tampons sur une
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.028|                    carte dont le maximum est 512 devient 512, et le
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.029|                    journal le dit au lieu de faire semblant.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.030|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.031| Defaut Windows : Sans objet - c est une primitive generique. Chaque
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.032|                    vraie valeur par defaut se trouve dans
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.033|                    Ndi\Params\<mot-cle>\default sur votre propre machine.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.034|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.035|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.036|    REG_SZ               : Le type que decrit le schema Ndi\Params et
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.037|                           celui dans lequel sont stockees les proprietes
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.038|                           avancees de pratiquement tous les pilotes
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.039| reseau Windows. Toujours le choix sur, et c est
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.040|                           pour cela que chaque ecriture de mot-cle par
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.041| OPTY l utilise.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.042|    REG_DWORD            : Peut etre pris en compte ou non selon le
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.043|                           pilote. NDIS lit les mots-cles via
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.044|                           NdisReadConfiguration, qui renvoie un entier a
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.045| partir de l un ou l autre type, et certains
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.046|                           pilotes stockent effectivement des mots-cles en
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.047| DWORD. Ce n est donc pas un echec silencieux
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.048| universel, mais c est une raison pour laquelle
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.049|                           des scripts qui ecrivent des DWORD annoncent
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.050|                           parfois une reussite sans que rien ne change.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.051|    the clamped value    : Ce qui est reellement ecrit quand la demande
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.052| depasse Ndi\Params\<mot-cle>\max ou n est pas
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.053|                           un multiple de \step. 1024 tampons sur une
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.054|                           carte dont le maximum est 512 devient 512, et
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.055|                           le journal indique 512.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.056|    keyword absent from Ndi\Params :
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.057| Rien n est ecrit du tout et la fonction sort immediatement : un
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.058| mot-cle que votre pilote n expose pas est ignore plutot
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.059| qu invente.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.060|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.061|  Pourquoi ces profils : Rien a decider ici, donc cinq colonnes
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.062|                         identiques. Cela vaut la lecture, car cela
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.063| explique pourquoi les chiffres d OPTY different
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.064|                         parfois de ceux que vous avez demandes, et
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.065| pourquoi les reglages d apparence identique
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.066| d autres scripts peuvent n avoir aucun effet sur
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.067|                         votre pilote precis.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.068|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.069|  Non verifie (en)  : Whether a given driver honours a REG_DWORD keyword
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.070|                      is driver-specific and was not tested here. The
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.071|                      claim made is only that REG_SZ is the type the
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.072|                      Ndi\Params schema describes and that it is always
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.073|                      accepted.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.074|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.075|  Cible           : :nicset - reg add "<class key>" /v "<keyword>" /t
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.076|                    REG_SZ /d "<value>" /f, after checking the keyword
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.077|                    exists under Ndi\Params, clamping to \max via
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.078|                    :nicclamp and rounding down to \step via :nicround;
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.079|                    the old value is read only to label the log line SET,
::X|FR|net.helper.nicset.regsz.080|                    written or FIXED (OPTY.bat ~3437-3465)
