:: ---- net.helper.nicdefault.read (repair) ------------------------
::P|net.helper.nicdefault.read|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|REG_SZ|
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.001|HOW THE NETWORK CARD RESTORE READS FACTORY VALUES
::T|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.002|Explains how OPTY finds each setting real factory default by asking your driver directly instead of guessing a number - informational only, this is the mechanism the restore step above relies on.
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.001|COMMENT LA RESTAURATION LIT LES VALEURS D USINE
::T|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.002|Explique comment OPTY trouve la vraie valeur d usine de chaque reglage en la demandant directement a votre pilote au lieu de deviner un nombre - purement informatif, c est le mecanisme dont depend l etape de restauration ci-dessus.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.001|  What it is      : The restore primitive: for a single NDIS keyword it
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.002|                    reads Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default - the factory value
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.003|                    the driver itself publishes - and writes that back as
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.004|                    REG_SZ.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.005|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.006|  Actual effect   : Nothing is hardcoded per chipset. The value comes from
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.007|                    your own machine's driver, so it is right on any
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.008|                    adapter and stays right after a driver update.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.009|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.010|  Gain            : A restore you can trust. Scripts that hardcode a
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.011|                    default are guessing for your card; this one asks the
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.012|                    driver, which is why the network Restore is safe to
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.013|                    run on hardware nobody tested it against.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.014|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.015|  Cost            : None. If the driver publishes no default for a
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.016|                    keyword, the helper leaves that keyword alone rather
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.017|                    than inventing a value.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.018|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.019|  Windows default : Not applicable - it reads the real default instead of
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.020|                    asserting one.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.021|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.022|  Possible values:
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.023|    the driver's published default, written as REG_SZ :
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.024|        The only value this helper ever writes. It comes from
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.025|        Ndi\Params\<keyword>\default on your own machine, so it is right
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.026|        on an Intel, a Realtek, a Killer or anything else, and it stays
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.027|        right after a driver update.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.028|    no default published : The helper returns without writing anything.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.029|                           There is no fallback number, because a guessed
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.030|                           default is worse than an untouched keyword.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.031|    a hardcoded number   : What other scripts do, and what this helper
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.032|                           deliberately never does - the default is 512 is
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.033|                           a guess about your card, not a fact about it.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.034|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.035|  Why these profiles : Nothing to decide, so five identical columns. This
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.036|                       is the brick the network Restore is built from, and
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.037|                       what it tells you is that Restore is trustworthy on
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.038|                       your specific adapter rather than on the adapter
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.039|                       the author happened to own.
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.040|
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.041|  Target          : :nicdefault - strips the keyword name out of the full
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.042|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword> key path, reads its default value
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.043|                    with reg query, and writes it back to the class key as
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.044|                    REG_SZ; if no default is published the keyword is left
::X|EN|net.helper.nicdefault.read.045|                    untouched (OPTY.bat ~3489-3499)
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.001|  Ce que c est    : La primitive de restauration : pour un seul mot-cle
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.002|                    NDIS, elle lit Ndi\Params\<mot-cle>\default - la
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.003| valeur d usine publiee par le pilote lui-meme - et la
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.004|                    reecrit en REG_SZ.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.005|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.006| Effet reel : Rien n est code en dur par chipset. La valeur vient du
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.007|                    pilote de votre propre machine : elle est donc juste
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.008| sur n importe quelle carte et le reste apres une mise
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.009|                    a jour de pilote.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.010|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.011|  Gain            : Une restauration digne de confiance. Les scripts qui
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.012|                    codent un defaut en dur devinent pour votre carte ;
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.013| celui-ci demande au pilote, et c est pourquoi la
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.014|                    restauration reseau peut etre lancee sans risque sur
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.015| du materiel que personne n a teste.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.016|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.017|  Cout            : Aucun. Si le pilote ne publie aucune valeur par defaut
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.018|                    pour un mot-cle, la fonction laisse ce mot-cle
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.019| tranquille au lieu d inventer une valeur.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.020|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.021|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet - elle lit la vraie valeur par defaut au
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.022| lieu d en affirmer une.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.023|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.024|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.025| the driver s published default, written as REG_SZ :
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.026|        La seule valeur que cette fonction ecrit jamais. Elle vient de
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.027|        Ndi\Params\<mot-cle>\default sur votre propre machine : elle est
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.028| donc juste sur une Intel, une Realtek, une Killer ou n importe
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.029| quoi d autre, et elle le reste apres une mise a jour de pilote.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.030| no default published : La fonction sort sans rien ecrire. Il n y a
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.031| aucun nombre de repli, parce qu un defaut
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.032| devine est pire qu un mot-cle laisse
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.033|                           tranquille.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.034| a hardcoded number : Ce que font d autres scripts, et ce que cette
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.035|                           fonction ne fait jamais volontairement : le
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.036| defaut, c est 512 est une supposition sur votre
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.037|                           carte, pas un fait la concernant.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.038|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.039|  Pourquoi ces profils : Rien a decider, donc cinq colonnes identiques.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.040| C est la brique sur laquelle repose la
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.041| restauration reseau, et ce qu elle vous dit,
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.042| c est que cette restauration est fiable sur votre
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.043|                         carte precise et pas seulement sur celle que
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.044| l auteur possedait.
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.045|
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.046|  Cible           : :nicdefault - strips the keyword name out of the full
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.047|                    Ndi\Params\<keyword> key path, reads its default value
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.048|                    with reg query, and writes it back to the class key as
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.049|                    REG_SZ; if no default is published the keyword is left
::X|FR|net.helper.nicdefault.read.050|                    untouched (OPTY.bat ~3489-3499)
