:: ---- fsutil.disablelastaccess (repair) -------------------------------
::P|fsutil.disablelastaccess|2|2|2|2|2|
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.001|NTFS LAST-ACCESS TIMESTAMP
::T|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.002|Leaves Windows tracking of each file last-read time exactly as it ships - the classic tweak to disable it does not measurably speed anything up here, and disabling it breaks some backup, archiving and forensic tools that rely on that timestamp.
::T|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.001|HORODATAGE NTFS DU DERNIER ACCES
::T|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.002|Laisse le suivi par Windows de la derniere lecture de chaque fichier tel qu il est livre - le tweak classique qui le desactive n accelere rien de mesurable ici, et le desactiver casse certains outils de sauvegarde, d archivage et d analyse qui en dependent.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.001|  What it is      : NTFS can record a last-accessed time on every file you
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.002|                    read. It is one machine-wide switch covering every
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.003|                    NTFS volume, not a per-folder or per-user setting.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.004|
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.005|  Actual effect   : Writes one of four states into
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.006|                    NtfsDisableLastAccessUpdate. Two axes are encoded in
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.007|                    the same number: who owns the decision (System Managed
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.008|                    or User Managed), and whether the write happens.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.009|                    OPTY's -fad writes 1, and +fad writes 2, the shipped
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.010|                    state.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.011|
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.012|  Gain            : No measurable gain on this machine or any machine like
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.013|                    it. The mechanism is real but tiny: NTFS only
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.014|                    refreshes the timestamp when the stored one is more
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.015|                    than about an hour old, so a file read a thousand
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.016|                    times an hour costs one metadata write, not a
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.017|                    thousand. On an SSD that write is invisible next to
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.018|                    everything else the system does.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.019|
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.020|  Cost            : Anything that reads last-access times stops being
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.021|                    trustworthy: some backup and archiving tools, File
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.022|                    Server Resource Manager, storage tiering rules, and
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.023|                    forensic timelines. That is a real loss traded for a
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.024|                    gain you cannot measure.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.025|
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.026|  Windows default : 2 - System Managed with updates enabled. Verified
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.027|                    live: fsutil reports System managed, last access time
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.028|                    updates enabled.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.029|
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.030|  Possible values:
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.031|    0                    : User Managed, updates ENABLED. You have taken
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.032|                           the decision away from Windows and told NTFS to
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.033|                           keep stamping the last-access time. Same
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.034|                           observable behaviour as 2, but Windows will no
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.035|                           longer change it for you on a future upgrade.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.036|    1                    : User Managed, updates DISABLED. NTFS stops
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.037|                           writing the last-access field of
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.038|                           $STANDARD_INFORMATION entirely. This is the
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.039|                           only value that actually removes the write.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.040|    2                    : System Managed, updates ENABLED. Windows owns
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.041|                           the decision and currently keeps updates on.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.042|                           This is what a fresh Windows 11 install ships
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.043|                           with, verified live on this machine.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.044|    3                    : System Managed, updates DISABLED. Windows keeps
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.045|                           ownership but currently suppresses the write.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.046|                           Windows itself moves between 2 and 3 on very
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.047|                           large volumes; you setting 3 by hand pins it
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.048|                           there.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.049|
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.050|  Why these profiles : All five profiles get 2, and that is the honest
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.051|                       answer rather than a shrug. The saving is bounded
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.052|                       at roughly one metadata write per file per hour,
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.053|                       which no profile can feel - not the gaming desktop,
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.054|                       not the torrent and Plex box, not the laptop on
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.055|                       battery. Even the server case, the one usually
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.056|                       cited, reads the same media files over and over,
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.057|                       which is exactly the case the one-hour coalescing
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.058|                       already collapses to almost nothing. If you
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.059|                       administer a volume doing millions of distinct
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.060|                       small reads per hour and nothing there consumes
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.061|                       access times, -fad and value 1 are still there for
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.062|                       you - that is a measurement you take, not a profile
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.063|                       you pick.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.064|
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.065|  Unverified      : An earlier audit note said the +fad option writes 0
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.066|                    rather than the shipped 2, so the script could not
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.067|                    undo its own change. Line 506 now reads: fsutil
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.068|                    behavior set disablelastaccess 2. That defect is
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.069|                    already fixed and must not be re-asserted. Value 3
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.070|                    exists and is documented, but I have not seen Windows
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.071|                    select it on this class of machine, so treat the claim
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.072|                    that Windows moves to 3 on very large volumes as
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.073|                    documented behaviour, not as something measured here.
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.074|
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.075|  Target          : fsutil behavior set disablelastaccess <N> - OPTY.bat
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.076|                    line 505 (-fad writes 1) and line 506 (+fad writes 2).
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.077|                    Backing value:
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.078|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\FileSystem /v
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.079|                    NtfsDisableLastAccessUpdate (REG_DWORD). Machine-wide,
::X|EN|fsutil.disablelastaccess.080|                    all NTFS volumes.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.001|  Ce que c est    : NTFS peut inscrire une date de dernier acces a chaque
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.002| lecture de fichier. C est un interrupteur unique
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.003|                    valable pour tous les volumes NTFS de la machine, pas
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.004|                    un reglage par dossier ni par utilisateur.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.005|
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.006| Effet reel : Ecrit l un des quatre etats dans
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.007|                    NtfsDisableLastAccessUpdate. Le meme nombre encode
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.008|                    deux choses : qui decide (systeme ou utilisateur), et
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.009| si l ecriture a lieu. Le -fad d OPTY ecrit 1, le +fad
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.010| ecrit 2, l etat d origine.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.011|
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.012|  Gain            : Aucun gain mesurable sur cette machine ni sur une
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.013|                    machine comparable. Le mecanisme existe mais il est
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.014| minuscule : NTFS ne rafraichit l horodatage que si
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.015| celui qui est stocke a plus d une heure environ. Un
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.016| fichier lu mille fois dans l heure coute une ecriture
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.017|                    de metadonnees, pas mille. Sur un SSD, cette ecriture
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.018|                    est invisible a cote de tout le reste.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.019|
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.020| Cout : Tout ce qui s appuie sur la date de dernier acces
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.021| cesse d etre fiable : certains outils de sauvegarde et
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.022| d archivage, le Gestionnaire de ressources du serveur
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.023|                    de fichiers, les regles de hierarchisation du
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.024| stockage, les analyses judiciaires. C est une perte
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.025|                    reelle echangee contre un gain que vous ne pouvez pas
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.026|                    mesurer.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.027|
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.028|  Defaut Windows  : 2 - gere par le systeme, mises a jour activees.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.029|                    Verifie en direct : fsutil annonce que les mises a
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.030| jour de l heure du dernier acces sont activees, en
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.031|                    mode gere par le systeme.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.032|
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.033|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.034| 0 : Gere par l utilisateur, mises a jour ACTIVEES.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.035|                           Vous retirez la decision a Windows et demandez
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.036|                           a NTFS de continuer a horodater le dernier
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.037|                           acces. Comportement observable identique a 2,
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.038|                           mais Windows ne le rechangera plus pour vous
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.039| lors d une mise a niveau.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.040| 1 : Gere par l utilisateur, mises a jour
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.041| DESACTIVEES. NTFS cesse completement d ecrire
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.042|                           le champ de dernier acces de
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.043| $STANDARD_INFORMATION. C est la seule valeur
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.044| qui supprime reellement l ecriture.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.045|    2                    : Gere par le systeme, mises a jour ACTIVEES.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.046|                           Windows garde la main et laisse les mises a
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.047| jour actives. C est l etat livre par une
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.048|                           installation Windows 11 neuve, verifie en
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.049|                           direct sur cette machine.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.050|    3                    : Gere par le systeme, mises a jour DESACTIVEES.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.051| Windows garde la main mais supprime l ecriture.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.052|                           Windows bascule lui-meme entre 2 et 3 sur de
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.053|                           tres gros volumes ; le fixer a 3 a la main le
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.054|                           fige la.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.055|
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.056| Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq profils recoivent 2, et c est une vraie
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.057| reponse, pas une derobade. L economie est
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.058|                         plafonnee a environ une ecriture de metadonnees
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.059|                         par fichier et par heure : aucun profil ne peut
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.060|                         la sentir, ni le PC de jeu, ni la machine a
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.061|                         torrents et Plex, ni le portable sur batterie.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.062| Meme le cas serveur, celui qu on cite toujours,
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.063|                         relit sans cesse les memes fichiers medias,
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.064| c est-a-dire exactement ce que le regroupement
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.065|                         horaire ecrase deja. Si vous administrez un
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.066|                         volume qui enchaine des millions de lectures
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.067| distinctes par heure et que rien n y consomme les
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.068| dates d acces, -fad et la valeur 1 restent
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.069| disponibles : c est une mesure a faire, pas un
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.070|                         profil a choisir.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.071|
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.072|  Non verifie (en)  : An earlier audit note said the +fad option writes 0
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.073|                      rather than the shipped 2, so the script could not
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.074|                      undo its own change. Line 506 now reads: fsutil
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.075|                      behavior set disablelastaccess 2. That defect is
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.076|                      already fixed and must not be re-asserted. Value 3
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.077|                      exists and is documented, but I have not seen
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.078|                      Windows select it on this class of machine, so treat
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.079|                      the claim that Windows moves to 3 on very large
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.080|                      volumes as documented behaviour, not as something
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.081|                      measured here.
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.082|
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.083|  Cible           : fsutil behavior set disablelastaccess <N> - OPTY.bat
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.084|                    line 505 (-fad writes 1) and line 506 (+fad writes 2).
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.085|                    Backing value:
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.086|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\FileSystem /v
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.087|                    NtfsDisableLastAccessUpdate (REG_DWORD). Machine-wide,
::X|FR|fsutil.disablelastaccess.088|                    all NTFS volumes.
