:: ---- reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles (repair) --------------------
::P|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.001|DELETE OPTY'S OLD DISK CLEANUP PROFILES
::T|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.002|Removes two unused Disk Cleanup settings an earlier version of OPTY left behind in the registry - pure housekeeping that restores the empty state Windows ships with, with nothing lost unless you happened to create a cleanup profile with that exact number yourself.
::T|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.001|SUPPRIMER LES ANCIENS PROFILS DE NETTOYAGE LAISSES PAR OPTY
::T|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.002|Retire du registre deux reglages de Nettoyage de disque inutilises, laisses par une ancienne version d OPTY - du simple menage, qui retablit l etat vide livre par Windows, sans rien perdre sauf si vous aviez vous-meme cree un profil de nettoyage portant exactement ce numero.
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.001|  What it is      : Disk Cleanup keeps its saved profiles under
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.002|                    VolumeCaches as StateFlagsNNNN values, one per handler
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.003|                    per profile. This step deletes two profiles left
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.004|                    behind by an earlier OPTY version: StateFlags6553 and
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.005|                    StateFlags65535.
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.006|
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.007|  Actual effect   : Removes those two value names from every VolumeCaches
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.008|                    subkey. Windows ships with no StateFlagsNNNN value at
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.009|                    all, so this restores the original absent state
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.010|                    exactly. The profile OPTY uses today, StateFlags0064,
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.011|                    is written separately and is untouched by this step.
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.012|
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.013|  Gain            : A genuine clean-up of a mess an earlier version made.
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.014|                    Two orphaned profiles are removed, and with them any
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.015|                    handler set they had armed. Deleting them cannot make
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.016|                    cleanup worse, and it removes a run-anything trigger
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.017|                    nobody is tracking. Note on the record: cleanmgr
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.018|                    accepts /sageset:0 through 65535 and zero-pads the
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.019|                    value name to at least four digits, so StateFlags65535
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.020|                    is a perfectly valid name and no truncation occurs.
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.021|                    Whatever produced the separate StateFlags6553 value
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.022|                    came from OPTY's own handling of the number, not from
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.023|                    a cleanmgr limit. Which handlers those profiles had
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.024|                    armed cannot be read off the value name, so this card
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.025|                    does not claim to know.
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.026|
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.027|  Cost            : Nothing. Absent is the Windows default. The only loss
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.028|                    would be a profile 6553 or 65535 you had deliberately
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.029|                    configured yourself with cleanmgr /sageset, which is
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.030|                    not a number anybody picks by accident twice.
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.031|
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.032|  Windows default : Absent - StateFlagsNNNN values exist only after
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.033|                    someone has run cleanmgr /sageset with that number.
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.034|
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.035|  Possible values:
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.036|    DELETE               : Both values are removed from every VolumeCaches
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.037|                           handler subkey. A handler with no
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.038|                           StateFlagsNNNN value for a given profile is
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.039|                           simply not part of that profile - cleanmgr
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.040|                           /sagerun:NNNN then has nothing to act on.
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.041|                           Absent is the shipped state.
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.042|    KEEP                 : The two profiles stay in the registry. They are
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.043|                           inert until something runs cleanmgr
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.044|                           /sagerun:6553 or /sagerun:65535, but they
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.045|                           remain a loaded gun: whatever handler set they
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.046|                           encode will fire the day anyone does.
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.047|
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.048|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETE columns, including the
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.049|                       Windows one, and that is not a compromise - it is
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.050|                       the same answer arrived at from both directions.
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.051|                       For the four use profiles it removes stale state
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.052|                       that no longer matches what OPTY does. For the
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.053|                       Windows profile it is literally the shipped
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.054|                       configuration: a clean install has no
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.055|                       StateFlagsNNNN value anywhere under VolumeCaches,
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.056|                       so deleting these is restoring the default, not
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.057|                       overriding it.
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.058|
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.059|  Unverified      : The source comment at OPTY.bat lines 725 to 731 still
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.060|                    asserts the refuted four-digit truncation story and
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.061|                    still names the exact handler set the old profile
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.062|                    supposedly armed. The card no longer says either of
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.063|                    those things, so that comment now contradicts the
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.064|                    shipped documentation and should be reworded to match:
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.065|                    two orphaned profiles removed, mechanism unknown, no
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.066|                    truncation claimed.
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.067|
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.068|  Target          : For every subkey enumerated under HKLM\SOFTWARE\Micros
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.069|                    oft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Explorer\VolumeCaches: reg
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.070|                    delete "<subkey>" /v StateFlags6553 /f and reg delete
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.071|                    "<subkey>" /v StateFlags65535 /f - OPTY.bat lines 735
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.072|                    to 738. StateFlags0064, the profile OPTY actually
::X|EN|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.073|                    uses, is deliberately not touched here.
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.001|  Ce que c est    : Le Nettoyage de disque range ses profils enregistres
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.002|                    sous VolumeCaches dans des valeurs StateFlagsNNNN, une
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.003|                    par gestionnaire et par profil. Cette etape supprime
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.004| deux profils laisses par une ancienne version d OPTY :
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.005|                    StateFlags6553 et StateFlags65535.
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.006|
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.007|  Effet reel      : Retire ces deux noms de valeur de chaque sous-cle de
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.008| VolumeCaches. Windows n est livre avec aucune valeur
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.009| StateFlagsNNNN : on revient donc exactement a l etat
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.010| d origine, celui ou elles n existent pas. Le profil
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.011| qu OPTY utilise aujourd hui, StateFlags0064, est ecrit
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.012| ailleurs et n est pas concerne.
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.013|
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.014| Gain : Un vrai nettoyage d un desordre laisse par une
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.015|                    ancienne version. Deux profils orphelins
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.016|                    disparaissent, et avec eux la liste de gestionnaires
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.017| qu ils armaient. Les supprimer ne peut pas degrader le
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.018|                    nettoyage, et cela retire une gachette que plus
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.019|                    personne ne surveille. Precision pour le proces-verbal
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.020|                    : cleanmgr accepte /sageset:0 a 65535 et complete le
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.021|                    nom de valeur a quatre chiffres minimum par des zeros.
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.022|                    StateFlags65535 est donc un nom parfaitement valide et
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.023| il n y a aucune troncature. Ce qui a produit la valeur
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.024|                    distincte StateFlags6553 vient du traitement du nombre
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.025| par OPTY lui-meme, pas d une limite de cleanmgr. Quels
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.026|                    gestionnaires ces profils armaient ne se lit pas dans
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.027|                    le nom de la valeur : cette fiche ne pretend donc pas
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.028|                    le savoir.
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.029|
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.030| Cout : Rien. L absence EST le defaut Windows. La seule perte
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.031|                    possible serait un profil 6553 ou 65535 que vous
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.032|                    auriez volontairement configure avec cleanmgr
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.033| /sageset, et ce ne sont pas des numeros qu on choisit
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.034|                    deux fois par hasard.
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.035|
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.036| Defaut Windows : Absent - les valeurs StateFlagsNNNN n existent
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.037| qu apres un cleanmgr /sageset portant ce numero.
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.038|
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.039|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.040|    DELETE               : Les deux valeurs sont retirees de chaque sous-
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.041|                           cle de gestionnaire sous VolumeCaches. Un
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.042|                           gestionnaire depourvu de valeur StateFlagsNNNN
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.043|                           pour un profil donne ne fait tout simplement
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.044|                           pas partie de ce profil : cleanmgr
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.045| /sagerun:NNNN n a alors rien a executer.
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.046| L absence est l etat livre.
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.047|    KEEP                 : Les deux profils restent dans le registre. Ils
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.048|                           sont inertes tant que personne ne lance
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.049|                           cleanmgr /sagerun:6553 ou /sagerun:65535, mais
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.050|                           ils restent une arme chargee : la liste de
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.051| gestionnaires qu ils encodent partira le jour
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.052| ou quelqu un le fera.
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.053|
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.054|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes DELETE identiques, y compris celle
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.055| de Windows, et ce n est pas un compromis : c est
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.056|                         la meme reponse atteinte par deux chemins. Pour
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.057| les quatre profils d usage, on retire un etat
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.058|                         perime qui ne correspond plus a ce que fait OPTY.
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.059| Pour le profil Windows, c est litteralement la
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.060|                         configuration livree : une installation propre
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.061| n a aucune valeur StateFlagsNNNN sous
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.062|                         VolumeCaches, donc les supprimer retablit le
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.063|                         defaut au lieu de le contredire.
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.064|
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.065|  Non verifie (en)  : The source comment at OPTY.bat lines 725 to 731
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.066|                      still asserts the refuted four-digit truncation
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.067|                      story and still names the exact handler set the old
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.068|                      profile supposedly armed. The card no longer says
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.069|                      either of those things, so that comment now
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.070|                      contradicts the shipped documentation and should be
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.071|                      reworded to match: two orphaned profiles removed,
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.072|                      mechanism unknown, no truncation claimed.
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.073|
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.074|  Cible           : For every subkey enumerated under HKLM\SOFTWARE\Micros
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.075|                    oft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Explorer\VolumeCaches: reg
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.076|                    delete "<subkey>" /v StateFlags6553 /f and reg delete
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.077|                    "<subkey>" /v StateFlags65535 /f - OPTY.bat lines 735
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.078|                    to 738. StateFlags0064, the profile OPTY actually
::X|FR|reg.cleanmgr.wipe.old.profiles.079|                    uses, is deliberately not touched here.
::
:: ---- cleanmgr.sagerun64 (risky) --------------------------------
::P|cleanmgr.sagerun64|RUN|RUN|RUN|RUN|SKIP|
::T|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.001|RUN DISK CLEANUP
::T|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.002|Frees up real disk space, typically 1 to 10 GB, by deleting temp files, caches and old logs - but it also empties the Recycle Bin and deletes crash dump files, so save anything you need from either first, since this cannot be undone.
::T|FR|cleanmgr.sagerun64.001|LANCER LE NETTOYAGE DE DISQUE
::T|FR|cleanmgr.sagerun64.002|Libere de l espace disque bien reel, en general 1 a 10 Go, en supprimant fichiers temporaires, caches et anciens journaux - mais cela vide aussi la Corbeille et supprime les fichiers de vidage memoire, donc sauvegardez ce dont vous avez besoin avant, car c est irreversible.
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.001|  What it is      : Actually runs Disk Cleanup, non-interactively, with
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.002|                    the handler list configured in the two previous steps,
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.003|                    across every drive.
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.004|
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.005|  Actual effect   : Launches cleanmgr /sagerun:64 detached, then polls
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.006|                    every five seconds and force-kills it at ten minutes.
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.007|                    Everything armed at StateFlags0064=2 is deleted
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.008|                    through the shell API; everything pinned to 0 is left
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.009|                    alone.
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.010|
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.011|  Gain            : Real disk space, typically one to ten GB depending on
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.012|                    how long it has been, mostly Delivery Optimization
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.013|                    payloads, Internet cache, setup and upgrade logs and
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.014|                    temp files. Those categories genuinely refill on their
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.015|                    own and cost nothing to lose.
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.016|
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.017|  Cost            : This is the irreversible step, and two of the
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.018|                    seventeen armed handlers are the reason. The Recycle
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.019|                    Bin is emptied on every drive. The memory dump and the
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.020|                    minidumps go too, so if you are chasing a blue screen
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.021|                    the evidence disappears with them - export or copy
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.022|                    %SystemRoot%\Minidump first if that matters.
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.023|                    Regeneration for the rest is fast and unattended: temp
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.024|                    and Internet cache within minutes of normal use,
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.025|                    Delivery Optimization on the next update, thumbnails
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.026|                    as you browse each folder again, D3D shader cache over
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.027|                    one stuttery session per game. Nothing here touches
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.028|                    your Downloads folder, your documents, installed
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.029|                    programs or Windows Update's own cache - those
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.030|                    handlers are explicitly pinned to 0. The ten-minute
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.031|                    cap exists because /sagerun walks every drive and a
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.032|                    disconnected network mapping stalls it; if the cap
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.033|                    fires, the cleanup was only partly done and no record
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.034|                    says how far it got.
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.035|
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.036|  Windows default : Not applicable - Windows never runs a sageset profile
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.037|                    on its own.
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.038|
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.039|  Possible values:
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.040|    RUN                  : cleanmgr runs headless against profile 0064
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.041|                           across every drive. Each handler armed at 2
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.042|                           deletes its own category through the shell API;
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.043|                           each handler pinned to 0 is explicitly left
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.044|                           alone. Armed here: temp files, Internet cache,
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.045|                           Delivery Optimization, D3D shader cache,
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.046|                           thumbnail cache, setup and upgrade logs,
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.047|                           Defender and Error Reporting files, plus the
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.048|                           Recycle Bin, the memory dump and the minidumps.
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.049|                           Pinned off here: Downloads, Update Cleanup,
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.050|                           Previous Installations, Language Packs, Device
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.051|                           Driver Packages, User file versions and six
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.052|                           more.
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.053|    SKIP                 : Nothing runs. The allow-list written in the
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.054|                           previous step stays in the registry, inert,
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.055|                           until some future run uses it. Windows itself
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.056|                           never invokes a sageset profile.
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.057|
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.058|  Why these profiles : RUN on all four use profiles, because the
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.059|                       maintainer decided that the Recycle Bin and the
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.060|                       crash dumps go, and that decision does not vary by
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.061|                       whether the machine plays games or serves Plex. The
::X|EN|cleanmgr.sagerun64.062|                       distinction that would matter is not the profile,
