:: ---- defrag.all.volumes (preference) --------------------------------
::P|defrag.all.volumes|SKIP|RUN|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.001|OPTIMIZE ALL DRIVES (DEFRAG / TRIM)
::T|EN|defrag.all.volumes.002|Runs Windows' own disk optimizer right now instead of waiting for its weekly schedule - genuinely useful on an aging mechanical hard drive, but essentially no benefit on an SSD, which Windows already keeps trimmed.
::T|FR|defrag.all.volumes.001|OPTIMISER TOUS LES DISQUES (DEFRAGMENTATION / TRIM)
::T|FR|defrag.all.volumes.002|Lance tout de suite l optimiseur de disque de Windows au lieu d attendre sa tache hebdomadaire - vraiment utile sur un vieux disque dur mecanique, mais sans benefice reel sur un SSD, que Windows garde deja a jour cote TRIM.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.001|  What it is      : Windows' own volume optimiser, run over every volume
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.002|                    and adapted to the media. On an SSD that means TRIM
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.003|                    and slab consolidation, not defragmentation. On a
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.004|                    mechanical HDD it means real defragmentation.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.005|
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.006|  Actual effect   : Runs the optimisation immediately, at normal priority.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.007|                    No setting is changed anywhere - Windows already runs
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.008|                    the same work weekly through a scheduled task, just
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.009|                    throttled and only when the machine is idle.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.010|
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.011|  Gain            : On a rotational disk that has not actually been
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.012|                    optimised in a long time, a genuine and measurable
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.013|                    improvement in file access times. On an SSD, none
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.014|                    worth the words: the weekly task keeps TRIM current,
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.015|                    and defragmenting an SSD is not a thing that helps. Be
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.016|                    honest with yourself about which disk you have before
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.017|                    answering.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.018|
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.019|  Cost            : Time and I/O, and /H means it takes that I/O from you
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.020|                    rather than waiting for you to be idle. On a large,
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.021|                    full mechanical disk it can run for a long time. On an
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.022|                    SSD it is quick and harmless but writes for no
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.023|                    benefit.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.024|
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.025|  Windows default : Windows already does the equivalent weekly, at low
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.026|                    priority, via
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.027|                    \Microsoft\Windows\Defrag\ScheduledDefrag, and no
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.028|                    manual run is part of the shipped behaviour.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.029|
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.030|  Possible values:
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.031|    RUN                  : defrag /C /O /U /V /H. /C is every volume; /O
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.032|                           optimises by media type, which means a real
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.033|                           defragmentation pass on a rotational disk and a
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.034|                           TRIM plus slab consolidation pass on an SSD; /U
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.035|                           and /V print progress and a verbose report; /H
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.036|                           drops the low-priority throttle so the job
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.037|                           competes with your work instead of yielding to
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.038|                           it. The whole point of /H is that it finishes
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.039|                           rather than being deferred.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.040|    RUN WITHOUT /H       : The same pass at the default low priority. It
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.041|                           yields to your work, so on a busy machine it
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.042|                           can take far longer or be interrupted, but it
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.043|                           will not make the system feel sluggish while it
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.044|                           runs. This is what the weekly scheduled task
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.045|                           does.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.046|    SKIP                 : Nothing runs now.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.047|                           \Microsoft\Windows\Defrag\ScheduledDefrag still
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.048|                           runs weekly at low priority through Automatic
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.049|                           Maintenance, which is the state Windows ships
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.050|                           in.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.051|
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.052|  Why these profiles : Only the server column says RUN, and it earns it
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.053|                       through a mechanism rather than a vibe.
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.054|                       ScheduledDefrag runs inside Automatic Maintenance,
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.055|                       which needs the machine to be idle; a box seeding
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.056|                       torrents and serving Plex around the clock is
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.057|                       rarely idle, so on that machine the weekly job is
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.058|                       repeatedly deferred and, on a large bulk HDD, may
::X|EN|defrag.all.volumes.059|                       never finish a pass. /H is precisely the switch
