:: ---- ssd.trim.on (repair) ---------------------------------------
::P|ssd.trim.on|0|0|0|0|0|
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.001|SSD TRIM
::T|EN|ssd.trim.on.002|Makes sure Windows tells your SSD which deleted blocks it can erase in advance, which is what keeps write speed from degrading over time - already the default almost everywhere, so on most PCs this changes nothing.
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.001|TRIM SUR SSD
::T|FR|ssd.trim.on.002|S assure que Windows indique bien au SSD quels blocs supprimes il peut effacer a l avance, ce qui evite que la vitesse d ecriture se degrade avec le temps - deja la valeur par defaut presque partout, donc sur la plupart des PC cela ne change rien.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.001|  What it is      : TRIM is how Windows tells an SSD which blocks no
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.002|                    longer hold useful data, so the drive can erase them
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.003|                    in advance instead of during your next write. Without
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.004|                    it an SSD gets progressively slower at writing as it
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.005|                    fills and ages.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.006|
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.007|  Actual effect   : Writes 0 to the delete-notify behaviour, which means
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.008|                    TRIM notifications are sent. This is the default; the
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.009|                    write matters on a machine where someone applied the
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.010|                    old disable delete notify tweak, and is a confirmed
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.011|                    no-op everywhere else.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.012|
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.013|  Gain            : On a machine where TRIM had been turned off, this
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.014|                    stops a slow, cumulative destruction of write
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.015|                    performance and lets the drive recover as blocks are
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.016|                    rewritten. On a machine where it was already on -
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.017|                    which is nearly all of them - there is no change at
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.018|                    all, and no gain to claim.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.019|
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.020|  Cost            : None. There is no credible reason to have TRIM off on
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.021|                    a modern SSD. The only side effect worth naming is
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.022|                    that deleted data becomes genuinely unrecoverable
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.023|                    sooner, which is a security property, not a cost - but
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.024|                    it does mean undelete tools will not help you.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.025|
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.026|  Windows default : 0 - TRIM is enabled by default on every supported
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.027|                    Windows 11 build.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.028|
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.029|  Possible values:
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.030|    0                    : Delete notification ENABLED - TRIM is sent.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.031|                           When a file is deleted, the filesystem issues a
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.032|                           TRIM (or UNMAP on NVMe) telling the drive which
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.033|                           logical blocks no longer hold data. The
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.034|                           controller can then erase those flash pages
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.035|                           during idle time, so your next write lands in
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.036|                           an already-erased page instead of triggering a
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.037|                           read-modify-erase-write cycle. This is the
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.038|                           shipped value.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.039|    1                    : Delete notification DISABLED - no TRIM is sent.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.040|                           The drive keeps believing every block it ever
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.041|                           wrote still holds live data, so its pool of
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.042|                           pre-erased pages shrinks toward nothing. Write
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.043|                           performance then degrades progressively and
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.044|                           permanently until the drive is secure-erased or
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.045|                           its own background garbage collection guesses
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.046|                           right, which without TRIM it largely cannot.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.047|                           This is the state the old disable delete notify
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.048|                           tweak leaves behind.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.049|
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.050|  Why these profiles : Five identical zeroes, and there is nothing to
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.051|                       apologise for: TRIM is a property of how flash
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.052|                       erases, not of what you do with the computer.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.053|                       Gaming, serving, office work and battery life all
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.054|                       want the same thing, and the Windows column wants
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.055|                       it too because 0 is exactly what ships. Writing it
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.056|                       unconditionally is the point - on a machine someone
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.057|                       else tuned, this is the write that makes the
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.058|                       default true again.
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.059|
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.060|  Target          : fsutil behavior set disabledeletenotify 0 - OPTY.bat
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.061|                    line 1558, in the setup path (the re-assert good
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.062|                    defaults block). Backing values:
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.063|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\FileSystem /v
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.064|                    NtfsDisableDeleteNotify and /v RefsDisableDeleteNotify
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.065|                    (REG_DWORD). With no filesystem argument, current
::X|EN|ssd.trim.on.066|                    builds set both.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.001|  Ce que c est    : Le TRIM est la facon dont Windows indique a un SSD
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.002|                    quels blocs ne contiennent plus de donnees utiles,
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.003|                    pour que le disque puisse les effacer a l avance au
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.004|                    lieu de le faire pendant votre prochaine ecriture.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.005|                    Sans lui, un SSD ecrit de plus en plus lentement a
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.006|                    mesure qu il se remplit et vieillit.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.007|
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.008|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 0 dans le comportement de notification de
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.009|                    suppression, donc les notifications TRIM sont emises.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.010|                    C est la valeur par defaut ; l ecriture compte sur une
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.011|                    machine ou quelqu un a applique le vieux tweak disable
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.012|                    delete notify, et c est une operation sans effet
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.013|                    partout ailleurs.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.014|
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.015|  Gain            : Sur une machine ou le TRIM avait ete coupe, cela
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.016|                    arrete une degradation lente et cumulative des
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.017|                    performances en ecriture et laisse le disque se
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.018|                    retablir a mesure que les blocs sont reecrits. Sur une
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.019|                    machine ou il etait deja actif - c est-a-dire presque
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.020|                    toutes - il n y a aucun changement, et aucun gain a
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.021|                    revendiquer.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.022|
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.023|  Cout            : Aucun. Il n existe aucune raison credible de
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.024|                    desactiver le TRIM sur un SSD moderne. Le seul effet
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.025|                    de bord a nommer est que les donnees supprimees
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.026|                    deviennent vraiment irrecuperables plus tot, ce qui
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.027|                    est une propriete de securite et non un cout - mais
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.028|                    cela signifie qu aucun outil de recuperation ne vous
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.029|                    aidera.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.030|
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.031|  Defaut Windows  : 0 - le TRIM est active par defaut sur toutes les
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.032|                    versions de Windows 11 prises en charge.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.033|
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.034|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.035|    0                    : Notification de suppression ACTIVEE - le TRIM
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.036|                           est envoye. A la suppression d un fichier, le
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.037|                           systeme de fichiers emet un TRIM (ou un UNMAP
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.038|                           sur NVMe) indiquant au disque quels blocs
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.039|                           logiques ne contiennent plus de donnees. Le
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.040|                           controleur peut alors effacer ces pages de
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.041|                           flash pendant ses temps morts, si bien que
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.042|                           votre prochaine ecriture arrive dans une page
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.043|                           deja effacee au lieu de declencher un cycle
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.044|                           lecture-modification-effacement-ecriture. C est
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.045|                           la valeur livree.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.046|    1                    : Notification de suppression DESACTIVEE - aucun
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.047|                           TRIM n est envoye. Le disque continue de croire
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.048|                           que chaque bloc qu il a ecrit contient encore
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.049|                           des donnees vivantes, si bien que sa reserve de
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.050|                           pages pre-effacees se reduit a presque rien.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.051|                           Les performances en ecriture se degradent alors
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.052|                           progressivement et durablement, jusqu a un
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.053|                           effacement securise du disque ou jusqu a ce que
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.054|                           son ramasse-miettes interne devine juste, ce
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.055|                           qu il ne peut largement pas faire sans TRIM.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.056|                           C est l etat que laisse le vieux tweak disable
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.057|                           delete notify.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.058|
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.059|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq zeros identiques, et il n y a pas a s en
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.060|                         excuser : le TRIM releve de la facon dont la
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.061|                         flash s efface, pas de ce que vous faites avec
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.062|                         l ordinateur. Le jeu, l hebergement, la
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.063|                         bureautique et l autonomie veulent tous la meme
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.064|                         chose, et la colonne Windows aussi, puisque 0 est
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.065|                         exactement ce qui est livre. L ecrire sans
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.066|                         condition est justement l objectif : sur une
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.067|                         machine reglee par quelqu un d autre, c est cette
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.068|                         ecriture qui rend le defaut a nouveau vrai.
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.069|
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.070|  Cible           : fsutil behavior set disabledeletenotify 0 - OPTY.bat
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.071|                    line 1558, in the setup path (the re-assert good
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.072|                    defaults block). Backing values:
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.073|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\FileSystem /v
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.074|                    NtfsDisableDeleteNotify and /v RefsDisableDeleteNotify
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.075|                    (REG_DWORD). With no filesystem argument, current
::X|FR|ssd.trim.on.076|                    builds set both.
::
:: ---- disable8dot3.system.managed (repair) -----------------------
::P|disable8dot3.system.managed|2|2|2|2|2|
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.001|OLD-STYLE SHORT FILENAMES (8.3 NAMES)
::T|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.002|Puts Windows back to its own default of deciding, drive by drive, whether to create old DOS-style short filenames - this avoids breaking old installers, with no measurable speed difference on a normal PC.
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.001|NOMS DE FICHIERS COURTS A L ANCIENNE (8.3)
::T|FR|disable8dot3.system.managed.002|Remet Windows sur son propre reglage par defaut, ou chaque disque decide seul de creer ou non les vieux noms courts DOS - cela evite de casser de vieux installeurs, sans aucune difference de vitesse mesurable sur un PC normal.
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.001|  What it is      : NTFS can generate an old-style 8.3 short name
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.002|                    (PROGRA~1) alongside every long filename, for
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.003|                    compatibility with software written before long names
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.004|                    existed. Windows ships this as a per-volume decision,
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.005|                    value 2. A common tweak forces 1, disabling short
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.006|                    names everywhere.
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.007|
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.008|  Actual effect   : Writes 2, the shipped per-volume policy. Important:
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.009|                    this only affects names created from now on. Existing
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.010|                    short names are unchanged either way, so it cannot
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.011|                    repair damage that a period at value 1 has already
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.012|                    done - a program whose short path was never created
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.013|                    still will not find it.
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.014|
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.015|  Gain            : It prevents a class of breakage rather than adding
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.016|                    speed. Legacy MSI uninstallers and older applications
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.017|                    that stored PROGRA~1 style paths stop failing on files
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.018|                    created from here on. The performance argument for
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.019|                    disabling 8.3 names is real only on volumes with
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.020|                    hundreds of thousands of files in a single directory,
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.021|                    where each creation has to scan for a name collision -
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.022|                    not on any normal PC.
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.023|
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.024|  Cost            : None on a desktop or laptop. On a genuine large file
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.025|                    server you might measure a difference and choose 1 or
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.026|                    3 deliberately; here the difference is not measurable.
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.027|
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.028|  Windows default : 2 - per-volume, which on a default install means 8.3
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.029|                    names on the system volume and off elsewhere.
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.030|
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.031|  Possible values:
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.032|    0                    : 8.3 name creation ENABLED on every volume.
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.033|                           Every new long filename also gets a PROGRA~1
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.034|                           style short name written into the directory
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.035|                           entry. Maximum compatibility, maximum metadata.
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.036|    1                    : 8.3 name creation DISABLED on every volume,
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.037|                           system volume included. This is what the
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.038|                           popular tweak forces. It is the setting that
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.039|                           breaks legacy MSI uninstallers and older
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.040|                           applications which stored a short path at
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.041|                           install time and cannot resolve it afterwards.
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.042|    2                    : Per-volume - each volume carries its own flag
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.043|                           and Windows decides. On a default install that
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.044|                           means short names are created on the system
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.045|                           volume and not on the others. This is the
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.046|                           shipped policy.
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.047|    3                    : Disabled on every volume EXCEPT the system
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.048|                           volume. The compromise setting: the system
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.049|                           volume keeps the compatibility that installers
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.050|                           depend on, data volumes with huge directories
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.051|                           skip the cost. Meaningful only if you have
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.052|                           actually measured that cost.
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.053|
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.054|  Why these profiles : Five identical twos. The only argument for a
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.055|                       different value is directory-size driven, and it
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.056|                       needs hundreds of thousands of entries in one
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.057|                       folder before the name-collision scan costs
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.058|                       anything - a threshold none of the four use
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.059|                       profiles reaches, including the media server, whose
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.060|                       files are large rather than numerous. The Windows
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.061|                       column is 2 for the plainest reason available: 2 is
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.062|                       what a clean install has, so this card and the
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.063|                       shipped default are the same answer.
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.064|
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.065|  Target          : fsutil behavior set disable8dot3 2 - OPTY.bat line
::X|EN|disable8dot3.system.managed.066|                    1563, in the setup path. Backing value:
