:: ---- rad.driverpolicy.killkeys (repair) -------------------------
::P|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.001|UNBLOCK DRIVER, FIRMWARE AND DEVICE UPDATES
::T|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.002|Removes three leftover policy values that can stop a new device from getting a driver, a firmware update, or even a proper name in Device Manager - deleting them just restores Windows' own default of not blocking any of that.
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.001|DEBLOQUER LES MISES A JOUR DE PILOTES, FIRMWARES ET PERIPHERIQUES
::T|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.002|Supprime trois valeurs de strategie oubliees qui peuvent empecher un nouveau peripherique d obtenir un pilote, une mise a jour de firmware ou meme un nom correct dans le Gestionnaire de peripheriques - les retirer ne fait que retablir le defaut de Windows, qui ne bloque rien de tout cela.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.001|  What it is      : Three policy values that each block a different part
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.002|                    of driver, firmware and device-metadata delivery:
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.003|                    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate,
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.004|                    DontSearchWindowsUpdate and
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.005|                    PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.006|
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.007|  Actual effect   : Deletes all three if present, so drivers, firmware and
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.008|                    device metadata can arrive normally again. Deletion
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.009|                    rather than zeroing, because the default for all three
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.010|                    is that the value does not exist. Each removal is
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.011|                    logged; a value that was already absent is left
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.012|                    silently alone.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.013|
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.014|  Gain            : Fixes the case where a newly attached device gets no
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.015|                    driver and no firmware update and shows up under a
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.016|                    generic model string. This is a repair with a visible
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.017|                    symptom attached, not a speculative improvement - the
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.018|                    symptom is a device in Device Manager with an error
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.019|                    code and no explanation of why Windows never looked.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.020|
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.021|  Cost            : Nothing on a personal machine. On a managed corporate
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.022|                    fleet these three may be set deliberately by IT, in
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.023|                    which case removing them puts you out of policy and
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.024|                    the next Group Policy refresh will write them straight
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.025|                    back.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.026|
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.027|  Windows default : All three absent.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.028|
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.029|  Possible values:
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.030|    DELETE               : All three value names are removed. Absent means
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.031|                           not configured: the Windows Update client
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.032|                           includes drivers in quality updates again,
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.033|                           device installation queries Windows Update for
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.034|                           a driver when the local store has none, and the
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.035|                           device metadata service can fetch a device's
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.036|                           friendly name, icon and Devices and Printers
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.037|                           tile from the network. Absent is the shipped
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.038|                           state for all three.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.039|    1                    : Policy enforced - each value blocks its own
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.040|                           channel. ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.041|                           strips drivers out of quality updates.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.042|                           DontSearchWindowsUpdate=1 stops device
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.043|                           installation from querying Windows Update at
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.044|                           all, so a device with no in-box driver sits in
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.045|                           Device Manager with an error and no
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.046|                           explanation. PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork=1
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.047|                           leaves devices showing a generic model string
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.048|                           with no icon.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.049|    0                    : Policy present and explicitly disabled. The
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.050|                           behaviour matches absent, but the value still
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.051|                           sits under the Policies hive, where Group
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.052|                           Policy tooling reports it as configured and
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.053|                           where a future refresh can flip it back. That
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.054|                           is a third state, not the default - which is
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.055|                           why this step deletes rather than zeroes.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.056|
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.057|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETE columns, and the Windows
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.058|                       column is the strongest of the five rather than the
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.059|                       odd one out: absent is literally what ships, so
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.060|                       deleting these restores the default instead of
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.061|                       overriding it. None of the four use profiles wants
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.062|                       a device that cannot get a driver - the gaming
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.063|                       machine needs GPU and controller drivers, the
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.064|                       server needs storage and NIC firmware, the office
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.065|                       PC needs printers, the laptop needs docks and Wi-
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.066|                       Fi. The only machine that should keep these is one
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.067|                       where IT owns the policy, and that is not a profile
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.068|                       on this scale.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.069|
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.070|  Known problems  : OPTY contradicts itself on
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.071|                    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate. Line 1482, in
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.072|                    :enable_windows_update, writes it to 0 with REG ADD -
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.073|                    creating the value on a machine that never had it,
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.074|                    which is the third state described above rather than
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.075|                    the default. Line 1845 deletes it, correctly. The rule
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.076|                    this project set is that DELETE means the true default
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.077|                    is absence, so line 1482 should be a :killkey call
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.078|                    too, not a REG ADD of 0.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.079|
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.080|  Unverified      : DontSearchWindowsUpdate lives under
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.081|                    Policies\Microsoft\Windows\DriverSearching, while the
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.082|                    user-visible Device installation settings dialog
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.083|                    writes SearchOrderConfig under
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.084|                    CurrentVersion\DriverSearching. They are different
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.085|                    keys with overlapping effects, so a machine can show
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.086|                    driver downloads as allowed in the GUI while the
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.087|                    policy value still blocks them. Which one wins in
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.088|                    every combination is not something I verified on this
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.089|                    machine.
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.090|
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.091|  Target          : Three :killkey calls at OPTY.bat lines 1845 to 1847 -
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.092|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.093|                    /v ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate, HKLM\SOFTWARE\Poli
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.094|                    cies\Microsoft\Windows\DriverSearching /v
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.095|                    DontSearchWindowsUpdate, and
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.096|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\Device
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.097|                    Metadata /v PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork. :killkey
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.098|                    (OPTY.bat line 3987) queries first and deletes only if
::X|EN|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.099|                    the value exists, logging the removal.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.001|  Ce que c est    : Trois valeurs de strategie qui bloquent chacune une
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.002|                    partie differente de la distribution des pilotes, des
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.003|                    firmwares et des metadonnees de peripheriques :
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.004|                    ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate,
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.005|                    DontSearchWindowsUpdate et
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.006|                    PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.007|
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.008|  Effet reel      : Supprime les trois si elles existent, pour que
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.009|                    pilotes, firmwares et metadonnees repassent
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.010|                    normalement. Suppression plutot que mise a zero, parce
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.011|                    que le defaut des trois est que la valeur n existe
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.012|                    pas. Chaque retrait est journalise ; une valeur deja
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.013|                    absente est laissee tranquille, en silence.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.014|
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.015|  Gain            : Corrige le cas du peripherique fraichement branche qui
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.016|                    n obtient ni pilote ni mise a jour de firmware et
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.017|                    s affiche sous un nom de modele generique. C est une
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.018|                    reparation avec un symptome visible a la cle, pas une
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.019|                    amelioration hypothetique : le symptome, c est un
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.020|                    peripherique en erreur dans le Gestionnaire de
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.021|                    peripheriques sans la moindre explication du fait que
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.022|                    Windows n a jamais cherche.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.023|
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.024|  Cout            : Rien sur une machine personnelle. Sur un parc
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.025|                    d entreprise gere, l informatique peut les avoir
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.026|                    posees volontairement : les retirer vous met alors
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.027|                    hors strategie, et la prochaine actualisation de
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.028|                    strategie de groupe les reecrira aussitot.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.029|
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.030|  Defaut Windows  : Les trois absentes.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.031|
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.032|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.033|    DELETE               : Les trois noms de valeur sont supprimes. Absent
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.034|                           signifie non configure : le client Windows
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.035|                           Update reinclut les pilotes dans les mises a
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.036|                           jour qualite, l installation de peripherique
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.037|                           interroge Windows Update quand le magasin local
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.038|                           n a pas de pilote, et le service de metadonnees
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.039|                           peut recuperer sur le reseau le nom convivial,
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.040|                           l icone et la vignette Peripheriques et
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.041|                           imprimantes. L absence est l etat livre pour
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.042|                           les trois.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.043|    1                    : Strategie appliquee - chaque valeur bloque son
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.044|                           propre canal. ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate=1
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.045|                           retire les pilotes des mises a jour qualite.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.046|                           DontSearchWindowsUpdate=1 empeche totalement
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.047|                           l installation de peripherique d interroger
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.048|                           Windows Update : un peripherique sans pilote
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.049|                           integre reste dans le Gestionnaire de
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.050|                           peripheriques avec une erreur et aucune
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.051|                           explication. PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork=1
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.052|                           laisse les peripheriques affiches sous un nom
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.053|                           de modele generique, sans icone.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.054|    0                    : Strategie presente et explicitement desactivee.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.055|                           Le comportement rejoint celui de l absence,
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.056|                           mais la valeur reste sous la ruche Policies, ou
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.057|                           les outils de strategie de groupe la signalent
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.058|                           comme configuree et ou une actualisation
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.059|                           ulterieure peut la rebasculer. C est un
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.060|                           troisieme etat, pas le defaut - d ou la
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.061|                           suppression plutot que la mise a zero.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.062|
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.063|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes DELETE identiques, et la colonne
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.064|                         Windows est la plus solide des cinq plutot que
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.065|                         l intruse : l absence est litteralement ce qui
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.066|                         est livre, donc supprimer ces valeurs retablit le
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.067|                         defaut au lieu de le contredire. Aucun des quatre
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.068|                         profils d usage ne veut d un peripherique
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.069|                         incapable d obtenir un pilote - la machine de jeu
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.070|                         a besoin des pilotes de carte graphique et de
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.071|                         manette, le serveur des firmwares de stockage et
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.072|                         de carte reseau, le poste bureautique des
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.073|                         imprimantes, le portable des stations d accueil
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.074|                         et du Wi-Fi. La seule machine qui devrait les
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.075|                         garder est celle dont l informatique detient la
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.076|                         strategie, et ce n est pas un profil de cette
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.077|                         echelle.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.078|
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.079|  Problemes connus : OPTY se contredit sur
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.080|                     ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate. La ligne 1482, dans
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.081|                     :enable_windows_update, l ecrit a 0 avec REG ADD -
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.082|                     creant la valeur sur une machine qui ne l avait
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.083|                     jamais eue, soit le troisieme etat decrit plus haut
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.084|                     et non le defaut. La ligne 1845 la supprime,
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.085|                     correctement. La regle posee par ce projet est que
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.086|                     DELETE signifie que le vrai defaut est l absence : la
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.087|                     ligne 1482 devrait donc etre elle aussi un appel a
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.088|                     :killkey, pas un REG ADD de 0.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.089|
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.090|  Non verifie (en)  : DontSearchWindowsUpdate lives under
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.091|                      Policies\Microsoft\Windows\DriverSearching, while
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.092|                      the user-visible Device installation settings dialog
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.093|                      writes SearchOrderConfig under
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.094|                      CurrentVersion\DriverSearching. They are different
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.095|                      keys with overlapping effects, so a machine can show
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.096|                      driver downloads as allowed in the GUI while the
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.097|                      policy value still blocks them. Which one wins in
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.098|                      every combination is not something I verified on
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.099|                      this machine.
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.100|
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.101|  Cible           : Three :killkey calls at OPTY.bat lines 1845 to 1847 -
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.102|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.103|                    /v ExcludeWUDriversInQualityUpdate, HKLM\SOFTWARE\Poli
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.104|                    cies\Microsoft\Windows\DriverSearching /v
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.105|                    DontSearchWindowsUpdate, and
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.106|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\Device
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.107|                    Metadata /v PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork. :killkey
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.108|                    (OPTY.bat line 3987) queries first and deletes only if
::X|FR|rad.driverpolicy.killkeys.109|                    the value exists, logging the removal.
