:: ---- rad.driververifier.killkey (repair) ------------------------
::P|rad.driververifier.killkey|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.001|STOP A FORGOTTEN DRIVER-DEBUGGING MODE
::T|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.002|Clears a heavy kernel driver-testing mode meant for developers, which can silently slow the whole system for months with no indication anywhere in Windows that it is even running.
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.001|ARRETER UN MODE DE DEBOGAGE DE PILOTES OUBLIE
::T|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.002|Efface un mode lourd de test des pilotes destine aux developpeurs, capable de ralentir tout le systeme en silence pendant des mois sans que rien dans Windows ne signale qu il est actif.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.001|  What it is      : VerifyDrivers and VerifyDriverLevel put the kernel
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.002|                    into a heavy driver-testing mode meant for developers
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.003|                    debugging a driver crash. Nothing in normal use
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.004|                    switches it on - it takes verifier.exe or a script
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.005|                    writing these values by hand.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.006|
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.007|  Actual effect   : Deletes both values if present, so Verifier stops at
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.008|                    the next boot. The kernel only reads them during
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.009|                    startup, so nothing changes in the current session.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.010|
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.011|  Gain            : Substantial where it applies, and invisible otherwise.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.012|                    Verifier's whole design trades performance for
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.013|                    detection - special pool and I/O verification in
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.014|                    particular add work to every kernel allocation and
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.015|                    every I/O request - and Microsoft's own documentation
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.016|                    warns that a verified system runs measurably slower.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.017|                    What makes this worth a card is not the size of the
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.018|                    number but the fact that there is no indication
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.019|                    anywhere in the Windows interface that Verifier is
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.020|                    running: a machine can sit in this mode for months
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.021|                    while its owner blames the hardware.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.022|
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.023|  Cost            : Nothing, unless you are actively debugging a driver
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.024|                    crash with Verifier on purpose - in which case you
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.025|                    already know, and you will restart it yourself with
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.026|                    verifier.exe.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.027|
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.028|  Windows default : Both values absent, Verifier off.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.029|
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.030|  Possible values:
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.031|    DELETE               : Both values removed. The kernel reads them at
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.032|                           boot only, so Driver Verifier stops at the next
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.033|                           restart and not before. Absent is the shipped
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.034|                           state: a machine that has never run
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.035|                           verifier.exe has neither value.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.036|    PRESENT              : VerifyDrivers names which drivers to verify - a
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.037|                           list, or an asterisk meaning every driver,
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.038|                           which is the pathological case.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.039|                           VerifyDriverLevel is a bitmask selecting which
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.040|                           checks run. The expensive ones are special
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.041|                           pool, which puts every allocation on its own
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.042|                           guarded page, and I/O verification, which wraps
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.043|                           and validates every IRP. Deadlock detection and
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.044|                           pool tracking add their own overhead. So the
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.045|                           slowdown is real but its size depends entirely
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.046|                           on which bits are set and on how many drivers
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.047|                           are in scope.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.048|
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.049|  Why these profiles : Five identical DELETE columns, Windows included,
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.050|                       because absent is the shipped state and no use case
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.051|                       has a reason to run a kernel debugging mode in
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.052|                       production. The right way to read a distinction
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.053|                       here is not by profile but by intent: if you did
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.054|                       not start Verifier, it should not be running, and
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.055|                       if you did, you do not need this card.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.056|
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.057|  Unverified      : The previous version of this card claimed Verifier can
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.058|                    roughly halve I/O throughput. I could not substantiate
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.059|                    that figure, and it cannot be right as a constant: the
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.060|                    cost is a function of the VerifyDriverLevel bitmask
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.061|                    and of how many drivers VerifyDrivers covers, so it
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.062|                    ranges from modest to severe. The card now states the
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.063|                    mechanism and drops the number. The claim that
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.064|                    survives is the one that matters and is checkable:
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.065|                    nothing in the Windows interface tells you Verifier is
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.066|                    on.
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.067|
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.068|  Target          : Two :killkey calls at OPTY.bat lines 1927 and 1928
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.069|                    against %MMK%, which is set at OPTY.bat line 1899 to
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.070|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.071|                    Manager\Memory Management - value names VerifyDrivers
::X|EN|rad.driververifier.killkey.072|                    (REG_SZ) and VerifyDriverLevel (REG_DWORD).
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.001|  Ce que c est    : VerifyDrivers et VerifyDriverLevel mettent le noyau
::X|FR|rad.driververifier.killkey.002|                    dans un mode lourd de test de pilotes, concu pour les
