::X|EN|svc.audioendpointbuilder.021|                           endpoint - speakers, headset, HDMI output on
::X|EN|svc.audioendpointbuilder.022|                           each monitor, microphone - and keeps that list
::X|EN|svc.audioendpointbuilder.023|                           current as devices are plugged and unplugged.
::X|EN|svc.audioendpointbuilder.024|                           Audiosrv depends on it, so this one has to be
::X|EN|svc.audioendpointbuilder.025|                           up first.
::X|EN|svc.audioendpointbuilder.026|    demand               : Start=3. Audiosrv declares a service dependency
::X|EN|svc.audioendpointbuilder.027|                           on it, so starting Windows Audio pulls this one
::X|EN|svc.audioendpointbuilder.028|                           up with it. Manual therefore usually still
::X|EN|svc.audioendpointbuilder.029|                           works - but it makes your audio stack depend on
::X|EN|svc.audioendpointbuilder.030|                           dependency resolution rather than on the
::X|EN|svc.audioendpointbuilder.031|                           service simply being there, for no gain at all.
::X|EN|svc.audioendpointbuilder.032|    disabled             : Start=4. The dependency cannot be satisfied, so
