:: ---- helper.svcset (preference) -------------------------------------
::P|helper.svcset|APPLY|APPLY|APPLY|APPLY|APPLY|
::T|EN|helper.svcset.001|Internal tool: how OPTY changes a service's start type
::T|EN|helper.svcset.002|This is the shared code that writes any service's startup mode, used today only by the gaming-profile restore step; it has no setting of its own, so there is nothing to gain or lose here directly.
::T|FR|helper.svcset.001|Outil interne : comment OPTY change le démarrage d un service
::T|FR|helper.svcset.002|C est le code partagé qui écrit le mode de démarrage de n importe quel service, utilisé aujourd hui seulement par l étape de restauration du profil jeu ; il n a pas de réglage propre, donc rien à gagner ou perdre ici directement.
::X|EN|helper.svcset.001|  What it is      : The generic writer behind every service change: sc
::X|EN|helper.svcset.002|                    config <service> start= auto, delayed-auto, demand or
::X|EN|helper.svcset.003|                    disabled. Today it is reached only from the gaming
::X|EN|helper.svcset.004|                    restore path, which sets SysMain=auto,
::X|EN|helper.svcset.005|                    WSearch=delayed-auto, Spooler=auto, DPS=auto,
::X|EN|helper.svcset.006|                    WerSvc=demand and TabletInputService=demand.
::X|EN|helper.svcset.007|
::X|EN|helper.svcset.008|  Actual effect   : Reads the service's Start value straight from the
::X|EN|helper.svcset.009|                    registry rather than parsing sc qc output, which is
::X|EN|helper.svcset.010|                    translated and breaks on a non-English Windows. It
::X|EN|helper.svcset.011|                    reports absent when the service does not exist on your
::X|EN|helper.svcset.012|                    edition, and it issues the sc config every time, even
::X|EN|helper.svcset.013|                    when the value already matches.
::X|EN|helper.svcset.014|
::X|EN|helper.svcset.015|  Gain            : Puts services back to their shipped start types in one
::X|EN|helper.svcset.016|                    step, with a log line per service saying whether
::X|EN|helper.svcset.017|                    anything actually changed. Note what it does not
::X|EN|helper.svcset.018|                    claim: setting a service to Manual is usually a
::X|EN|helper.svcset.019|                    preference, not damage. Tested live, WSearch left at
::X|EN|helper.svcset.020|                    Manual was still demand-started, SearchIndexer.exe was
::X|EN|helper.svcset.021|                    running, and Start-menu search worked - so 'Manual
::X|EN|helper.svcset.022|                    breaks search' is folklore. Turning off web results is
::X|EN|helper.svcset.023|                    a separate knob entirely, BingSearchEnabled. Likewise
::X|EN|helper.svcset.024|                    Spooler at Manual with no printer installed breaks
::X|EN|helper.svcset.025|                    nothing.
::X|EN|helper.svcset.026|
::X|EN|helper.svcset.027|  Cost            : Putting services back to Automatic costs you the RAM
::X|EN|helper.svcset.028|                    and boot-time work you set out to avoid - SysMain in
::X|EN|helper.svcset.029|                    particular starts reading your disk again after boot.
::X|EN|helper.svcset.030|                    Nothing breaks.
::X|EN|helper.svcset.031|
::X|EN|helper.svcset.032|  Windows default : On Windows 11 25H2: SysMain=Automatic,
::X|EN|helper.svcset.033|                    WSearch=Automatic (Delayed Start), Spooler=Automatic,
::X|EN|helper.svcset.034|                    DPS=Automatic, WerSvc=Manual,
::X|EN|helper.svcset.035|                    TabletInputService=Manual.
::X|EN|helper.svcset.036|
::X|EN|helper.svcset.037|  Possible values:
::X|EN|helper.svcset.038|    auto                 : Writes Start=2. The Service Control Manager
::X|EN|helper.svcset.039|                           starts the service during boot, before the
::X|EN|helper.svcset.040|                           logon screen, in the load-order group the
::X|EN|helper.svcset.041|                           service declares. Use this for services that
::X|EN|helper.svcset.042|                           must be there before anything asks for them.
::X|EN|helper.svcset.043|    delayed-auto         : Writes Start=2 and also sets
::X|EN|helper.svcset.044|                           DelayedAutostart=1. The service starts about
::X|EN|helper.svcset.045|                           two minutes after the auto-start services are
::X|EN|helper.svcset.046|                           done, at lowered I/O and CPU priority, so it
::X|EN|helper.svcset.047|                           does not compete with logon. Because the Start
::X|EN|helper.svcset.048|                           value is still 2, :svcset compares it against
::X|EN|helper.svcset.049|                           an expected value of 2 - the delayed flag is
::X|EN|helper.svcset.050|                           invisible to that comparison, and sc config
::X|EN|helper.svcset.051|                           sets it separately.
::X|EN|helper.svcset.052|    demand               : Writes Start=3. The service is not started by
::X|EN|helper.svcset.053|                           the Service Control Manager at boot. Whether it
::X|EN|helper.svcset.054|                           ever runs depends entirely on whether something
::X|EN|helper.svcset.055|                           starts it - a trigger, a service dependency, a
::X|EN|helper.svcset.056|                           COM activation or a scheduled task. For some
::X|EN|helper.svcset.057|                           services that is constant (WSearch), for others
::X|EN|helper.svcset.058|                           nothing ever does it (SysMain), and that
