::P|helper.svcfixifdisabled|REPAIR|REPAIR|REPAIR|REPAIR|REPAIR|
::T|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.001|Internal repair: turn a disabled service back on
::T|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.002|This only touches services that were fully disabled and restores each to its own shipped start type, fixing real failures like broken Windows Update or sign-in without ever touching a service you set to Manual on purpose.
::T|FR|helper.svcfixifdisabled.001|Réparation interne : réactiver un service désactivé
::T|FR|helper.svcfixifdisabled.002|Elle ne touche que les services complètement désactivés et remet chacun à son propre type de démarrage d origine, corrigeant de vraies pannes comme Windows Update ou l ouverture de session cassée, sans jamais toucher un service mis sur Manuel volontairement.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.001|  What it is      : A narrow repair that touches a service only when its
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.002|                    Start value is 4 (Disabled). It covers the Windows
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.003|                    Update chain - wuauserv, UsoSvc, DoSvc, BITS,
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.004|                    CryptSvc, TrustedInstaller, msiserver, InstallService,
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.005|                    AppIDSvc, wlidsvc and mpssvc - plus the services
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.006|                    debloat lists actually wreck: AppInfo, ClipSVC,
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.007|                    TokenBroker, TimeBrokerSvc, FontCache, WpnService,
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.008|                    AppXSvc, StateRepository, ProfSvc, nsi, BFE and Power.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.009|                    Each is repaired to ITS OWN shipped start type, not to
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.010|                    a blanket Manual.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.011|
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.012|  Actual effect   : Reads Start; if it is anything other than 4 it does
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.013|                    nothing at all and moves on. Only a genuinely disabled
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.014|                    service is put back, and only to the value Windows
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.015|                    ships for that service. A service you deliberately set
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.016|                    to Manual is left alone by design.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.017|
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.018|  Gain            : It fixes real, specific and otherwise unreadable
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.019|                    failures: wlidsvc disabled makes Windows Update fail
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.020|                    with 0x80070426, mpssvc disabled leaves update
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.021|                    downloads stuck at 0 percent forever, AppInfo disabled
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.022|                    makes every UAC elevation fail silently, ProfSvc
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.023|                    disabled stops you signing in at all. If your Windows
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.024|                    Update has been broken since some debloat script, this
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.025|                    is usually why.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.026|
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.027|  Cost            : Nothing - this is a pure repair. The only thing you
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.028|                    give up is whatever you believed you gained by
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.029|                    disabling Windows Update machinery, and mpssvc coming
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.030|                    back means Windows Firewall enforces rules again,
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.031|                    which is the shipped and safe state.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.032|
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.033|  Windows default : On Windows 11 25H2 wuauserv, BITS, TrustedInstaller,
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.034|                    msiserver, InstallService, AppIDSvc and wlidsvc ship
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.035|                    Manual or Manual (Trigger Start). CryptSvc ships
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.036|                    Automatic, mpssvc ships Automatic, and DoSvc and
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.037|                    UsoSvc ship Automatic (Delayed / Trigger Start) -
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.038|                    verified live: CryptSvc Start=2, DoSvc Start=2 with
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.039|                    DelayedAutostart=1, UsoSvc Start=2 with
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.040|                    DelayedAutostart=1, mpssvc Start=2, BITS Start=3,
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.041|                    wuauserv Start=3.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.042|
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.043|  Possible values:
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.044|    REPAIR               : For each service in the list, read Start. If
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.045|                           the value is missing, the service does not
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.046|                           exist on this edition and the helper returns.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.047|                           If the value is anything other than 4, the
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.048|                           helper returns without writing. Only Start=4 is
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.049|                           rewritten, and only to the shipped value the
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.050|                           caller passed for that particular service.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.051|    SKIP                 : Nothing is read and nothing is written. Any
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.052|                           service left at Disabled stays there, and the
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.053|                           failures it causes keep happening with no
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.054|                           message that names it.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.055|    demand               : The value the caller passes for wuauserv, BITS,
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.056|                           TrustedInstaller, msiserver, InstallService,
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.057|                           AppIDSvc, wlidsvc, AppInfo, ClipSVC,
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.058|                           TokenBroker, TimeBrokerSvc, FontCache and
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.059|                           WpnService. All of these ship Manual or Manual
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.060|                           (Trigger Start), so Manual is genuinely their
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.061|                           default and writing it restores the shipped
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.062|                           state.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.063|    auto                 : The value the caller passes for CryptSvc,
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.064|                           mpssvc, AppXSvc, StateRepository, ProfSvc, nsi,
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.065|                           BFE and Power. CryptSvc and mpssvc in
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.066|                           particular ship Automatic on every Windows
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.067|                           client edition - repairing them to Manual, as
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.068|                           an earlier version of this loop did, leaves the
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.069|                           machine below its shipped state while the log
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.070|                           reports a successful repair.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.071|    delayed-auto         : The value the caller passes for DoSvc and
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.072|                           UsoSvc, both of which ship Automatic (Delayed /
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.073|                           Trigger Start). DoSvc is the primary update
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.074|                           downloader on Windows 11 - BITS is only the
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.075|                           fallback - so getting its start type wrong
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.076|                           matters more than the name suggests.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.077|
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.078|  Why these profiles : All five identical, and there is no honest
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.079|                       alternative. A disabled ProfSvc is not a gaming
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.080|                       preference, it is a machine nobody can log into.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.081|                       The only people who should decline are those
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.082|                       deliberately keeping Windows Update disabled and
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.083|                       accepting the security consequence, and that is an
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.084|                       answer of skip, not a profile.
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.085|
::X|EN|helper.svcfixifdisabled.086|  Known problems  : Not scripted anywhere on purpose: DcomLaunch, RpcSs,
