:: ---- reboot.machine (risky) ------------------------------------
::P|reboot.machine|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|SKIP|
::T|EN|reboot.machine.001|Restart the PC when OPTY finishes
::T|EN|reboot.machine.002|Some changes in this run, like Memory Integrity or a pagefile repair, only take effect after a restart, but the forced 15-second restart closes everything without a save prompt.
::T|FR|reboot.machine.001|Redémarrer le PC à la fin d OPTY
::T|FR|reboot.machine.002|Certains changements de cette exécution, comme l intégrité de la mémoire ou une réparation du fichier d échange, n agissent qu après un redémarrage, mais le redémarrage forcé de 15 secondes ferme tout sans proposer d enregistrer.
::X|EN|reboot.machine.001|  What it is      : Chains a restart onto the end of the run, with
::X|EN|reboot.machine.002|                    shutdown /r /f /t 15 - restart in 15 seconds, forcing
::X|EN|reboot.machine.003|                    every application closed.
::X|EN|reboot.machine.004|
::X|EN|reboot.machine.005|  Actual effect   : Schedules the restart. /f kills applications rather
::X|EN|reboot.machine.006|                    than asking them to close, so nothing prompts you to
::X|EN|reboot.machine.007|                    save.
::X|EN|reboot.machine.008|
::X|EN|reboot.machine.009|  Gain            : A reboot is what actually finishes several of the
::X|EN|reboot.machine.010|                    things this tool does: caches locked by a running
::X|EN|reboot.machine.011|                    Explorer are released, restarted services settle, and
::X|EN|reboot.machine.012|                    any setting that needs a restart takes effect. If you
::X|EN|reboot.machine.013|                    touched Memory Integrity or VBS, or the pagefile was
::X|EN|reboot.machine.014|                    repaired, a reboot is not optional - only its timing
::X|EN|reboot.machine.015|                    is.
::X|EN|reboot.machine.016|
::X|EN|reboot.machine.017|  Cost            : Unsaved work is lost without a prompt, and a scheduled
::X|EN|reboot.machine.018|                    CHKDSK /f /r starts on this reboot and can hold the
::X|EN|reboot.machine.019|                    machine for hours. shutdown /a within 15 seconds
::X|EN|reboot.machine.020|                    cancels it.
::X|EN|reboot.machine.021|
::X|EN|reboot.machine.022|  Windows default : Not applicable.
::X|EN|reboot.machine.023|
::X|EN|reboot.machine.024|  Possible values:
::X|EN|reboot.machine.025|    APPLY                : shutdown /r /f /t 15 is issued: restart in 15
::X|EN|reboot.machine.026|                           seconds, /f killing applications instead of
::X|EN|reboot.machine.027|                           asking them to close.
::X|EN|reboot.machine.028|    SKIP                 : OPTY returns to the menu. Settings that need a
::X|EN|reboot.machine.029|                           restart - HVCI and VBS above all, and the
::X|EN|reboot.machine.030|                           pagefile repair - stay pending until you reboot
::X|EN|reboot.machine.031|                           on your own terms.
::X|EN|reboot.machine.032|
::X|EN|reboot.machine.033|  Why these profiles : Five identical columns. The reboot is needed on
::X|EN|reboot.machine.034|                       every profile that changed HVCI, VBS or the
::X|EN|reboot.machine.035|                       pagefile, and needed on none that did not - which
::X|EN|reboot.machine.036|                       is a property of the run, not of the machine's job.
::X|EN|reboot.machine.037|                       SKIP is the default everywhere because you can
::X|EN|reboot.machine.038|                       reboot yourself in ten seconds, with your documents
::X|EN|reboot.machine.039|                       saved, and because the forced version gives no
::X|EN|reboot.machine.040|                       warning at all.
::X|EN|reboot.machine.041|
::X|EN|reboot.machine.042|  Target          : :reboot label, OPTY.bat 1358-1367 - shutdown /r /f /t
::X|EN|reboot.machine.043|                    15 followed by timeout /t 15 and exit
::X|FR|reboot.machine.001|  Ce que c est    : Enchaine un redemarrage a la fin de l execution, via
::X|FR|reboot.machine.002|                    shutdown /r /f /t 15 : redemarrage dans 15 secondes,
::X|FR|reboot.machine.003|                    en fermant de force toutes les applications.
::X|FR|reboot.machine.004|
::X|FR|reboot.machine.005|  Effet reel      : Planifie le redemarrage. /f tue les applications au
::X|FR|reboot.machine.006|                    lieu de leur demander de se fermer : rien ne vous
::X|FR|reboot.machine.007|                    propose d enregistrer.
::X|FR|reboot.machine.008|
::X|FR|reboot.machine.009|  Gain            : Un redemarrage est ce qui termine reellement plusieurs
::X|FR|reboot.machine.010|                    actions de cet outil : les caches verrouilles par
::X|FR|reboot.machine.011|                    l Explorateur sont liberes, les services relances se
::X|FR|reboot.machine.012|                    stabilisent, et tout reglage qui l exige prend effet.
::X|FR|reboot.machine.013|                    Si vous avez touche a l integrite de la memoire ou a
::X|FR|reboot.machine.014|                    la VBS, ou si le fichier d echange a ete repare, le
::X|FR|reboot.machine.015|                    redemarrage n est pas optionnel : seul son moment
::X|FR|reboot.machine.016|                    l est.
::X|FR|reboot.machine.017|
::X|FR|reboot.machine.018|  Cout            : Le travail non enregistre est perdu sans
::X|FR|reboot.machine.019|                    avertissement, et un CHKDSK /f /r planifie demarre a
::X|FR|reboot.machine.020|                    ce redemarrage et peut monopoliser la machine des
::X|FR|reboot.machine.021|                    heures. shutdown /a dans les 15 secondes l annule.
::X|FR|reboot.machine.022|
::X|FR|reboot.machine.023|  Defaut Windows  : Sans objet.
::X|FR|reboot.machine.024|
::X|FR|reboot.machine.025|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|reboot.machine.026|    APPLY                : shutdown /r /f /t 15 est lance : redemarrage
::X|FR|reboot.machine.027|                           dans 15 secondes, /f tuant les applications au
::X|FR|reboot.machine.028|                           lieu de leur demander de se fermer.
::X|FR|reboot.machine.029|    SKIP                 : OPTY revient au menu. Les reglages qui exigent
::X|FR|reboot.machine.030|                           un redemarrage - HVCI et VBS en premier lieu,
::X|FR|reboot.machine.031|                           ainsi que la reparation du fichier d echange -
::X|FR|reboot.machine.032|                           restent en attente jusqu a ce que vous
::X|FR|reboot.machine.033|                           redemarriez a votre convenance.
::X|FR|reboot.machine.034|
::X|FR|reboot.machine.035|  Pourquoi ces profils : Cinq colonnes identiques. Le redemarrage est
::X|FR|reboot.machine.036|                         necessaire sur tout profil ayant modifie HVCI, la
::X|FR|reboot.machine.037|                         VBS ou le fichier d echange, et inutile sur ceux
::X|FR|reboot.machine.038|                         qui n y ont pas touche : c est une propriete de
::X|FR|reboot.machine.039|                         l execution, pas du role de la machine. SKIP est
::X|FR|reboot.machine.040|                         le defaut partout parce que vous pouvez
::X|FR|reboot.machine.041|                         redemarrer vous-meme en dix secondes, documents
::X|FR|reboot.machine.042|                         enregistres, et parce que la version forcee
::X|FR|reboot.machine.043|                         n avertit personne.
::X|FR|reboot.machine.044|
::X|FR|reboot.machine.045|  Cible           : :reboot label, OPTY.bat 1358-1367 - shutdown /r /f /t
::X|FR|reboot.machine.046|                    15 followed by timeout /t 15 and exit
::
:: ---- mmcss.systemresponsiveness (repair) ------------------------
::P|mmcss.systemresponsiveness|20|20|20|20|20|
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.001|CPU reserve for multimedia apps (MMCSS): 20, the Windows default
::T|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.002|This sets how much CPU is guaranteed to background work versus multimedia threads, not your frame rate; 20 is the Windows default, so this mainly just undoes an old tweak-guide value with no real gain.
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.001|Réserve CPU pour applis multimédias (MMCSS) : 20, la valeur Windows
::T|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.002|Cela règle la part de CPU garantie au travail en arrière-plan face aux threads multimédias, pas vos images par seconde ; 20 est la valeur Windows, donc ceci ne fait le plus souvent qu annuler un ancien réglage de guide, sans gain réel.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.001|  What it is      : SystemResponsiveness tells the Multimedia Class
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.002|                    Scheduler Service what percentage of CPU time to keep
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.003|                    guaranteed for ordinary, non-multimedia work when a
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.004|                    multimedia thread has registered with it. Windows 11
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.005|                    client editions ship 20. Optimizer guides have been
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.006|                    telling people to write 0 or 10 for years.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.007|
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.008|  Actual effect   : Writes 20, the value your machine already has unless
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.009|                    something changed it. On a clean install this is a
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.010|                    deliberate no-op; on a machine an older OPTY build
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.011|                    touched, it is a real repair - 10 was measured live
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.012|                    here.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.013|
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.014|  Gain            : None on a healthy machine, and this card will not
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.015|                    pretend otherwise. Its only job is to undo a previous
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.016|                    tweak. There is no frame-rate mechanism here: the
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.017|                    value governs a CPU reserve for threads that
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.018|                    registered with MMCSS, not the game's render thread.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.019|
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.020|  Cost            : Nothing. It restores or preserves the shipped value.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.021|
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.022|  Windows default : 20 (0x14) on client editions. Windows Server ships
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.023|                    100.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.024|
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.025|  Possible values:
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.026|    20                   : The shipped client value. MMCSS guarantees 20
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.027|                           percent of CPU time to ordinary, non-multimedia
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.028|                           threads and lets registered multimedia threads
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.029|                           have priority over the remaining 80 percent.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.030|                           This is what Windows 11 Home and Pro install
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.031|                           with.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.032|    10                   : Half the reserve. Registered multimedia threads
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.033|                           get a larger guaranteed slice, ordinary threads
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.034|                           a smaller one. This is the value earlier OPTY
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.035|                           builds left behind, measured live on this
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.036|                           machine.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.037|    0                    : Ask for no reserve at all for ordinary threads.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.038|                           This is the classic tweak-guide value. Starving
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.039|                           background threads of guaranteed CPU is what
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.040|                           produces audio crackle under heavy load - the
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.041|                           opposite of what the guides promise.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.042|    100                  : Reserve everything for ordinary threads, which
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.043|                           effectively neutralises the multimedia priority
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.044|                           boost. This is what Windows Server ships,
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.045|                           because a server has no local media playback to
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.046|                           protect.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.047|    absent               : If the value is missing, MMCSS falls back to
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.048|                           its built-in behaviour. Windows 11 client
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.049|                           always ships it present at 20, so absence means
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.050|                           something removed it.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.051|
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.052|  Why these profiles : All five profiles write 20 because 20 IS the
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.053|                       Windows value and nothing about gaming, hosting,
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.054|                       office work or battery changes what a CPU reserve
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.055|                       for multimedia threads should be. A machine that
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.056|                       never had a tweak applied will notice no difference
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.057|                       at all - that is the expected outcome, not a
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.058|                       failure.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.059|
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.060|  Unverified      : Microsoft documents the useful range as 10 to 100 and
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.061|                    values outside it are said to be clamped, but I have
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.062|                    not verified the clamping behaviour on this machine,
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.063|                    so treat 0 as 'requests zero reserve' rather than as a
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.064|                    proven starvation setting.
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.065|
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.066|  Target          : reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.067|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile" /v
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.068|                    "SystemResponsiveness" /t REG_DWORD /d 20 /f - written via
::X|EN|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.069|                    :askreg inside :setup_system
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.001|  Ce que c est    : SystemResponsiveness indique au service MMCSS quel
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.002|                    pourcentage de temps CPU garder garanti pour le
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.003|                    travail ordinaire, non multimedia, quand un thread
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.004|                    multimedia s est enregistre aupres de lui. Les
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.005|                    editions client de Windows 11 livrent 20. Les guides
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.006|                    d optimisation conseillent 0 ou 10 depuis des annees.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.007|
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.008|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 20, la valeur que votre machine possede deja
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.009|                    sauf si quelque chose l a changee. Sur une
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.010|                    installation propre, c est volontairement une ecriture
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.011|                    sans effet ; sur une machine touchee par une ancienne
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.012|                    version d OPTY, c est une vraie reparation - 10 a ete
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.013|                    mesure ici en vrai.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.014|
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.015|  Gain            : Aucun sur une machine saine, et cette fiche ne
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.016|                    pretendra pas le contraire. Son seul role est
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.017|                    d annuler un tweak anterieur. Il n y a aucun mecanisme
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.018|                    cote images par seconde : cette valeur gouverne une
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.019|                    reserve CPU pour les threads enregistres aupres de
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.020|                    MMCSS, pas le thread de rendu du jeu.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.021|
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.022|  Cout            : Rien. Elle restaure ou preserve la valeur d origine.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.023|
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.024|  Defaut Windows  : 20 (0x14) sur les editions client. Windows Server est
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.025|                    livre a 100.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.026|
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.027|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.028|    20                   : La valeur d origine des editions client. MMCSS
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.029|                           garantit 20 pour cent du temps CPU aux threads
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.030|                           ordinaires, non multimedias, et laisse les
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.031|                           threads multimedias enregistres prioritaires
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.032|                           sur les 80 pour cent restants. C est ce
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.033|                           qu installent Windows 11 Famille et Pro.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.034|    10                   : Moitie moins de reserve. Les threads
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.035|                           multimedias enregistres obtiennent une part
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.036|                           garantie plus grande, les threads ordinaires
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.037|                           une plus petite. C est la valeur laissee par
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.038|                           d anciennes versions d OPTY, mesuree en vrai
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.039|                           sur cette machine.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.040|    0                    : Ne rien reserver du tout aux threads
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.041|                           ordinaires. C est la valeur classique des
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.042|                           guides de bidouille. Priver les threads
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.043|                           d arriere-plan de CPU garanti est precisement
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.044|                           ce qui produit les craquements audio en forte
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.045|                           charge, l inverse de ce que promettent ces
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.046|                           guides.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.047|    100                  : Tout reserver aux threads ordinaires, ce qui
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.048|                           neutralise de fait la priorite multimedia.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.049|                           C est la valeur livree par Windows Server, un
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.050|                           serveur n ayant pas de lecture media locale a
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.051|                           proteger.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.052|    absent               : Si la valeur manque, MMCSS retombe sur son
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.053|                           comportement interne. Windows 11 client la
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.054|                           livre toujours presente a 20 : son absence
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.055|                           signifie que quelque chose l a supprimee.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.056|
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.057|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq profils ecrivent 20 parce que 20 EST la
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.058|                         valeur Windows, et que ni le jeu, ni
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.059|                         l hebergement, ni la bureautique, ni la batterie
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.060|                         ne changent ce que devrait valoir une reserve CPU
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.061|                         pour threads multimedias. Une machine jamais
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.062|                         bidouillee ne verra strictement aucune difference
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.063|                         : c est le resultat attendu, pas un echec.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.064|
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.065|  Non verifie (en)  : Microsoft documents the useful range as 10 to 100
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.066|                      and values outside it are said to be clamped, but I
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.067|                      have not verified the clamping behaviour on this
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.068|                      machine, so treat 0 as  requests zero reserve 
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.069|                      rather than as a proven starvation setting.
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.070|
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.071|  Cible           : reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.072|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile" /v
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.073|                    "SystemResponsiveness" /t REG_DWORD /d 20 /f - ecrit via
::X|FR|mmcss.systemresponsiveness.074|                    :askreg dans :setup_system
::
:: ---- gr.systemresponsiveness (repair) ---------------------------
::P|gr.systemresponsiveness|20|20|20|20|20|
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.001|Undo a gaming tweak: MMCSS CPU reserve back to 20
::T|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.002|Old tweak guides set this to 0 or 10 hoping to help games, which can instead starve background audio threads; putting it back to 20 undoes that with no downside and no direct gain either.
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.001|Annuler un réglage jeu : réserve CPU MMCSS remise à 20
::T|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.002|D anciens guides réglaient ceci à 0 ou 10 en espérant aider les jeux, ce qui peut au contraire affamer les threads audio en arrière-plan ; revenir à 20 annule cela sans inconvénient ni gain direct non plus.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.001|  What it is      : The share of CPU that MMCSS keeps guaranteed for non-
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.002|                    multimedia work while a media application is
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.003|                    registered with it. This is the revert half of the
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.004|                    gaming profile.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.005|
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.006|  Actual effect   : Writes 20. Tweak guides use 0 or 10, which changes
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.007|                    scheduling only for threads registered with MMCSS -
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.008|                    typically the audio render threads of games and media
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.009|                    applications, since the Games, Audio and Pro Audio
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.010|                    task profiles exist for exactly that, and not a game's
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.011|                    main render thread.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.012|
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.013|  Gain            : None if you were already at 20. On a machine sitting
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.014|                    at 0 it removes the condition that starves audio
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.015|                    threads under load, which is the audible symptom
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.016|                    people report.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.017|
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.018|  Cost            : Nothing. This is a pure repair back to the shipped
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.019|                    number.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.020|
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.021|  Windows default : 20 (0x14).
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.022|
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.023|  Possible values:
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.024|    20                   : The shipped client value: 20 percent of CPU
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.025|                           time stays guaranteed to ordinary threads, the
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.026|                           rest is available to threads registered with
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.027|                           MMCSS.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.028|    10                   : Half the reserve for ordinary threads. The
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.029|                           value earlier OPTY builds left behind on this
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.030|                           very machine.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.031|    0                    : Asks for no reserve at all. The tweak-guide
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.032|                           favourite, and the state in which audio render
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.033|                           threads can be starved under heavy load.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.034|    100                  : Everything reserved for ordinary threads,
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.035|                           neutralising the multimedia boost. The Windows
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.036|                           Server value.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.037|    absent               : MMCSS falls back to internal behaviour. Windows
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.038|                           11 client ships the value present, so absence
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.039|                           means it was removed.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.040|
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.041|  Why these profiles : All five write 20 because 20 is the Windows value
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.042|                       and no use case argues for a different CPU reserve.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.043|                       Say no only if you run a low-latency audio rig on a
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.044|                       number you chose yourself and can justify.
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.045|
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.046|  Target          : reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.047|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile" /v
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.048|                    "SystemResponsiveness" /t REG_DWORD /d 20 /f (OPTY.bat
::X|EN|gr.systemresponsiveness.049|                    1621, in :gaming_restore)
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.001|  Ce que c est    : La part de CPU que MMCSS garde garantie au travail non
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.002|                    multimedia pendant qu une application media est
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.003|                    enregistree aupres de lui. C est la moitie « retour en
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.004|                    arriere » du profil jeu.
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.005|
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.006|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 20. Les guides mettent 0 ou 10, ce qui ne
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.007|                    modifie l ordonnancement que pour les threads
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.008|                    enregistres aupres de MMCSS - typiquement les threads
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.009|                    de rendu audio des jeux et des applications media,
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.010|                    puisque les profils de tache Games, Audio et Pro Audio
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.011|                    existent precisement pour cela, et non le thread de
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.012|                    rendu principal d un jeu.
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.013|
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.014|  Gain            : Aucun si vous etiez deja a 20. Sur une machine restee
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.015|                    a 0, cela supprime la condition qui affame les threads
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.016|                    audio en charge, symptome audible que les gens
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.017|                    rapportent.
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.018|
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.019|  Cout            : Rien. Simple remise a la valeur d usine.
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.020|
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.021|  Defaut Windows  : 20 (0x14).
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.022|
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.023|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.024|    20                   : La valeur d origine des editions client : 20
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.025|                           pour cent du temps CPU restent garantis aux
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.026|                           threads ordinaires, le reste est disponible
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.027|                           pour les threads enregistres aupres de MMCSS.
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.028|    10                   : Moitie moins de reserve pour les threads
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.029|                           ordinaires. La valeur laissee sur cette machine
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.030|                           meme par d anciennes versions d OPTY.
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.031|    0                    : Ne demande aucune reserve. Le chouchou des
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.032|                           guides de bidouille, et l etat dans lequel les
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.033|                           threads de rendu audio peuvent manquer de CPU
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.034|                           en forte charge.
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.035|    100                  : Tout reserve aux threads ordinaires, ce qui
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.036|                           neutralise le coup de pouce multimedia. La
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.037|                           valeur de Windows Server.
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.038|    absent               : MMCSS retombe sur son comportement interne.
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.039|                           Windows 11 client livre la valeur presente :
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.040|                           son absence signifie qu elle a ete supprimee.
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.041|
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.042|  Pourquoi ces profils : Les cinq ecrivent 20 parce que 20 est la valeur
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.043|                         Windows et qu aucun usage ne plaide pour une
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.044|                         reserve CPU differente. Refusez seulement si vous
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.045|                         avez un setup audio faible latence regle sur un
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.046|                         chiffre que vous savez justifier.
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.047|
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.048|  Cible           : reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.049|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile" /v
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.050|                    "SystemResponsiveness" /t REG_DWORD /d 20 /f (OPTY.bat
::X|FR|gr.systemresponsiveness.051|                    1621, in :gaming_restore)
