:: ---- rad.mmcss.scheduler (repair) -------------------------------
::P|rad.mmcss.scheduler|20/10/2|20/10/2|20/10/2|20/10/2|20/10/2|
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.001|RESTORE THE STOCK MULTIMEDIA SCHEDULER VALUES
::T|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.002|Puts SystemResponsiveness, NetworkThrottlingIndex and Win32PrioritySeparation back to their Windows values, undoing old tweaks that can cause audio glitches without ever raising your frame rate.
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.001|REMETTRE LES VALEURS D ORIGINE DU PLANIFICATEUR MULTIMEDIA
::T|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.002|Remet SystemResponsiveness, NetworkThrottlingIndex et Win32PrioritySeparation a leur valeur Windows, annulant de vieux reglages capables de provoquer des coupures audio sans jamais ameliorer les images par seconde.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.001|  What it is      : SystemResponsiveness, NetworkThrottlingIndex and
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.002|                    Win32PrioritySeparation, written together because
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.003|                    tweak guides always change them together.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.004|
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.005|  Actual effect   : Writes 20, 10 and 2 - the shipped numbers. This is not
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.006|                    theoretical: 10, 0xFFFFFFFF and 38 were measured on
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.007|                    this very machine, left by older builds of this tool,
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.008|                    and survived every earlier restore because
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.009|                    :reassert_defaults never touched them.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.010|
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.011|  Gain            : Removes tweaks that can cause audio glitching under
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.012|                    load, with no frame-rate gain to show for them. On a
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.013|                    machine that was never tweaked, no observable change -
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.014|                    the write exists so an unknown machine ends in a known
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.015|                    state.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.016|
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.017|  Cost            : Nothing. All three are being set back to what Windows
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.018|                    installs.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.019|
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.020|  Windows default : 20 / 10 / 2.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.021|
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.022|  Possible values:
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.023|    20/10/2              : The shipped trio. SystemResponsiveness=20 keeps
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.024|                           a fifth of CPU time guaranteed to ordinary
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.025|                           threads. NetworkThrottlingIndex=10 caps non-
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.026|                           multimedia network processing at roughly 10
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.027|                           packets per millisecond while a multimedia
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.028|                           stream is registered. Win32PrioritySeparation=2
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.029|                           is the short, variable, foreground-boosted
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.030|                           quantum a client Windows uses.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.031|    10/0xFFFFFFFF/38     : The exact leftovers measured live on this
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.032|                           machine, written by earlier OPTY builds and
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.033|                           never cleaned up by any previous restore.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.034|                           0xFFFFFFFF disables network throttling
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.035|                           entirely, and 38 (0x26) is what 'Adjust for
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.036|                           best performance of: Programs' writes - on a
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.037|                           client SKU it decodes to the same
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.038|                           short/variable/foreground profile as 2, so it
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.039|                           buys nothing.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.040|    0 for SystemResponsiveness :
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.041|        No CPU reserve for ordinary threads. Under heavy load this is the
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.042|        configuration in which audio render threads glitch.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.043|    0xFFFFFFFF for NetworkThrottlingIndex :
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.044|        Throttling off completely. It removes a cap that only applies
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.045|        while a multimedia stream is registered, and the usual claim of
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.046|        lower game latency has no measurement behind it - game traffic is
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.047|        UDP and is not what the cap governs.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.048|    absent               : All three ship present on Windows 11, so an
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.049|                           absent value means a tool removed it. The
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.050|                           scheduler then falls back to its built-in
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.051|                           client behaviour.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.052|
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.053|  Why these profiles : One literal for all five profiles, because all
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.054|                       three values are simply the client defaults and
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.055|                       none of them behaves differently on a gaming rig, a
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.056|                       server, an office desktop or a laptop. Say no only
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.057|                       if you set these deliberately and can explain each
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.058|                       one.
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.059|
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.060|  Target          : OPTY.bat 1815-1817, in :reassert_defaults - call
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.061|                    :regset on SystemResponsiveness=20 and
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.062|                    NetworkThrottlingIndex=10 under
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.063|                    HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.064|                    NT\CurrentVersion\Multimedia\SystemProfile, and
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.065|                    Win32PrioritySeparation=2 under
::X|EN|rad.mmcss.scheduler.066|                    HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\PriorityControl
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.001|  Ce que c est    : SystemResponsiveness, NetworkThrottlingIndex et
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.002|                    Win32PrioritySeparation, ecrites ensemble parce que
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.003|                    les guides de bidouille les modifient toujours
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.004|                    ensemble.
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.005|
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.006|  Effet reel      : Ecrit 20, 10 et 2, les valeurs d origine. Ce n est pas
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.007|                    theorique : 10, 0xFFFFFFFF et 38 ont ete mesurees sur
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.008|                    cette machine meme, laissees par d anciennes versions
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.009|                    de cet outil, et ont survecu a toutes les
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.010|                    restaurations precedentes parce que :reassert_defaults
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.011|                    n y touchait pas.
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.012|
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.013|  Gain            : Supprime des tweaks capables de provoquer des coupures
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.014|                    audio en charge, sans le moindre gain en images par
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.015|                    seconde en echange. Sur une machine jamais bidouillee,
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.016|                    aucun changement observable : l ecriture sert a
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.017|                    remettre une machine inconnue dans un etat connu.
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.018|
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.019|  Cout            : Rien. Les trois reviennent a ce que Windows installe.
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.020|
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.021|  Defaut Windows  : 20 / 10 / 2.
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.022|
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.023|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.024|    20/10/2              : Le trio d origine. SystemResponsiveness=20
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.025|                           garde un cinquieme du temps CPU garanti aux
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.026|                           threads ordinaires. NetworkThrottlingIndex=10
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.027|                           plafonne le traitement reseau non multimedia a
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.028|                           environ 10 paquets par milliseconde quand un
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.029|                           flux multimedia est enregistre.
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.030|                           Win32PrioritySeparation=2, c est le quantum
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.031|                           court, variable et favorable au premier plan
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.032|                           qu utilise un Windows client.
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.033|    10/0xFFFFFFFF/38     : Les reliquats exacts mesures en vrai sur cette
::X|FR|rad.mmcss.scheduler.034|                           machine, ecrits par d anciennes versions d OPTY
