:: ---- cl.dnscache.flush (cleanup) ---------------------------------
::P|cl.dnscache.flush|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|KEEP|
::T|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.001|FLUSH THE DNS RESOLVER CACHE
::T|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.002|Empties the in-memory DNS cache with ipconfig /flushdns - no performance gain at all, but it is the standard fix when a name still resolves to a stale address after a DNS change, a VPN toggle or a hosts-file edit.
::T|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.001|VIDAGE DU CACHE DNS
::T|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.002|Vide le cache DNS en memoire avec ipconfig /flushdns - aucun gain de performance, mais c est le remede standard quand un nom pointe encore sur une adresse perimee apres un changement de DNS, une bascule VPN ou une modification du fichier hosts.
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.001|  What it is      : Windows keeps recent DNS answers in memory so repeated
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.002|                    lookups of the same name do not go back out to the
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.003|                    network. This empties that cache.
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.004|
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.005|  Actual effect   : One command, ipconfig /flushdns, which discards every
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.006|                    cached entry held by the Dnscache service. The next
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.007|                    lookup for each name goes back to your DNS server. It
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.008|                    writes nothing: your DNS servers, your hosts file,
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.009|                    your network profile, your adapter settings are all
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.010|                    untouched. It always succeeds - nothing can lock a
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.011|                    cache that lives in a service's memory. Note that the
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.012|                    same block then re-asserts three TCP settings on the
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.013|                    following lines, which is a separate step with a
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.014|                    separate answer.
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.015|
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.016|  Gain            : No performance gain. None. If anything the next few
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.017|                    lookups are marginally slower because they have to go
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.018|                    out to the network again. What it does fix, and the
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.019|                    only thing it fixes, is a name that still resolves to
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.020|                    an address it should no longer resolve to - after a
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.021|                    DNS change, after connecting or disconnecting a VPN,
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.022|                    or after editing the hosts file.
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.023|
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.024|  Cost            : Nothing. The cache refills by itself within seconds of
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.025|                    normal use. No user data, no configuration and no
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.026|                    history is involved.
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.027|
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.028|  Windows default : Not applicable in the usual sense. Windows never
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.029|                    flushes on demand; entries simply expire when their
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.030|                    TTL runs out, and the whole cache is empty again after
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.031|                    every reboot.
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.032|
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.033|  Possible values:
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.034|    DELETE               : Empty the in-memory DNS resolver cache now.
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.035|                           Free, instant, and the standard fix when a name
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.036|                           resolves to an address it should no longer
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.037|                           resolve to.
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.038|    KEEP                 : Leave the cache alone. Each entry expires on
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.039|                           its own when its TTL runs out, which for most
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.040|                           names is minutes to a day. Nothing accumulates:
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.041|                           the cache is in RAM and is empty again after a
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.042|                           reboot.
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.043|    ASK                  : No profile uses it and none should. There is
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.044|                           nothing here to weigh: no data is lost, no
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.045|                           setting is changed and the worst case is a
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.046|                           handful of lookups that take a few extra
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.047|                           milliseconds.
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.048|
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.049|  Why these profiles : Four DELETE and one KEEP. The four are identical on
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.050|                       purpose - there is no gaming, server, office or
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.051|                       battery argument in either direction, the step is
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.052|                       free and occasionally useful, so they all take it.
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.053|                       WINDOWS is KEEP because the shipped behaviour
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.054|                       really is different and not just a placeholder:
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.055|                       Windows lets each entry age out by TTL rather than
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.056|                       dropping the lot.
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.057|
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.058|  Target          : No file path. ipconfig /flushdns, OPTY.bat line 579,
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.059|                    label :netdns - the first line of that block, before
::X|EN|cl.dnscache.flush.060|                    the TCP defaults are re-asserted at lines 587 to 589.
::X|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.001|  Ce que c est    : Windows garde en mémoire les réponses DNS récentes
::X|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.002|                    pour éviter de refaire la même résolution sur le
::X|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.003|                    réseau. Cette étape vide ce cache.
::X|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.004|
::X|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.005|  Effet reel      : Une seule commande, ipconfig /flushdns, qui jette
::X|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.006|                    toutes les entrées mémorisées par le service Dnscache.
::X|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.007|                    La prochaine résolution de chaque nom repart vers
::X|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.008|                    votre serveur DNS. Rien n est écrit : vos serveurs
::X|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.009|                    DNS, votre fichier hosts, votre profil réseau et les
::X|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.010|                    réglages de vos cartes restent intacts. Ça réussit
::X|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.011|                    toujours : rien ne peut verrouiller un cache qui vit
::X|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.012|                    dans la mémoire d un service. À noter, le même bloc
::X|FR|cl.dnscache.flush.013|                    réaffirme ensuite trois réglages TCP sur les lignes
