:: ---- cl.wer.dumps (cleanup) --------------------------------------
::P|cl.wer.dumps|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|KEEP|
::T|EN|cl.wer.dumps.001|ERROR REPORTS AND APPLICATION CRASH DUMPS
::T|EN|cl.wer.dumps.002|Deletes queued crash reports and per-application crash dump files, usually a modest amount of space unless an app has been crashing repeatedly, but you permanently lose the evidence of what crashed and cannot get it back.
::T|FR|cl.wer.dumps.001|RAPPORTS D ERREUR ET DUMPS DE PLANTAGE DES APPLICATIONS
::T|FR|cl.wer.dumps.002|Supprime les rapports d erreur en file d attente et les dumps de plantage par application, généralement peu de place sauf si une application plante en boucle, mais vous perdez définitivement la preuve de ce qui a planté, sans retour possible.
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.001|  What it is      : WER is the machinery behind "This app has stopped
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.002|                    working". Reports queue under
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.003|                    %ProgramData%\Microsoft\Windows\WER and under your
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.004|                    profile, and per-application crash dumps land in
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.005|                    %LOCALAPPDATA%\CrashDumps. If reports are never sent,
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.006|                    nothing prunes them by size.
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.007|
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.008|  Actual effect   : Deletes the files under the two WER trees and under
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.009|                    CrashDumps. Because del /S removes files and not
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.010|                    directories, the folder skeleton stays behind - you
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.011|                    can be left with hundreds of empty ReportQueue and
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.012|                    ReportArchive folders taking essentially no space. A
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.013|                    report being written at that exact moment by
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.014|                    WerFault.exe is locked and skipped, silently. The two
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.015|                    user-level lines only cover the hardcoded
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.016|                    C:\Users\compt profile, not every account. WER itself
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.017|                    keeps running exactly as before.
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.018|
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.019|  Gain            : The ProgramData queue is usually 5 to 100 MB.
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.020|                    CrashDumps is the one that can be large: each dump is
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.021|                    a snapshot of the crashed process, so one badly
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.022|                    behaved 64-bit application can leave 200 MB to over 1
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.023|                    GB per crash, and several GB if it crashes in a loop.
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.024|                    On a machine that does not crash, both are near empty
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.025|                    and the honest answer is that you will reclaim almost
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.026|                    nothing.
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.027|
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.028|  Cost            : You lose the local record of what has been crashing,
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.029|                    permanently - nothing regenerates a dump of a crash
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.030|                    that already happened. If a vendor asked you for a
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.031|                    .dmp, send it before running this. Not touched: your
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.032|                    files, settings, event logs, and the kernel minidumps,
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.033|                    which are handled by a different step. There is
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.034|                    nothing to re-download and no rebuild time.
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.035|
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.036|  Windows default : Windows caps the WER queue by number of entries, never
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.037|                    by size, and %LOCALAPPDATA%\CrashDumps is not pruned
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.038|                    at all. Disk Cleanup offers the same thing under
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.039|                    "Windows Error Reporting Files".
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.040|
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.041|  Possible values:
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.042|    DELETE               : Empty the WER report queues and the per-
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.043|                           application crash dumps. WER keeps working; you
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.044|                           simply lose the backlog.
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.045|    KEEP                 : Right when an application is crashing and a
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.046|                           vendor has asked you for the .dmp files, or
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.047|                           when you want to see for yourself what has been
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.048|                           crashing.
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.049|    ASK                  : Worth asking on a machine you are actively
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.050|                           troubleshooting, since the dumps are the
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.051|                           evidence and nothing regenerates them.
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.052|
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.053|  Why these profiles : Four identical columns because the file is either
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.054|                       useful evidence or dead weight, and that depends on
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.055|                       your situation, not on the machine's role. SERVER
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.056|                       is the one place to think twice: an unattended box
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.057|                       that crashes at 4 in the morning leaves its only
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.058|                       trace here. On GAMING, OFFICE and LAPTOP these are
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.059|                       almost always stale reports nobody will ever open.
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.060|                       WINDOWS means leave it alone and let the queue sit
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.061|                       there, which costs you nothing but disk.
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.062|
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.063|  Known problems  : The two user-level paths use the hardcoded %USERHOME%
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.064|                    (C:\Users\compt) instead of the loop over C:\Users\*
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.065|                    that the Temp step uses. On any other account, or any
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.066|                    other machine, those two lines silently delete nothing
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.067|                    while the run still reports a clean.
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.068|
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.069|  Unverified      : WER has per-queue entry-count limits in its own
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.070|                    configuration, so the queue is not literally unlimited
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.071|                    on every machine; what it has no notion of is a size
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.072|                    budget, and CrashDumps has neither. The sizes below
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.073|                    are ranges from ordinary installs, not a measurement
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.074|                    of this machine.
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.075|
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.076|  Target          : OPTY.bat lines 1003-1007. del /F /S /Q on
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.077|                    %ProgramData%\Microsoft\Windows\WER\*,
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.078|                    %USERHOME%\AppData\Local\Microsoft\Windows\WER\* and
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.079|                    %USERHOME%\AppData\Local\CrashDumps\*. USERHOME is
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.080|                    hardcoded at line 81 to C:\Users\compt. Kernel dumps
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.081|                    are a different step: %SystemRoot%\Minidump and
::X|EN|cl.wer.dumps.082|                    MEMORY.DMP are deleted at lines 974-976.
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.001|  Ce que c est    : WER est la mécanique derrière « Cette application a
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.002| cessé de fonctionner ». Les rapports s empilent dans
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.003|                    %ProgramData%\Microsoft\Windows\WER et dans votre
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.004|                    profil, et les dumps par application atterrissent dans
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.005|                    %LOCALAPPDATA%\CrashDumps. Si les rapports ne sont
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.006|                    jamais envoyés, rien ne les purge en fonction de leur
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.007|                    taille.
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.008|
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.009|  Effet reel      : Supprime les fichiers des deux arborescences WER et de
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.010|                    CrashDumps. Comme del /S ne supprime que des fichiers,
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.011|                    la charpente de dossiers reste : vous pouvez vous
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.012|                    retrouver avec des centaines de dossiers ReportQueue
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.013|                    et ReportArchive vides, qui ne pèsent pratiquement
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.014| rien. Un rapport en cours d écriture par WerFault.exe
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.015|                    à cet instant précis est verrouillé et ignoré, en
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.016|                    silence. Les deux lignes de niveau utilisateur ne
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.017|                    couvrent que le profil C:\Users\compt codé en dur, pas
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.018|                    tous les comptes. WER lui-même continue exactement
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.019|                    comme avant.
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.020|
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.021|  Gain            : La file de ProgramData fait en général 5 à 100 Mo.
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.022| C est CrashDumps qui peut être gros : chaque dump est
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.023|                    une photographie du processus planté, donc une
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.024|                    application 64 bits capricieuse laisse 200 Mo à plus
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.025|                    de 1 Go par plantage, et plusieurs Go si elle plante
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.026|                    en boucle. Sur une machine qui ne plante pas, les deux
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.027|                    sont quasi vides et la réponse honnête est que vous ne
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.028|                    récupérerez presque rien.
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.029|
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.030|  Cout            : Vous perdez définitivement la trace locale de ce qui
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.031| plante : rien ne régénère le dump d un plantage déjà
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.032|                    passé. Si un éditeur vous a réclamé un .dmp, envoyez-
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.033|                    le avant. Non touchés : vos fichiers, vos réglages,
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.034| les journaux d événements, et les minidumps noyau, qui
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.035| relèvent d une autre étape. Rien à retélécharger,
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.036|                    aucun temps de reconstruction.
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.037|
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.038| Defaut Windows : Windows limite la file WER au nombre d entrées, jamais
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.039| à la taille, et %LOCALAPPDATA%\CrashDumps n est pas
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.040|                    purgé du tout. Le Nettoyage de disque propose la même
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.041| chose sous « Fichiers d archive des rapports d erreurs
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.042|                    Windows ».
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.043|
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.044|  Valeurs possibles :
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.045|    DELETE               : Vider les files de rapports WER et les dumps
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.046| d applications. WER continue de fonctionner,
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.047| vous perdez seulement l arriéré.
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.048|    KEEP                 : Le bon choix quand une application plante et
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.049| qu un éditeur vous a demandé les fichiers .dmp,
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.050|                           ou quand vous voulez constater vous-même ce qui
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.051|                           plante.
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.052|    ASK                  : Une question utile sur une machine en cours de
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.053|                           diagnostic : les dumps sont la preuve, et rien
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.054|                           ne les régénère.
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.055|
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.056| Pourquoi ces profils : Quatre colonnes identiques, parce qu un dump est
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.057|                         soit une preuve utile, soit du poids mort, et
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.058|                         cela dépend de votre situation, pas du rôle de la
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.059|                         machine. SERVEUR est le seul cas où réfléchir :
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.060|                         une machine sans surveillance qui plante à 4 h du
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.061| matin ne laisse de trace qu ici. Sur GAMING,
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.062|                         BUREAU et PORTABLE, ce sont presque toujours de
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.063| vieux rapports que personne n ouvrira jamais.
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.064|                         WINDOWS veut dire ne touchez à rien et laissez la
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.065|                         file où elle est : cela ne vous coûte que du
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.066|                         disque.
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.067|
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.068|  Problemes connus : Les deux chemins de niveau utilisateur utilisent le
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.069|                     %USERHOME% codé en dur (C:\Users\compt) au lieu de la
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.070| boucle sur C:\Users\* qu emploie l étape Temp. Sur
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.071|                     tout autre compte, ou toute autre machine, ces deux
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.072|                     lignes ne suppriment rien en silence alors que
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.073| l exécution annonce un nettoyage.
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.074|
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.075|  Non verifie (en)  : WER has per-queue entry-count limits in its own
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.076|                      configuration, so the queue is not literally
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.077|                      unlimited on every machine; what it has no notion of
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.078|                      is a size budget, and CrashDumps has neither. The
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.079|                      sizes below are ranges from ordinary installs, not a
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.080|                      measurement of this machine.
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.081|
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.082|  Cible           : OPTY.bat lines 1003-1007. del /F /S /Q on
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.083|                    %ProgramData%\Microsoft\Windows\WER\*,
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.084|                    %USERHOME%\AppData\Local\Microsoft\Windows\WER\* and
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.085|                    %USERHOME%\AppData\Local\CrashDumps\*. USERHOME is
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.086|                    hardcoded at line 81 to C:\Users\compt. Kernel dumps
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.087|                    are a different step: %SystemRoot%\Minidump and
::X|FR|cl.wer.dumps.088|                    MEMORY.DMP are deleted at lines 974-976.
::
:: ---- cl.logs.unbounded (cleanup) ---------------------------------
::P|cl.logs.unbounded|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|KEEP|
::T|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.001|LOG AND TRACE FILES THAT NEVER GET TRIMMED
::T|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.002|Deletes a specific list of logs and traces that grow forever because nothing else ever prunes them, freeing real space (often several hundred MB), at the cost of losing driver-install and update history that helps diagnose a problem device.
::T|FR|cl.logs.unbounded.001|JOURNAUX ET TRACES QUE RIEN NE PURGE JAMAIS
::T|FR|cl.logs.unbounded.002|Supprime une liste précise de journaux et de traces qui grossissent sans fin parce que rien d autre ne les purge, libérant un espace réel (souvent plusieurs centaines de Mo), au prix de l historique d installation des pilotes et de mise à jour utile pour diagnostiquer un périphérique en panne.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.001|  What it is      : A short list of files that grow forever because
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.002|                    neither Windows nor the AMD driver ever trims them:
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.003|                    AMD Adrenalin usage telemetry CSVs, rotated WMI trace
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.004|                    files, the AutoLogger diagnostics traces, update-
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.005|                    orchestrator traces, DISM and servicing logs, and the
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.006|                    two setupapi device-install logs. Only named files and
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.007|                    narrow globs are targeted - the WMI and RtBackup
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.008|                    folders themselves are never removed, and %WINDIR%\inf
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.009|                    keeps every real driver INF.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.010|
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.011|  Actual effect   : Deletes exactly those files. Most of them delete
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.012|                    cleanly on an idle machine. Two do not always: the WMI
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.013|                    glob is *.etl.* deliberately, so it takes the rotated
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.014|                    files and leaves the live trace, which the ETW session
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.015|                    holds open anyway; and in %WINDIR%\Logs\WindowsUpdate
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.016|                    an active trace session keeps its newest .etl locked,
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.017|                    so that one is skipped without a message. The step
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.018|                    needs administrator rights - without them the %WINDIR%
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.019|                    lines all fail silently. Folders always survive, and
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.020|                    each subsystem simply starts a new file.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.021|
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.022|  Gain            : Real, measurable, and no standard cleaner touches it.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.023|                    On the machine the rule was written for: 317 MB of AMD
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.024|                    telemetry CSVs, 228 MB of rotated WMI traces, 86 MB of
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.025|                    update-orchestrator logs - roughly 630 MB in one pass.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.026|                    On a recently installed machine, or one without
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.027|                    Adrenalin, expect tens of MB instead. No performance
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.028|                    effect at all: these files are written and never read
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.029|                    back by anything you run.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.030|
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.031|  Cost            : You lose driver-install history, which matters more
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.032|                    than it sounds - setupapi.dev.log is the file that
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.033|                    explains why a device stopped working after an update
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.034|                    - and you lose servicing history. None of it affects
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.035|                    how the machine runs, and nothing has to be
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.036|                    downloaded. What comes back does so within minutes of
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.037|                    the next update or device change, so this passes the
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.038|                    regeneration test easily. Not touched:
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.039|                    USOPrivate\UpdateStore, the RtBackup folder, the real
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.040|                    INF store, and the live CBS log.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.041|
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.042|  Windows default : Not applicable. These grow without bound by design,
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.043|                    and no Windows component trims them.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.044|
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.045|  Possible values:
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.046|    DELETE               : Remove those named log and trace files. The
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.047|                           subsystems that write them carry on and open
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.048|                           fresh files.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.049|    KEEP                 : The right answer while you are chasing a device
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.050|                           that will not install: setupapi.dev.log is
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.051|                           precisely the file that tells you why.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.052|    ASK                  : Only worth asking on a machine under active
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.053|                           diagnosis, since the driver-install and
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.054|                           servicing history goes for good.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.055|
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.056|  Why these profiles : Four identical columns because the mechanism is
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.057|                       identical everywhere: a log file nobody reads.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.058|                       SERVER benefits most in absolute terms, since an
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.059|                       always-on machine accumulates traces fastest.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.060|                       GAMING on an AMD card is where the 317 MB of
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.061|                       Adrenalin CSVs come from. OFFICE and LAPTOP simply
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.062|                       gain the space. WINDOWS means leave it alone:
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.063|                       nothing breaks, the files just keep growing.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.064|
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.065|  Unverified      : The sizes quoted - AMD PPC 317 MB, WMI ETL 228 MB,
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.066|                    USOShared 86 MB - were measured once, on the
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.067|                    maintainer's machine. Yours will differ, possibly by
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.068|                    an order of magnitude in either direction. The AMD PPC
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.069|                    files only exist if Adrenalin is installed. Reports of
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.070|                    those CSVs reaching tens of GB come from the field,
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.071|                    not from a measurement here.
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.072|
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.073|  Target          : OPTY.bat lines 1009-1036. Named files and globs only:
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.074|                    %LOCALAPPDATA%\AMD\PPC\sdkusage.csv, apprecord.csv,
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.075|                    driverworkloadstats.csv;
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.076|                    %LOCALAPPDATA%\AMD\CN\RSX_*.log*;
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.077|                    %WINDIR%\System32\LogFiles\WMI\*.etl.* (no /S, so
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.078|                    RtBackup is never entered); %ProgramData%\Microsoft\Di
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.079|                    agnosis\ETLLogs\AutoLogger\*.etl;
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.080|                    %ProgramData%\USOShared\Logs\*.etl;
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.081|                    %WINDIR%\Logs\DISM\dism.log; %WINDIR%\Logs\waasmedic,
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.082|                    SIH and NetSetup; %WINDIR%\Logs\WindowsUpdate\*.etl;
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.083|                    %WINDIR%\inf\setupapi.dev.log and setupapi.app.log;
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.084|                    %WINDIR%\debug\wiatrace.log. No folder is ever
::X|EN|cl.logs.unbounded.085|                    removed.
::X|FR|cl.logs.unbounded.001|  Ce que c est    : Une liste courte de fichiers qui grossissent sans fin
::X|FR|cl.logs.unbounded.002|                    parce que ni Windows ni le pilote AMD ne les rognent
::X|FR|cl.logs.unbounded.003| jamais : les CSV de télémétrie d usage d AMD
::X|FR|cl.logs.unbounded.004|                    Adrenalin, les fichiers de trace WMI en rotation, les
::X|FR|cl.logs.unbounded.005|                    traces de diagnostic AutoLogger, les traces de
::X|FR|cl.logs.unbounded.006| l orchestrateur de mises à jour, les journaux DISM et
::X|FR|cl.logs.unbounded.007| de maintenance, et les deux journaux d installation de
::X|FR|cl.logs.unbounded.008|                    périphériques setupapi. Seuls des fichiers nommés et
::X|FR|cl.logs.unbounded.009|                    des motifs étroits sont visés : les dossiers WMI et
