:: ---- cl.startupdelay.zero (preference) ------------------------------
::P|cl.startupdelay.zero|0|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.001|STARTUP APP DELAY AT LOGON
::T|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.002|Removes the short wait before your startup apps launch, so tray apps like Discord and Steam appear sooner, at the cost of a busier and possibly less responsive first minute after login.
::T|FR|cl.startupdelay.zero.001|DÉLAI DE LANCEMENT DES APPLICATIONS AU DÉMARRAGE
::T|FR|cl.startupdelay.zero.002|Supprime la courte attente avant le lancement de vos applications de démarrage, pour que Discord, Steam et les autres apparaissent plus tôt, au prix d une première minute plus chargée et parfois moins réactive.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.001|  What it is      : After you sign in, Explorer deliberately waits before
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.002|                    launching your startup applications, so the desktop,
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.003|                    taskbar and shell finish loading first. This step
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.004|                    writes StartupDelayInMSec=0 to remove that wait.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.005|
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.006|  Actual effect   : Creates the Serialize key if it is missing and sets
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.007|                    the value to 0 - it currently reads 0x0 on this
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.008|                    machine, so OPTY has already applied it. It changes
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.009|                    only what Explorer launches: the HKCU and HKLM Run
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.010|                    keys and the Startup folder. Services, scheduled tasks
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.011|                    and packaged-app startup tasks are unaffected. The
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.012|                    value is per-user, so it applies to the account that
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.013|                    ran OPTY and to no other, and it takes effect at the
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.014|                    next logon.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.015|
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.016|  Gain            : Your startup applications appear sooner. That is the
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.017|                    entire benefit, and it is a preference, not a
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.018|                    performance win.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.019|
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.020|  Cost            : The first minute after logon is busier: the shell and
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.021|                    every startup app compete for the same disk and CPU at
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.022|                    once. On a mechanical disk or with a long startup list
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.023|                    the desktop itself can feel less responsive than
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.024|                    before, which is exactly what the delay exists to
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.025|                    prevent. Total time until everything is loaded does
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.026|                    not improve.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.027|
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.028|  Windows default : The value, and usually the Serialize key itself, is
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.029|                    absent - Explorer then applies its built-in delay.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.030|
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.031|  Possible values:
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.032|    0                    : No wait: Explorer launches your Run-key and
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.033|                           Startup-folder apps at the same time as the
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.034|                           shell. Discord, Steam and a password manager
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.035|                           are ready sooner, at the price of a busier
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.036|                           first minute.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.037|    DELETE               : Remove the value and Explorer applies its
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.038|                           built-in stagger again. This is how Windows
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.039|                           ships, and it is what keeps the desktop
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.040|                           responsive while a long startup list loads.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.041|    ASK                  : Reasonable on a machine you do not know: the
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.042|                           right answer depends on how many startup items
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.043|                           there are and what the system disk is, neither
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.zero.044|                           of which this step reads.
