::P|cl.startupdelay.restore|0|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|DELETE|
::T|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.001|RESTORE THE LOGON STARTUP DELAY
::T|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.002|Undoes the zero-delay tweak so Windows staggers your startup apps again - a real improvement on a slow disk with many startup apps, unmeasurable on a fast NVMe with few.
::T|FR|cl.startupdelay.restore.001|RÉTABLIR LE DÉLAI DE DÉMARRAGE À L OUVERTURE DE SESSION
::T|FR|cl.startupdelay.restore.002|Annule le réglage à délai zéro pour que Windows étale de nouveau vos applications de démarrage - une vraie amélioration sur un disque lent avec beaucoup d applications, invisible sur un NVMe rapide avec peu d applications.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.001|  What it is      : Explorer normally waits before launching your startup
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.002|                    apps so the shell settles first. This step deletes the
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.003|                    StartupDelayInMSec override, putting the built-in wait
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.004|                    back.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.005|
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.006|  Actual effect   : Deletes the value only. The Serialize key it lived in
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.007|                    is left behind, empty - inert, but the account is not
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.008|                    byte-identical to a machine that was never tweaked.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.009|                    Takes effect at the next logon, and only for the
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.010|                    account that runs it.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.011|
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.012|  Gain            : Startup programs no longer all start at once while the
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.013|                    shell is still coming up. On a mechanical disk with a
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.014|                    long startup list that is a real difference you can
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.015|                    feel; on NVMe with three startup apps it is a
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.016|                    difference nobody in this project has been able to
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.017|                    measure.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.018|
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.019|  Cost            : Your tray applications appear roughly ten seconds
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.020|                    later than with the 0 override. Nothing else changes,
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.021|                    nothing is disabled and nothing is removed from your
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.022|                    startup list.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.023|
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.024|  Windows default : Value absent - which is what this step produces, apart
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.025|                    from the leftover empty key.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.026|
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.027|  Possible values:
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.028|    DELETE               : Remove the override and let Explorer stagger
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.029|                           startup apps again, as Windows ships it. This
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.030|                           is the undo for the step that wrote 0.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.031|    0                    : Keep the override. Only worth it if you have
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.032|                           very few startup apps on fast storage and you
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.033|                           want them the instant the desktop appears.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.034|    ASK                  : Sensible if you do not remember whether the 0
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.035|                           was deliberate: this step deletes it without
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.036|                           reading it back, so an intentional choice
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.037|                           disappears in silence.
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.038|
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.039|  Why these profiles : Same split as the step that writes 0, read from the
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.040|                       other direction: only the gaming desktop has a
::X|EN|cl.startupdelay.restore.041|                       reason to keep the override, and everything else is
